<section id="S1" class="ltx_section">
<h2 class="ltx_title ltx_title_section"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_section">1 </span>Introduction</h2>

<div id="S1.p1" class="ltx_para ltx_noindent">
<p id="S1.p1.1" class="ltx_p">Multimodal large language models (MLLMs) are increasingly used as reasoning engines over images, documents, webpages, and videos. However, many real-world visual tasks require information that the model cannot reliably infer from the input alone. Answering a question may require localizing numerous tiny objects in dense scenes, retrieving external knowledge about a long-tail entity, or computing quantities from intermediate measurements. Such tasks require additional multimodal evidence acquired through perception, search, and computation. Our inference-time scaling study (Fig. <a href="#S0.F1" title="Figure 1 ‣ OpenVLHarness: Real World Visual Agentic Tool-Use with Multimodal Memory" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">1</span></a>c) reveals diminishing returns at higher reasoning budgets: moving the base model from xhigh to max effort multiplies its model-token spending by 2.7 but improves its average score by only 0.7 points. This raises the question of whether further progress requires better access to task-relevant multimodal evidence, beyond additional reasoning over the initial input.</p>
</div>
<div id="S1.p2" class="ltx_para ltx_noindent">
<p id="S1.p2.1" class="ltx_p">Visual tool-use agents address this need by connecting MLLMs to perception models, search engines, and code execution <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_citep">(<a href="#bib.bib4" title="" class="ltx_ref">Hong et al., 2026a</a>; <a href="#bib.bib53" title="" class="ltx_ref">Wu et al., 2025a</a>; <a href="#bib.bib54" title="" class="ltx_ref">Narayan et al., 2025</a>; <a href="#bib.bib47" title="" class="ltx_ref">Geng et al., 2026</a>; <a href="#bib.bib7" title="" class="ltx_ref">Su et al., 2025</a>; <a href="#bib.bib8" title="" class="ltx_ref">Wu et al., 2025b</a>; <a href="#bib.bib12" title="" class="ltx_ref">Surís et al., 2023</a>; <a href="#bib.bib13" title="" class="ltx_ref">Gupta and Kembhavi, 2023</a>)</cite>. Building a general harness around these tools presents two connected challenges. First, heterogeneous backends expose different calling conventions and return outputs such as segmentation masks, depth maps, and retrieved webpages that need to be presented in a form the model can interpret. Second, subsequent reasoning steps may still need access to the underlying artifacts: a rendered depth map supports visual inspection, but a precise measurement requires its numerical values. Effective multimodal tool use therefore requires both consistent tool interfaces with evidence rendering and a mechanism for retaining and reusing the resulting artifacts.</p>
</div>
<div id="S1.p3" class="ltx_para ltx_noindent">
<p id="S1.p3.1" class="ltx_p">We introduce OpenVLHarness, a multimodal reasoning harness organized around a <em id="S1.p3.1.1" class="ltx_emph ltx_font_italic">capability layer</em> and <em id="S1.p3.1.2" class="ltx_emph ltx_font_italic">persistent multimodal memory</em>. The capability layer combines <em id="S1.p3.1.3" class="ltx_emph ltx_font_italic">task-level tool interfaces</em> with <em id="S1.p3.1.4" class="ltx_emph ltx_font_italic">model-facing evidence rendering</em>. Task-level interfaces define operations independently of a particular backend, allowing tools to be replaced without changing the orchestrator’s task vocabulary. Evidence rendering converts heterogeneous outputs into observations that combine visualizations, concise descriptions, and compact structured information. For example, visual grounding accepts an image and a textual query, then presents the detected regions through an annotated image and coordinates. Together, these mechanisms let the orchestrator invoke tools consistently and inspect their results in a form suited to multimodal reasoning.</p>
</div>
<div id="S1.p4" class="ltx_para ltx_noindent">
<p id="S1.p4.1" class="ltx_p">Persistent multimodal memory makes the corresponding artifacts available across turns within a query. Images, structured data, and web references are registered under reusable handles, allowing later calls to operate on earlier evidence. In the grounding example, the orchestrator can inspect the overlay, then reuse the retained masks and a subsequently acquired depth array to measure depth within each region. A Python Coding Agent supports such compositions: the orchestrator specifies a computational objective in natural language, and a code-generation session backed by the same backbone model writes a program that executes over the stored data. The capability layer makes tool results interpretable; memory makes their underlying artifacts addressable and reusable. Together, they support an iterative process of acquiring, inspecting, and computing over multimodal evidence.</p>
</div>
<div id="S1.p5" class="ltx_para ltx_noindent">
<p id="S1.p5.1" class="ltx_p">We evaluate OpenVLHarness on 22 benchmarks spanning grounded perception, visual search and deep research, general VQA and hallucination, and spatial reasoning, using eight open and proprietary MLLM backbones of different scales (Fig. <a href="#S0.F1" title="Figure 1 ‣ OpenVLHarness: Real World Visual Agentic Tool-Use with Multimodal Memory" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">1</span></a>a). Across these settings, OpenVLHarness consistently improves average performance over the base MLLMs by 9.7–12.6 percentage points and outperforms existing visual tool-use baselines (Fig. <a href="#S0.F1" title="Figure 1 ‣ OpenVLHarness: Real World Visual Agentic Tool-Use with Multimodal Memory" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">1</span></a>c). Scaling experiments on 22 benchmarks further show that OpenVLHarness also benefits from greater reasoning effort (Fig. <a href="#S0.F1" title="Figure 1 ‣ OpenVLHarness: Real World Visual Agentic Tool-Use with Multimodal Memory" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">1</span></a>c), suggesting that evidence acquisition and inference-time scaling are complementary.
We conduct ablation studies to examine the incremental effects of the capability layer and memory, showing the value of both usable tool outputs and retained multimodal artifacts. Lastly, for users who would like to adapt our harness to a custom domain, we further showcase two coding-agent-driven workflows for specialization: harness search edits prompts, tool descriptions, and output templates, while tool generation composes existing capabilities into new tools. Both operate without updating the underlying MLLM weights.</p>
</div>
<div id="S1.p6" class="ltx_para ltx_noindent">
<p id="S1.p6.1" class="ltx_p">Our contributions are:
<span id="S1.p6.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">(1)</span> a capability layer that combines task-level tool interfaces with model-facing evidence rendering for heterogeneous perception, search, and computation tools;
<span id="S1.p6.1.2" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">(2)</span> persistent multimodal memory that retains addressable artifacts across turns and enables their reuse in subsequent tool calls and memory-grounded computation;
and <span id="S1.p6.1.3" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">(3)</span> an evaluation across 22 benchmarks and eight backbones, with component ablations and domain-specialization studies, accompanied by an open-source release of the harness, tools, evaluation protocols, and two optimization workflows.</p>
</div>
</section>
<section id="S2" class="ltx_section">
<h2 class="ltx_title ltx_title_section"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_section">2 </span>Harness Design</h2>

<figure id="S2.F2" class="ltx_figure"><img src="2610.26860v1/architecture.png" id="S2.F2.g1" class="ltx_graphics ltx_centering ltx_img_landscape" style="aspect-ratio:548/266;" width="548" height="266" alt="Refer to caption">
<figcaption class="ltx_caption ltx_centering"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_figure">Figure 2: </span><span id="S2.F2.3" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">Overview of OpenVLHarness.</span> The MLLM orchestrator invokes capabilities through typed interfaces. The capability layer connects these interfaces to underlying models and services and renders their outputs into model-facing evidence: here, a labeled mask overlay accompanied by a textual description and normalized bounding boxes. Persistent multimodal memory retains image artifacts, structured data, and web references under reusable handles. An Environment Update advertises the available artifacts to the orchestrator, allowing subsequent calls to reuse earlier evidence, including through memory-grounded computation.</figcaption>
</figure>
<div id="S2.p1" class="ltx_para ltx_noindent">
<p id="S2.p1.1" class="ltx_p">OpenVLHarness augments an MLLM orchestrator with two complementary mechanisms (Figure <a href="#S2.F2" title="Figure 2 ‣ 2 Harness Design ‣ OpenVLHarness: Real World Visual Agentic Tool-Use with Multimodal Memory" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">2</span></a>). The <em id="S2.p1.1.1" class="ltx_emph ltx_font_italic">capability layer</em> combines task-level tool interfaces with model-facing evidence rendering, providing consistent access to tools and observations the orchestrator can inspect (Sec. <a href="#S2.SS1" title="2.1 Capability Layer ‣ 2 Harness Design ‣ OpenVLHarness: Real World Visual Agentic Tool-Use with Multimodal Memory" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">2.1</span></a>). <em id="S2.p1.1.2" class="ltx_emph ltx_font_italic">Persistent multimodal memory</em> retains the corresponding artifacts and exposes them by reference for reuse across turns within a query (Sec. <a href="#S2.SS2" title="2.2 Persistent Multimodal Memory ‣ 2 Harness Design ‣ OpenVLHarness: Real World Visual Agentic Tool-Use with Multimodal Memory" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">2.2</span></a>). At each step, the orchestrator uses the observations and available memory references to select a tool call or produce an answer. A Python Coding Agent supports computation over stored artifacts, connecting outputs from different capabilities into multi-step procedures. The modular interfaces and renderers also support harness specialization through text edits and tool composition (Sec. <a href="#S3.SS6" title="3.6 Harness Optimization ‣ 3 Experiments ‣ OpenVLHarness: Real World Visual Agentic Tool-Use with Multimodal Memory" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">3.6</span></a>).</p>
</div>
<section id="S2.SS1" class="ltx_subsection">
<h3 class="ltx_title ltx_title_subsection"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_subsection">2.1 </span>Capability Layer</h3>

<div id="S2.SS1.p1" class="ltx_para ltx_noindent">
<p id="S2.SS1.p1.1" class="ltx_p">We design the capability layer with two goals: defining how the orchestrator invokes a capability and rendering the resulting evidence for inspection. The first accommodates different backends behind a consistent interface; the second addresses the heterogeneous formats of their outputs.</p>
</div>
<section id="S2.SS1.SSS0.Px1" class="ltx_paragraph">
<h4 class="ltx_title ltx_title_paragraph">Task-level tool interfaces.</h4>

<div id="S2.SS1.SSS0.Px1.p1" class="ltx_para ltx_noindent">
<p id="S2.SS1.SSS0.Px1.p1.1" class="ltx_p">We define a capability by the operation it provides, its typed inputs, and the evidence and reusable data it exposes. For example, visual grounding accepts an image reference and a textual query and exposes object locations, associated metadata, and a visualization; a supporting backend may additionally provide segmentation masks. Each backend is wrapped to implement this task-level interface. Visual Grounding can thus use SAM 3 or Grounding DINO, and OCR can use PaddleOCR-VL or DeepSeek-OCR, while preserving the orchestrator’s calling convention (Appendix <a href="#A3" title="Appendix C Harness Implementation Details ‣ OpenVLHarness: Real World Visual Agentic Tool-Use with Multimodal Memory" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">C</span></a>). Backend selection can reflect accuracy, cost, and latency requirements. Our base instantiation includes nine capabilities: five for perception—Zoom-in, Visual Grounding, OCR, Depth Estimation, and Camera Trajectory; three for information seeking—Text Search, Image Search, and Webpage Visit; and a Python Coding Agent for computation over intermediate results. Please refer to Appendix <a href="#A3" title="Appendix C Harness Implementation Details ‣ OpenVLHarness: Real World Visual Agentic Tool-Use with Multimodal Memory" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">C</span></a> and Table <a href="#A3.T18" title="Table 18 ‣ Capabilities and backends. ‣ Appendix C Harness Implementation Details ‣ OpenVLHarness: Real World Visual Agentic Tool-Use with Multimodal Memory" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">18</span></a> for their implementation details.</p>
</div>
</section>
<section id="S2.SS1.SSS0.Px2" class="ltx_paragraph">
<h4 class="ltx_title ltx_title_paragraph">Model-facing evidence rendering.</h4>

<div id="S2.SS1.SSS0.Px2.p1" class="ltx_para ltx_noindent">
<p id="S2.SS1.SSS0.Px2.p1.1" class="ltx_p">Raw tool outputs often require additional processing to become useful observations. An RLE mask encodes a region precisely, but serializing that encoding into the context does not show the orchestrator which object was segmented. Dense numerical arrays can likewise consume substantial context without providing useful, interpretable evidence.</p>
</div>
<div id="S2.SS1.SSS0.Px2.p2" class="ltx_para ltx_noindent">
<p id="S2.SS1.SSS0.Px2.p2.1" class="ltx_p">Each capability therefore includes an output processor that renders its result into a model-facing observation. Depending on the capability, this observation combines visual attachments, concise natural-language descriptions, compact structured information, and references to reusable artifacts. Grounding can return a labeled mask overlay with the number of detected objects and normalized bounding boxes; depth estimation can return a colorized map; and Camera Trajectory can return a visualization of the recovered trajectory. The underlying masks, arrays, and camera parameters are passed to memory for later reuse. Figure <a href="#S2.F2" title="Figure 2 ‣ 2 Harness Design ‣ OpenVLHarness: Real World Visual Agentic Tool-Use with Multimodal Memory" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">2</span></a> illustrates grounding for “cabinet” in <span id="S2.SS1.SSS0.Px2.p2.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_typewriter">input_image_3</span>. The capability returns a mask overlay with a description of the five grounded objects and their bounding boxes, allowing the orchestrator to inspect the detections. If a subsequent step requires a depth measurement for each cabinet, this rendered observation supplies the visual context, while the retained masks and a depth array supply the data for computation. The memory mechanism below connects these steps.</p>
</div>
</section>
</section>
<section id="S2.SS2" class="ltx_subsection">
<h3 class="ltx_title ltx_title_subsection"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_subsection">2.2 </span>Persistent Multimodal Memory</h3>

<div id="S2.SS2.p1" class="ltx_para ltx_noindent">
<p id="S2.SS2.p1.1" class="ltx_p">The capability layer makes tool results interpretable; persistent multimodal memory makes their underlying artifacts addressable and reusable. A later tool call must be able to identify a particular image, mask, or array and access its contents. Repeating a description of a previous result does not provide this access.</p>
</div>
<div id="S2.SS2.p2" class="ltx_para ltx_noindent">
<p id="S2.SS2.p2.1" class="ltx_p">We maintain an explicit artifact registry for each query, with entries retained across its reasoning trajectory. The registry separates an artifact’s payload from the handle used to reference it. The orchestrator sees available names and compact descriptors, while the harness retains the underlying values and resolves references when needed. The registry contains three categories:</p>
</div>
<div id="S2.SS2.p3" class="ltx_para ltx_noindent">
<ul id="S2.I1" class="ltx_itemize" style="--ltx-enum-leftmargin:1.4em;">
<li id="S2.I1.i1" class="ltx_item" style="list-style-type:none;"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_item">•</span> 
<div id="S2.I1.i1.p1" class="ltx_para ltx_noindent">
<p id="S2.I1.i1.p1.1" class="ltx_p"><span id="S2.I1.i1.p1.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">Image artifacts:</span>
input, tool-generated, and retrieved images, exposed through handles such as <span id="S2.I1.i1.p1.1.2" class="ltx_text ltx_font_typewriter">input_image_3</span> and <span id="S2.I1.i1.p1.1.3" class="ltx_text ltx_font_typewriter">tool_generated_image_6</span>. Duplicate images reuse an existing registry entry.</p>
</div></li>
<li id="S2.I1.i2" class="ltx_item" style="list-style-type:none;"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_item">•</span> 
<div id="S2.I1.i2.p1" class="ltx_para ltx_noindent">
<p id="S2.I1.i2.p1.1" class="ltx_p"><span id="S2.I1.i2.p1.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">Structured data bindings:</span>
named values containing boxes, scores, masks, depth arrays, camera parameters, OCR results, and other tool outputs. Each binding has a descriptor identifying its fields and types, including array shapes where applicable.</p>
</div></li>
<li id="S2.I1.i3" class="ltx_item" style="list-style-type:none;"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_item">•</span> 
<div id="S2.I1.i3.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S2.I1.i3.p1.1" class="ltx_p"><span id="S2.I1.i3.p1.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">Web references:</span>
retrieved URLs exposed through handles such as <span id="S2.I1.i3.p1.1.2" class="ltx_text ltx_font_typewriter">webpage_url_1</span>, allowing later navigation without requiring the model to reproduce the original URL.</p>
</div></li>
</ul>
</div>
<section id="S2.SS2.SSS0.Px1" class="ltx_paragraph">
<h4 class="ltx_title ltx_title_paragraph">Registering and reusing artifacts.</h4>

<div id="S2.SS2.SSS0.Px1.p1" class="ltx_para ltx_noindent">
<p id="S2.SS2.SSS0.Px1.p1.1" class="ltx_p">After a successful tool call, the harness registers supported artifacts and associates their handles with the capability’s rendered observation. Images are delivered as visual attachments with named references; structured payloads remain accessible through data bindings. An <em id="S2.SS2.SSS0.Px1.p1.1.1" class="ltx_emph ltx_font_italic">Environment Update</em> advertises the available image handles, data bindings, and web references after each tool turn, exposing the current namespace without repeating every artifact’s full payload. In the cabinet example, the grounding call adds an overlay image and bindings for the detection data, including bounding boxes and RLE masks.</p>
</div>
<div id="S2.SS2.SSS0.Px1.p2" class="ltx_para ltx_noindent">
<p id="S2.SS2.SSS0.Px1.p2.1" class="ltx_p">When a subsequent call refers to an artifact, the harness resolves its handle to the input required by the receiving tool. An image handle supplies the image for a later visual operation, a web reference supplies a URL for Webpage Visit, and a data binding supplies values for code execution. The orchestrator can thus reuse the grounding and depth results without reconstructing their contents from earlier descriptions. References connect retained evidence across tools and turns. The detailed representation and handles can be found in Appendix <a href="#A3.SS4" title="C.4 Multimodal Memory Artifacts ‣ Appendix C Harness Implementation Details ‣ OpenVLHarness: Real World Visual Agentic Tool-Use with Multimodal Memory" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">C.4</span></a> and Table <a href="#A3.T19" title="Table 19 ‣ C.4 Multimodal Memory Artifacts ‣ Appendix C Harness Implementation Details ‣ OpenVLHarness: Real World Visual Agentic Tool-Use with Multimodal Memory" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">19</span></a>.</p>
</div>
</section>
<section id="S2.SS2.SSS0.Px2" class="ltx_paragraph">
<h4 class="ltx_title ltx_title_paragraph">Memory-grounded computation.</h4>

<div id="S2.SS2.SSS0.Px2.p1" class="ltx_para ltx_noindent">
<p id="S2.SS2.SSS0.Px2.p1.1" class="ltx_p">To compute depth within the detected cabinets, the orchestrator delegates a natural-language objective to the Python Coding Agent, such as “compute the average depth within each detected region.” The harness supplies descriptors of the available memory bindings to a fresh code-generation context of the same backbone model. The generated program executes with the actual binding values available as named variables, allowing it to combine the grounding masks with the depth array. Execution results and feedback return in language, with supported output artifacts registered for subsequent reuse. The orchestrator can then use the result or revise the objective in a later call.</p>
</div>
<div id="S2.SS2.SSS0.Px2.p2" class="ltx_para ltx_noindent">
<p id="S2.SS2.SSS0.Px2.p2.1" class="ltx_p">This mechanism also supports filtering detections, aggregating OCR values, and other computations over intermediate results without requiring the orchestrator to serialize large arrays into its request. The capability layer supplies reusable operations and interpretable observations; memory and code execution connect their outputs into longer reasoning procedures.</p>
</div>
<figure id="S2.F3" class="ltx_figure"><img src="2610.26860v1/qualitative_examples.png" id="S2.F3.g1" class="ltx_graphics ltx_centering ltx_img_landscape" style="aspect-ratio:548/237;" width="548" height="237" alt="Refer to caption">
<figcaption class="ltx_caption ltx_centering"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_figure">Figure 3: </span><span id="S2.F3.6" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">Qualitative examples of OpenVLHarness.</span>
<span id="S2.F3.7" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">(a)</span> Camera poses, depth, and grounding are combined in code to measure a 3D distance (Example <a href="#A6" title="Appendix F Qualitative Examples ‣ OpenVLHarness: Real World Visual Agentic Tool-Use with Multimodal Memory" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">F</span></a>).
<span id="S2.F3.8" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">(b)</span> Grounded crops are passed to OCR and image search, and the answer is verified on the web (Example <a href="#A6" title="Appendix F Qualitative Examples ‣ OpenVLHarness: Real World Visual Agentic Tool-Use with Multimodal Memory" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">F</span></a>).
<span id="S2.F3.9" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">(c)</span> Masks from two grounding queries are reconciled in code to reach the exact count (Example <a href="#A6" title="Appendix F Qualitative Examples ‣ OpenVLHarness: Real World Visual Agentic Tool-Use with Multimodal Memory" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">F</span></a>).</figcaption>
</figure>
</section>
<section id="S2.SS2.SSS0.Px3" class="ltx_paragraph">
<h4 class="ltx_title ltx_title_paragraph">Qualitative examples.</h4>

<div id="S2.SS2.SSS0.Px3.p1" class="ltx_para ltx_noindent">
<p id="S2.SS2.SSS0.Px3.p1.1" class="ltx_p">Figure <a href="#S2.F3" title="Figure 3 ‣ Memory-grounded computation. ‣ 2.2 Persistent Multimodal Memory ‣ 2 Harness Design ‣ OpenVLHarness: Real World Visual Agentic Tool-Use with Multimodal Memory" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">3</span></a> shows three trajectories from our evaluation. In each, the agent chains several capabilities and reuses their outputs through memory: camera poses, depth maps, and grounding masks become bindings for exact 3D computation (a); grounded crops are passed by identifier to zoom-in, OCR, and image search before the answer is verified on the web (b); and masks from two grounding queries are reconciled in code to correct an undercount (c).</p>
</div>
</section>
</section>
