<figcaption class="ltx_caption"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_table">Table 2: </span><span id="S3.T2.5" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">Reasoning effort versus harness, by domain.</span> GPT-6 Luna alone (Base) and with Codex (CX), Visual Sketchpad (VS), or OpenVLHarness (Ours) on the full evaluation splits of our <math id="S3.T2.m7" class="ltx_Math" alttext="22" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mn>22</mn><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">22</annotation></semantics></math> benchmarks. Rows average each domain’s benchmarks: Grounding (grounded perception, <math id="S3.T2.m8" class="ltx_Math" alttext="5" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mn>5</mn><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">5</annotation></semantics></math>), VS &amp; DR (visual search and deep research, <math id="S3.T2.m9" class="ltx_Math" alttext="6" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mn>6</mn><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">6</annotation></semantics></math>), General VQA (general VQA and hallucination, <math id="S3.T2.m10" class="ltx_Math" alttext="6" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mn>6</mn><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">6</annotation></semantics></math>), and Spatial (spatial reasoning, <math id="S3.T2.m11" class="ltx_Math" alttext="5" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mn>5</mn><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">5</annotation></semantics></math>). <em id="S3.T2.6" class="ltx_emph ltx_font_italic">$ / 1k questions</em> is the model-token cost at Standard-tier prices, including reasoning tokens and excluding tool execution and judging, averaged over the <math id="S3.T2.m12" class="ltx_Math" alttext="22" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mn>22</mn><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">22</annotation></semantics></math> benchmarks. At each effort, bold marks the best score, and in the cost row the cheapest method, with the second cheapest underlined. Low effort is in Appendix Table <a href="#A2.T13" title="Table 13 ‣ Setup. ‣ B.6 Reasoning-Effort Study: Setup and Full Results ‣ Appendix B Additional Results and Analysis ‣ OpenVLHarness: Real World Visual Agentic Tool-Use with Multimodal Memory" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">13</span></a>.</figcaption>
</figure>
<div id="S3.SS3.p1" class="ltx_para ltx_noindent">
<p id="S3.SS3.p1.1" class="ltx_p">OpenVLHarness makes several model calls per question, each carrying tool outputs and memory, so it spends more model tokens than prompting the base model directly. To test whether its gains come simply from extra computation, we run Base, Codex, Visual Sketchpad, and OpenVLHarness with GPT-6 Luna at five reasoning-effort settings, from low to max, on the full evaluation splits of all 22 benchmarks (<math id="S3.SS3.p1.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="26{,}229" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mn>26,229</mn><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">26{,}229</annotation></semantics></math> questions per setting). Figure <a href="#S0.F1" title="Figure 1 ‣ OpenVLHarness: Real World Visual Agentic Tool-Use with Multimodal Memory" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">1</span></a>c reports the 22-benchmark averages against model-token cost, and Table <a href="#S3.T2" title="Table 2 ‣ 3.3 Can More Reasoning Replace the Harness? ‣ 3 Experiments ‣ OpenVLHarness: Real World Visual Agentic Tool-Use with Multimodal Memory" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">2</span></a> breaks them down by domain.</p>
</div>
<div id="S3.SS3.p2" class="ltx_para ltx_noindent">
<p id="S3.SS3.p2.1" class="ltx_p">Additional effort yields limited gains for the other methods: from low to max effort, Base rises from 44.3 to 48.0 and Visual Sketchpad from 44.7 to 48.8, while Codex, the weakest method at low effort, rises from 36.9 to 48.6. The step from xhigh to max effort alone multiplies their model-token cost by 1.5–2.7. At max effort, all three remain well below OpenVLHarness at medium effort (56.5), which spends at most 37% of their model-token cost per question, and OpenVLHarness at low effort (49.9) is already on par with their max-effort averages. The domain breakdown shows where the harness matters most. On grounded perception and on visual search and deep research, every effort setting of OpenVLHarness, including low, scores above every setting of the other methods, and at max effort it leads the best of them by 15.6 and 12.4 points. On general VQA and hallucination and on spatial reasoning, the harness benefits from more reasoning: at low effort, OpenVLHarness trails Base slightly (61.5 vs. 61.6 and 54.5 vs. 56.5), but from medium effort onward it scores above every setting of every other method in every domain, and at max effort it leads by 6.1 and 7.1 points. Raising the effort of OpenVLHarness itself lifts its average from 56.5 to 60.1. Acquiring and composing multimodal evidence therefore complements stronger reasoning: additional computation remains useful, but does not eliminate the benefit of the harness within the tested budgets. Costs cover model tokens, including reasoning tokens, and exclude tool execution and judging; Appendix <a href="#A2.SS6" title="B.6 Reasoning-Effort Study: Setup and Full Results ‣ Appendix B Additional Results and Analysis ‣ OpenVLHarness: Real World Visual Agentic Tool-Use with Multimodal Memory" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">B.6</span></a> gives the setup, per-benchmark scores, token usage, and how often Codex executes code.</p>
</div>
</section>
<section id="S3.SS4" class="ltx_subsection">
<h3 class="ltx_title ltx_title_subsection"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_subsection">3.4 </span>Ablation Study</h3>

<div id="S3.SS4.p1" class="ltx_para ltx_noindent">
<p id="S3.SS4.p1.1" class="ltx_p">Table <a href="#S3.T3" title="Table 3 ‣ 3.4 Ablation Study ‣ 3 Experiments ‣ OpenVLHarness: Real World Visual Agentic Tool-Use with Multimodal Memory" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">3</span></a> examines the two mechanisms introduced in Sec. <a href="#S2" title="2 Harness Design ‣ OpenVLHarness: Real World Visual Agentic Tool-Use with Multimodal Memory" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">2</span></a>: the <em id="S3.SS4.p1.1.1" class="ltx_emph ltx_font_italic">capability layer</em>, which combines task-level tool interfaces with model-facing evidence rendering, followed by <em id="S3.SS4.p1.1.2" class="ltx_emph ltx_font_italic">persistent multimodal memory</em>, which makes the resulting artifacts addressable and reusable across turns. Starting from the no-tool baseline, we add raw tool access, then the capability layer, and finally memory, using a textual-memory variant as an intermediate comparison (details in Appendix <a href="#A2.SS2" title="B.2 Ablation Study Details ‣ Appendix B Additional Results and Analysis ‣ OpenVLHarness: Real World Visual Agentic Tool-Use with Multimodal Memory" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">B.2</span></a>).</p>
