<figcaption class="ltx_caption ltx_centering"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_table">Table 3: </span><span id="S3.T3.4" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">Ablation study of OpenVLHarness across model scales.</span> We progressively add raw tool access, capability layer, textual memory, and multimodal memory to the no-tool baseline. Results are reported across four domains and overall for Qwen3-VL 8B and 32B models, averaged over three runs; standard deviations are provided in Table <a href="#A8.T27" title="Table 27 ‣ Main results. ‣ Appendix H Run-to-Run Variability ‣ OpenVLHarness: Real World Visual Agentic Tool-Use with Multimodal Memory" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">27</span></a>. Bold marks the best result in each column.</figcaption>
</figure>
<div id="S3.SS4.p2" class="ltx_para ltx_noindent">
<p id="S3.SS4.p2.1" class="ltx_p">Raw tool access raises the overall average by <math id="S3.SS4.p2.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="+3.8" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mo>+</mo><mn>3.8</mn></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">+3.8</annotation></semantics></math> points at 8B and <math id="S3.SS4.p2.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="+4.3" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mo>+</mo><mn>4.3</mn></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">+4.3</annotation></semantics></math> at 32B, with the largest gains in grounded perception. Adding the capability layer restores full tool schemas and renders heterogeneous outputs into concise observations, yielding another <math id="S3.SS4.p2.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="+3.4" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mo>+</mo><mn>3.4</mn></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">+3.4</annotation></semantics></math> and <math id="S3.SS4.p2.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="+3.2" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mo>+</mo><mn>3.2</mn></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">+3.2</annotation></semantics></math> points. These gains support the joint value of task-level tool interfaces and model-facing evidence rendering; the comparison does not separate their individual contributions.</p>
</div>
<div id="S3.SS4.p3" class="ltx_para ltx_noindent">
<p id="S3.SS4.p3.1" class="ltx_p">Adding textual memory enables structured data bindings, web references, and Environment Updates, but represents tool-generated images only through descriptions, without reusable image handles. This adds <math id="S3.SS4.p3.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="+0.7" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mo>+</mo><mn>0.7</mn></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">+0.7</annotation></semantics></math> points at 8B and <math id="S3.SS4.p3.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="+0.3" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mo>+</mo><mn>0.3</mn></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">+0.3</annotation></semantics></math> at 32B. Completing persistent multimodal memory with visual attachments and reusable image handles adds a further <math id="S3.SS4.p3.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="+3.1" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mo>+</mo><mn>3.1</mn></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">+3.1</annotation></semantics></math> and <math id="S3.SS4.p3.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="+4.5" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mo>+</mo><mn>4.5</mn></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">+4.5</annotation></semantics></math>, bringing the full harness to <math id="S3.SS4.p3.m5" class="ltx_Math" alttext="47.1" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mn>47.1</mn><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">47.1</annotation></semantics></math> and <math id="S3.SS4.p3.m6" class="ltx_Math" alttext="52.5" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mn>52.5</mn><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">52.5</annotation></semantics></math>. The largest gains from this final step occur in visual search and deep research (<math id="S3.SS4.p3.m7" class="ltx_Math" alttext="+6.2" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mo>+</mo><mn>6.2</mn></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">+6.2</annotation></semantics></math>/<math id="S3.SS4.p3.m8" class="ltx_Math" alttext="+3.5" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mo>+</mo><mn>3.5</mn></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">+3.5</annotation></semantics></math>), with additional gains in spatial reasoning (<math id="S3.SS4.p3.m9" class="ltx_Math" alttext="+2.8" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mo>+</mo><mn>2.8</mn></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">+2.8</annotation></semantics></math>/<math id="S3.SS4.p3.m10" class="ltx_Math" alttext="+3.1" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mo>+</mo><mn>3.1</mn></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">+3.1</annotation></semantics></math>). The results support the method’s complementary mechanisms: interpretable tool observations, followed by retained multimodal artifacts that can be revisited and composed across turns.</p>
</div>
</section>
<section id="S3.SS5" class="ltx_subsection">
<h3 class="ltx_title ltx_title_subsection"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_subsection">3.5 </span>Failure Analysis</h3>

<div id="S3.SS5.p1" class="ltx_para ltx_noindent">
<p id="S3.SS5.p1.1" class="ltx_p">We use Kimi K3 to critique <math id="S3.SS5.p1.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="9{,}893" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mn>9,893</mn><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">9{,}893</annotation></semantics></math> failed Qwen3-VL-8B trajectories across 22 benchmarks. Given the question, answer, images, trajectory, and toolbox, the critic assesses each tool call and identifies the trajectory’s primary failure cause and proposes a fix. Its labels agree with human annotations on <math id="S3.SS5.p1.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="92\%" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mn>92</mn><mo>%</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">92\%</annotation></semantics></math> of 100 sampled failures. The protocol, coverage, and taxonomy are detailed in Appendix <a href="#A5.SS1" title="E.1 Critique Protocol ‣ Appendix E Failure Analysis and Harness Optimization Details ‣ OpenVLHarness: Real World Visual Agentic Tool-Use with Multimodal Memory" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">E.1</span></a>.</p>
</div>
<div id="S3.SS5.p2" class="ltx_para ltx_noindent">
<p id="S3.SS5.p2.1" class="ltx_p">Surprisingly, we find that failure patterns differ across domains (Fig. <a href="#S3.F4" title="Figure 4 ‣ 3.6 Harness Optimization ‣ 3 Experiments ‣ OpenVLHarness: Real World Visual Agentic Tool-Use with Multimodal Memory" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">4</span></a>a). Despite substantial overall gains, grounded perception remains challenging in crowded images with more than 25 objects, where missed or merged instances limit exhaustive detection. Such perception errors account for the dominant “incorrect tool output” category (<math id="S3.SS5.p2.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="75\%" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mn>75</mn><mo>%</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">75\%</annotation></semantics></math> of failures in this domain). Failures in visual search and deep research most often involve an omitted tool call (<math id="S3.SS5.p2.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="49\%" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mn>49</mn><mo>%</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">49\%</annotation></semantics></math>), while failures in spatial reasoning and in general VQA and hallucination involve both omitted tool calls and reasoning errors, consistent with our hypothesis that the model has limited proficiency in spatial reasoning and tool use. Failures involving unusable or ignored correct tool outputs are uncommon (<math id="S3.SS5.p2.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="3" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mn>3</mn><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">3</annotation></semantics></math>–<math id="S3.SS5.p2.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="8\%" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mn>8</mn><mo>%</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">8\%</annotation></semantics></math> across domains), suggesting that the capability layer generally presents retrieved evidence in a usable form.</p>
</div>
<div id="S3.SS5.p3" class="ltx_para ltx_noindent">
<p id="S3.SS5.p3.1" class="ltx_p">Failure causes alone do not determine how to specialize the harness: an omitted call may require clearer instructions or a new composition of tools. We therefore combine the cause and proposed fix into remedy channels (Fig. <a href="#S3.F4" title="Figure 4 ‣ 3.6 Harness Optimization ‣ 3 Experiments ‣ OpenVLHarness: Real World Visual Agentic Tool-Use with Multimodal Memory" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">4</span></a>b). Constructing new task-specific tools can make evidence interpretation explicit (A), enforce systematic verification, filtering of tool results (B), or supply a missing tool composition (C). In contrast, harness text changes (Y)—edits to the system prompt, tool descriptions, and output messages—guide the model to call existing tools when needed and use their results appropriately. The remaining channel (Z) covers cases without a proposed remedy. These diagnostic recommendations guide optimization rather than predict recoverable accuracy, since the critique covers only failed trajectories. Exact mapping rules and per-dataset shares are in Appendix <a href="#A5.SS2" title="E.2 Remedy Channels and Per-Dataset Results ‣ Appendix E Failure Analysis and Harness Optimization Details ‣ OpenVLHarness: Real World Visual Agentic Tool-Use with Multimodal Memory" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">E.2</span></a>.</p>
</div>
</section>
<section id="S3.SS6" class="ltx_subsection">
<h3 class="ltx_title ltx_title_subsection"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_subsection">3.6 </span>Harness Optimization</h3>

<figure id="S3.F4" class="ltx_figure"><object type="image/svg+xml" data="2610.26860v1/failure_analysis.svg" id="S3.F4.g1" class="ltx_graphics ltx_centering ltx_img_landscape" style="aspect-ratio:548/237;" width="548" height="237"></object>
<figcaption class="ltx_caption ltx_centering"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_figure">Figure 4: </span><span id="S3.F4.7" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">Failure diagnosis and harness specialization with Qwen3-VL-8B.</span> We critique failure trajectories using Kimi K3. <span id="S3.F4.8" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">(a)</span> Distribution of failure causes across four domains. <span id="S3.F4.9" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">(b)</span> Proposed remedies: grouped into creating task-specific tools (A–C), updating harness text (Y), and no proposed remedy (Z). <span id="S3.F4.10" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">(c)</span> Four datasets selected for contrasting remedy profiles: Y (orange) dominates ERQA and RealWorldQA, while A–C (blue) dominates SparBench and FREAK-VQA. <span id="S3.F4.11" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">(d)</span> Gains from harness search and tool generation over the unoptimized harness. Harness search is more effective on datasets dominated by text-level changes, while tool generation yields larger gains when task-specific tools are preferred.</figcaption>
</figure>
<div id="S3.SS6.p1" class="ltx_para ltx_noindent">
<p id="S3.SS6.p1.1" class="ltx_p">When adapting OpenVLHarness to a custom domain, users can use failures on their own data to identify which parts of the harness need refinement. We provide two coding-agent-driven workflows for specializing the general-purpose harness without updating model weights. <span id="S3.SS6.p1.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">Harness search</span> edits system prompts, tool descriptions, and evidence-rendering templates, selecting candidates through repeated evaluation. <span id="S3.SS6.p1.1.2" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">Tool generation</span> implements and refines task-specific tools by composing existing capabilities (algorithms in Appendix <a href="#A5.SS3" title="E.3 Optimization Algorithms ‣ Appendix E Failure Analysis and Harness Optimization Details ‣ OpenVLHarness: Real World Visual Agentic Tool-Use with Multimodal Memory" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">E.3</span></a>). To compare them, we select four datasets with contrasting remedy profiles (Fig. <a href="#S3.F4" title="Figure 4 ‣ 3.6 Harness Optimization ‣ 3 Experiments ‣ OpenVLHarness: Real World Visual Agentic Tool-Use with Multimodal Memory" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">4</span></a>c): harness text changes (Y) dominate ERQA (<math id="S3.SS6.p1.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="71\%" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mn>71</mn><mo>%</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">71\%</annotation></semantics></math>) and RealWorldQA (<math id="S3.SS6.p1.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="56\%" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mn>56</mn><mo>%</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">56\%</annotation></semantics></math>), whereas task-specific tools (A+B+C) dominate SparBench (<math id="S3.SS6.p1.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="75\%" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mn>75</mn><mo>%</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">75\%</annotation></semantics></math>) and FREAK-VQA (<math id="S3.SS6.p1.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="62\%" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mn>62</mn><mo>%</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">62\%</annotation></semantics></math>). We run both workflows on all four datasets with Qwen3-VL-8B, testing whether their relative benefits align with these profiles.</p>
</div>
<div id="S3.SS6.p2" class="ltx_para ltx_noindent">
<p id="S3.SS6.p2.1" class="ltx_p">The results follow this distinction (Fig. <a href="#S3.F4" title="Figure 4 ‣ 3.6 Harness Optimization ‣ 3 Experiments ‣ OpenVLHarness: Real World Visual Agentic Tool-Use with Multimodal Memory" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">4</span></a>d). Harness search improves ERQA and RealWorldQA by <math id="S3.SS6.p2.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="+4.1" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mo>+</mo><mn>4.1</mn></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">+4.1</annotation></semantics></math> and <math id="S3.SS6.p2.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="+3.7" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mo>+</mo><mn>3.7</mn></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">+3.7</annotation></semantics></math> points, compared with <math id="S3.SS6.p2.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="+0.2" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mo>+</mo><mn>0.2</mn></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">+0.2</annotation></semantics></math> and <math id="S3.SS6.p2.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="+0.1" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mo>+</mo><mn>0.1</mn></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">+0.1</annotation></semantics></math> from tool generation. Conversely, tool generation improves SparBench and FREAK-VQA by <math id="S3.SS6.p2.m5" class="ltx_Math" alttext="+8.2" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mo>+</mo><mn>8.2</mn></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">+8.2</annotation></semantics></math> and <math id="S3.SS6.p2.m6" class="ltx_Math" alttext="+4.5" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mo>+</mo><mn>4.5</mn></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">+4.5</annotation></semantics></math>, while harness search yields <math id="S3.SS6.p2.m7" class="ltx_Math" alttext="-0.5" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mo>−</mo><mn>0.5</mn></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">-0.5</annotation></semantics></math> and <math id="S3.SS6.p2.m8" class="ltx_Math" alttext="-1.0" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mo>−</mo><mn>1.0</mn></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">-1.0</annotation></semantics></math>. The generated tools compute metric 3D relations on SparBench and make visibility decisions explicit on FREAK-VQA, matching the missing-composition and evidence-interpretation needs identified by the critique (Table <a href="#A5.T24" title="Table 24 ‣ Task-specific tools. ‣ E.4 Optimized Harnesses and Generated Tools ‣ Appendix E Failure Analysis and Harness Optimization Details ‣ OpenVLHarness: Real World Visual Agentic Tool-Use with Multimodal Memory" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">24</span></a>). These results suggest a practical approach to adaptation on custom data: diagnose the prevailing failures, then use harness search to improve the use of existing capabilities or tool generation to add the task-specific operations the domain requires.</p>
</div>
</section>
</section>
<section id="S4" class="ltx_section">
<h2 class="ltx_title ltx_title_section"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_section">4 </span>Related Work</h2>

<section id="S4.SS0.SSS0.Px1" class="ltx_paragraph">
<h4 class="ltx_title ltx_title_paragraph">Visual Tool-Use Baselines.</h4>

<div id="S4.SS0.SSS0.Px1.p1" class="ltx_para ltx_noindent">
<p id="S4.SS0.SSS0.Px1.p1.1" class="ltx_p">Early visual tool-use work treats the problem as program synthesis, compiling a question into a sequential plan that invokes vision tools in a pipeline to produce an answer <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_citep">(<a href="#bib.bib12" title="" class="ltx_ref">Surís et al., 2023</a>; <a href="#bib.bib13" title="" class="ltx_ref">Gupta and Kembhavi, 2023</a>; <a href="#bib.bib14" title="" class="ltx_ref">Kamali and Kordjamshidi, 2026</a>; <a href="#bib.bib15" title="" class="ltx_ref">Zhao et al., 2025</a>; <a href="#bib.bib21" title="" class="ltx_ref">Wu et al., 2023</a>; <a href="#bib.bib22" title="" class="ltx_ref">Yang et al., 2023b</a>; <a href="#bib.bib17" title="" class="ltx_ref">Gao et al., 2023</a>; <a href="#bib.bib23" title="" class="ltx_ref">Castrejon et al., 2024</a>; <a href="#bib.bib19" title="" class="ltx_ref">Zhang et al., 2026b</a>; <a href="#bib.bib20" title="" class="ltx_ref">Zhou et al., 2024</a>; <a href="#bib.bib18" title="" class="ltx_ref">Menon et al., 2024</a>; <a href="#bib.bib112" title="" class="ltx_ref">Chen et al., 2024b</a>; <a href="#bib.bib115" title="" class="ltx_ref">You et al., 2023</a>)</cite>; because these plans are fixed in place, they cannot be adjusted based on tool execution results. A later line adopts multi-turn frameworks that interleave reasoning with visual feedback, but depends on brittle, task-specific framework designs that require extensive training to work <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_citep">(<a href="#bib.bib1" title="" class="ltx_ref">Ma et al., 2025</a>; <a href="#bib.bib2" title="" class="ltx_ref">Zhou et al., 2025a</a>; <a href="#bib.bib3" title="" class="ltx_ref">Song et al., 2026</a>; <a href="#bib.bib6" title="" class="ltx_ref">Wang et al., 2025a</a>; <a href="#bib.bib5" title="" class="ltx_ref">Zheng et al., 2025</a>; <a href="#bib.bib4" title="" class="ltx_ref">Hong et al., 2026a</a>; <a href="#bib.bib7" title="" class="ltx_ref">Su et al., 2025</a>; <a href="#bib.bib8" title="" class="ltx_ref">Wu et al., 2025b</a>; <a href="#bib.bib16" title="" class="ltx_ref">Zhao et al., 2026</a>)</cite>, and many domain-specific visual tool-use frameworks fall into the same category <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_citep">(<a href="#bib.bib9" title="" class="ltx_ref">Chen et al., 2025a</a>; <a href="#bib.bib53" title="" class="ltx_ref">Wu et al., 2025a</a>; <a href="#bib.bib54" title="" class="ltx_ref">Narayan et al., 2025</a>; <a href="#bib.bib47" title="" class="ltx_ref">Geng et al., 2026</a>; <a href="#bib.bib75" title="" class="ltx_ref">Huang et al., 2026</a>; <a href="#bib.bib52" title="" class="ltx_ref">Yao et al., 2026</a>; <a href="#bib.bib35" title="" class="ltx_ref">Yang et al., 2026</a>; <a href="#bib.bib36" title="" class="ltx_ref">Du et al., 2026</a>; <a href="#bib.bib63" title="" class="ltx_ref">Wang et al., 2025d</a>)</cite>. Closest to our setting, Visual Sketchpad <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_citep">(<a href="#bib.bib10" title="" class="ltx_ref">Hu et al., 2024</a>)</cite> and OctoTools <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_citep">(<a href="#bib.bib11" title="" class="ltx_ref">Lu et al., 2025</a>)</cite> are training-free tool-use frameworks built around manually designed tool interfaces and execution strategies. In contrast, we focus on making the harness itself more adaptable, so its evidence processing and tool composition can be specialized as tools, MLLMs, and target tasks evolve.</p>
</div>
</section>
<section id="S4.SS0.SSS0.Px2" class="ltx_paragraph">
<h4 class="ltx_title ltx_title_paragraph">Working Memory for Tool-Using Agents.</h4>

<div id="S4.SS0.SSS0.Px2.p1" class="ltx_para ltx_noindent">
<p id="S4.SS0.SSS0.Px2.p1.1" class="ltx_p">Language agents maintain intermediate information to support reasoning and action across multiple steps. Context management approaches further separate compact model-visible state from more detailed external information, enabling the agent to selectively recover relevant content when needed <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_citep">(<a href="#bib.bib32" title="" class="ltx_ref">Packer et al., 2023</a>; <a href="#bib.bib88" title="" class="ltx_ref">Lee et al., 2024</a>)</cite>. Tool-using agents have also introduced explicit mechanisms for passing intermediate results across actions. ReWOO <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_citep">(<a href="#bib.bib87" title="" class="ltx_ref">Xu et al., 2023</a>)</cite> represents tool evidence through referenceable variables, while CodeAct <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_citep">(<a href="#bib.bib86" title="" class="ltx_ref">Wang et al., 2024a</a>)</cite> maintains intermediate results as variables in an executable interpreter. HuggingGPT <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_citep">(<a href="#bib.bib90" title="" class="ltx_ref">Shen et al., 2023</a>)</cite> and LLMCompiler <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_citep">(<a href="#bib.bib89" title="" class="ltx_ref">Kim et al., 2024</a>)</cite> go further in using symbolic references to intermediate tool outputs that are resolved when executing dependent downstream calls. Building on these ideas, OpenVLHarness extends them to the multimodal setting, maintaining a unified registry of typed artifacts spanning images, structured data, and web evidence. It exposes compact model-facing handles while preserving the underlying artifacts for later inspection, composition, and reuse through modality-appropriate downstream tools.</p>
</div>
</section>
</section>
<section id="S5" class="ltx_section">
<h2 class="ltx_title ltx_title_section"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_section">5 </span>Conclusion</h2>

<div id="S5.p1" class="ltx_para ltx_noindent">
<p id="S5.p1.1" class="ltx_p">We presented OpenVLHarness, a general harness for real-world visual agentic tool use built around a capability layer and persistent multimodal memory. The capability layer combines task-level tool interfaces with evidence rendering, enabling the orchestrator to invoke heterogeneous tools and inspect their outputs. Persistent multimodal memory retains the corresponding artifacts for reuse across turns, including computation over stored data through a Python Coding Agent. Together, these mechanisms support reasoning that acquires and composes multimodal evidence. Evaluation across 22 benchmarks and eight backbones demonstrates broad gains, while component ablations show the incremental benefits of the capability layer and multimodal memory. Harness search and tool generation further specialize the system on selected domains without updating the underlying MLLM weights. We release the harness, tools, evaluation protocols, and optimization workflows to support further work on multimodal tool-use agents.</p>
</div>
<div class="ltx_pagination ltx_role_newpage"></div>
</section>
