<section id="A1" class="ltx_appendix">
<h2 class="ltx_title ltx_title_appendix"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_appendix">Appendix A </span>Tool-Use Behavior</h2>

<div id="A1.p1" class="ltx_para ltx_noindent">
<p id="A1.p1.1" class="ltx_p">Table <a href="#S3.T1" title="Table 1 ‣ Tasks. ‣ 3.1 Tasks, Models, and Baselines ‣ 3 Experiments ‣ OpenVLHarness: Real World Visual Agentic Tool-Use with Multimodal Memory" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">1</span></a> reports the accuracy of OpenVLHarness. This section characterizes the behavior
that produces it: how frequently the agent invokes a tool, which capabilities it invokes, and how those
invocations relate to accuracy relative to the same model without tools. The analysis covers five base
models — Qwen3-VL-8B, Qwen3-VL-32B, GPT-6 Luna, GPT-6 Sol, and GPT-6 Astra, the GPT-6 models at medium reasoning effort — and reads every rollout of the OpenVLHarness runs behind Tables <a href="#S3.T1" title="Table 1 ‣ Tasks. ‣ 3.1 Tasks, Models, and Baselines ‣ 3 Experiments ‣ OpenVLHarness: Real World Visual Agentic Tool-Use with Multimodal Memory" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">1</span></a> and <a href="#A2.T7" title="Table 7 ‣ B.1 Additional Backbones ‣ Appendix B Additional Results and Analysis ‣ OpenVLHarness: Real World Visual Agentic Tool-Use with Multimodal Memory" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">7</span></a>, approximately <math id="A1.p1.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="123{,}000" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mn>123,000</mn><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">123{,}000</annotation></semantics></math> rollouts containing <math id="A1.p1.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="224{,}000" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mn>224,000</mn><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">224{,}000</annotation></semantics></math>
tool calls. All counts are exact rather than sampled. A question is <em id="A1.p1.1.1" class="ltx_emph ltx_font_italic">engaged</em> if the agent emitted
at least one tool call on it, regardless of whether the call succeeded.</p>
</div>
<section id="A1.SS0.SSS0.Px1" class="ltx_paragraph">
<h4 class="ltx_title ltx_title_paragraph">Measurement protocol.</h4>

<div id="A1.SS0.SSS0.Px1.p1" class="ltx_para ltx_noindent">
<p id="A1.SS0.SSS0.Px1.p1.1" class="ltx_p">Section <a href="#A1.SS3" title="A.3 Effect of Tool Use on Accuracy ‣ Appendix A Tool-Use Behavior ‣ OpenVLHarness: Real World Visual Agentic Tool-Use with Multimodal Memory" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">A.3</span></a> compares OpenVLHarness against the
<span id="A1.SS0.SSS0.Px1.p1.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">Base</span> columns of
Tables <a href="#S3.T1" title="Table 1 ‣ Tasks. ‣ 3.1 Tasks, Models, and Baselines ‣ 3 Experiments ‣ OpenVLHarness: Real World Visual Agentic Tool-Use with Multimodal Memory" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">1</span></a> and <a href="#A2.T7" title="Table 7 ‣ B.1 Additional Backbones ‣ Appendix B Additional Results and Analysis ‣ OpenVLHarness: Real World Visual Agentic Tool-Use with Multimodal Memory" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">7</span></a> on a per-question basis: both arms answer the same questions and are
scored with the same dataset metric, and differences are averaged over paired rows. This design supports
statements about individual capabilities, since the rows on which a given tool was invoked can be
isolated and both arms evaluated on that subset. The resulting quantities are correlational rather than
causal: the agent selects the rows on which it invokes a tool, and the remaining capabilities are
available on those rows as well. Each per-tool measurement is therefore reported alongside a control,
namely the same paired difference computed on the rows that did not invoke the tool. A capability whose
two values coincide is not, on its own, responsible for the observed gain. The control is a complement
set, so its composition depends on how often the capability is invoked: for a capability used on a small
fraction of questions the control approximates the model’s overall gain, whereas for one used on the
majority of questions the control is a small and atypical remainder.</p>
</div>
</section>
<section id="A1.SS1" class="ltx_subsection">
<h3 class="ltx_title ltx_title_subsection"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_subsection">A.1 </span>Tool-Call Volume and Engagement</h3>

<div id="A1.SS1.p1" class="ltx_para ltx_noindent">
<p id="A1.SS1.p1.1" class="ltx_p">Two quantities characterize how heavily a model uses the tools, and they vary independently: the number
of calls a question costs, and the fraction of questions answered with no call.
Table <a href="#A1.T4" title="Table 4 ‣ A.1 Tool-Call Volume and Engagement ‣ Appendix A Tool-Use Behavior ‣ OpenVLHarness: Real World Visual Agentic Tool-Use with Multimodal Memory" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">4</span></a> reports them per model, and Figure <a href="#A1.F5" title="Figure 5 ‣ A.1 Tool-Call Volume and Engagement ‣ Appendix A Tool-Use Behavior ‣ OpenVLHarness: Real World Visual Agentic Tool-Use with Multimodal Memory" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">5</span></a>
resolves them by domain.</p>
</div>
