<section id="A1.SS1.SSS0.Px1" class="ltx_paragraph">
<h4 class="ltx_title ltx_title_paragraph">Volume and engagement.</h4>

<div id="A1.SS1.SSS0.Px1.p1" class="ltx_para ltx_noindent">
<p id="A1.SS1.SSS0.Px1.p1.1" class="ltx_p">Engagement varies with model scale within a family but not across
families. The smaller models leave a substantial fraction of questions untouched (Qwen3-VL-8B <math id="A1.SS1.SSS0.Px1.p1.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="28.9\%" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mn>28.9</mn><mo>%</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">28.9\%</annotation></semantics></math>,
GPT-6 Luna <math id="A1.SS1.SSS0.Px1.p1.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="25.3\%" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mn>25.3</mn><mo>%</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">25.3\%</annotation></semantics></math>), whereas the larger models rarely do (<math id="A1.SS1.SSS0.Px1.p1.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="6.2\%" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mn>6.2</mn><mo>%</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">6.2\%</annotation></semantics></math>, <math id="A1.SS1.SSS0.Px1.p1.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="4.8\%" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mn>4.8</mn><mo>%</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">4.8\%</annotation></semantics></math> and <math id="A1.SS1.SSS0.Px1.p1.m5" class="ltx_Math" alttext="2.6\%" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mn>2.6</mn><mo>%</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">2.6\%</annotation></semantics></math>). Call volume does not
order the models by accuracy: GPT-6 Luna issues the fewest calls per question of the five and attains
higher accuracy than either Qwen model in Table <a href="#S3.T1" title="Table 1 ‣ Tasks. ‣ 3.1 Tasks, Models, and Baselines ‣ 3 Experiments ‣ OpenVLHarness: Real World Visual Agentic Tool-Use with Multimodal Memory" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">1</span></a>. By domain, grounded perception
engages all five models on nearly every question, while spatial reasoning and general VQA and
hallucination account for most of the unengaged questions in the smaller models.</p>
</div>
</section>
<section id="A1.SS1.SSS0.Px2" class="ltx_paragraph">
<h4 class="ltx_title ltx_title_paragraph">Reliability and context cost.</h4>

<div id="A1.SS1.SSS0.Px2.p1" class="ltx_para ltx_noindent">
<p id="A1.SS1.SSS0.Px2.p1.1" class="ltx_p">Fewer than <math id="A1.SS1.SSS0.Px2.p1.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="1\%" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mn>1</mn><mo>%</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">1\%</annotation></semantics></math> of calls return a tool error for any model, so
execution reliability is not a limiting factor. Repeated calls, defined as byte-identical invocations
within one rollout, are more frequent than errors but remain a small fraction of traffic and do not scale
with model size. The dominant cost is context: a rollout occupies between <math id="A1.SS1.SSS0.Px2.p1.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="8" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mn>8</mn><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">8</annotation></semantics></math> and <math id="A1.SS1.SSS0.Px2.p1.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="14" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mn>14</mn><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">14</annotation></semantics></math> transcript turns,
between two and <math id="A1.SS1.SSS0.Px2.p1.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="3.5" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mn>3.5</mn><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">3.5</annotation></semantics></math> of which are Environment Updates, one emitted at the start and one after each
call.</p>
</div>
</section>
</section>
<section id="A1.SS2" class="ltx_subsection">
<h3 class="ltx_title ltx_title_subsection"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_subsection">A.2 </span>Distribution of Tool Calls</h3>

<figure id="A1.F6" class="ltx_figure"><object type="image/svg+xml" data="2610.26860v1/tool_use_mix.svg" id="A1.F6.g1" class="ltx_graphics ltx_centering ltx_img_landscape" style="aspect-ratio:548/166;" width="548" height="166"></object>
<figcaption class="ltx_caption ltx_centering"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_figure">Figure 6: </span><span id="A1.F6.3" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">Distribution of tool calls across capabilities.</span> All calls issued by each model,
normalized to 100%. Visual Grounding is the largest consumer for every model; the models differ
mainly in the second-ranked capability.</figcaption>
</figure>
<figure id="A1.F7" class="ltx_figure"><object type="image/svg+xml" data="2610.26860v1/tool_use_pies.svg" id="A1.F7.g1" class="ltx_graphics ltx_centering ltx_img_portrait" style="aspect-ratio:505/637;" width="505" height="637"></object>
<figcaption class="ltx_caption ltx_centering"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_figure">Figure 7: </span><span id="A1.F7.3" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">Distribution of tool calls by domain.</span> Calls issued in each domain as a share of
that domain’s calls, with the call total below each panel. Colors are consistent across panels.
Grounded perception is dominated by Visual Grounding for every model except GPT-6 Sol and GPT-6 Astra; visual
search and deep research is the only domain dominated by the web capabilities; spatial reasoning is the only domain in which four
capabilities carry comparable load.</figcaption>
</figure>
<section id="A1.SS2.SSS0.Px1" class="ltx_paragraph">
<h4 class="ltx_title ltx_title_paragraph">Aggregate distribution.</h4>

<div id="A1.SS2.SSS0.Px1.p1" class="ltx_para ltx_noindent">
<p id="A1.SS2.SSS0.Px1.p1.1" class="ltx_p">Visual Grounding accounts for the largest share of calls in all five
models, ranging from <math id="A1.SS2.SSS0.Px1.p1.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="45\%" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mn>45</mn><mo>%</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">45\%</annotation></semantics></math> for GPT-6 Sol to <math id="A1.SS2.SSS0.Px1.p1.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="63\%" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mn>63</mn><mo>%</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">63\%</annotation></semantics></math> for Qwen3-VL-32B. The models differ mainly in the
second-ranked capability. GPT-6 Sol and GPT-6 Astra allocate <math id="A1.SS2.SSS0.Px1.p1.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="20\%" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mn>20</mn><mo>%</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">20\%</annotation></semantics></math> and <math id="A1.SS2.SSS0.Px1.p1.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="18\%" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mn>18</mn><mo>%</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">18\%</annotation></semantics></math> of their calls to Zoom-in, against <math id="A1.SS2.SSS0.Px1.p1.m5" class="ltx_Math" alttext="5" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mn>5</mn><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">5</annotation></semantics></math>–<math id="A1.SS2.SSS0.Px1.p1.m6" class="ltx_Math" alttext="9\%" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mn>9</mn><mo>%</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">9\%</annotation></semantics></math> for the
other models, corresponding to a ground-then-inspect pattern. The GPT-6 models allocate a larger share to
web retrieval (<math id="A1.SS2.SSS0.Px1.p1.m7" class="ltx_Math" alttext="17\%" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mn>17</mn><mo>%</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">17\%</annotation></semantics></math> to Text Search for Luna, <math id="A1.SS2.SSS0.Px1.p1.m8" class="ltx_Math" alttext="13\%" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mn>13</mn><mo>%</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">13\%</annotation></semantics></math> for Sol, and <math id="A1.SS2.SSS0.Px1.p1.m9" class="ltx_Math" alttext="9\%" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mn>9</mn><mo>%</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">9\%</annotation></semantics></math> for Astra), concentrated in the visual search and deep research domain. Depth Estimation, Camera
Trajectory and the Python Coding Agent are small in aggregate and concentrated in the spatial reasoning datasets.</p>
</div>
</section>
<section id="A1.SS2.SSS0.Px2" class="ltx_paragraph">
<h4 class="ltx_title ltx_title_paragraph">Distribution by domain.</h4>

<div id="A1.SS2.SSS0.Px2.p1" class="ltx_para ltx_noindent">
<p id="A1.SS2.SSS0.Px2.p1.1" class="ltx_p">Figure <a href="#A1.F7" title="Figure 7 ‣ A.2 Distribution of Tool Calls ‣ Appendix A Tool-Use Behavior ‣ OpenVLHarness: Real World Visual Agentic Tool-Use with Multimodal Memory" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">7</span></a> resolves the same budget by
domain. Grounded perception is dominated by Visual Grounding for every model, at <math id="A1.SS2.SSS0.Px2.p1.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="90" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mn>90</mn><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">90</annotation></semantics></math>–<math id="A1.SS2.SSS0.Px2.p1.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="94\%" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mn>94</mn><mo>%</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">94\%</annotation></semantics></math> of
domain calls, with the exception of GPT-6 Sol and GPT-6 Astra, which allocate roughly a quarter of their calls in that
domain to Zoom-in. Visual search and deep research is the only domain in which the three web capabilities dominate. Spatial
reasoning is the one domain in which four capabilities carry comparable load, combining grounding, camera
trajectory, depth and the coding agent, and general VQA and hallucination divides between grounding and inspection.</p>
</div>
</section>
</section>
