<div id="A2.SS3.p1" class="ltx_para ltx_noindent">
<p id="A2.SS3.p1.1" class="ltx_p">Table <a href="#A2.T9" title="Table 9 ‣ B.3 Training-Based Baselines ‣ Appendix B Additional Results and Analysis ‣ OpenVLHarness: Real World Visual Agentic Tool-Use with Multimodal Memory" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">9</span></a> compares OpenVLHarness with the three training-based harnesses on Qwen2.5-VL-7B-Instruct, the base model all three are fine-tuned from (setup in Appendix <a href="#A7.SS2" title="G.2 Training-Based Harnesses ‣ Appendix G Baseline Reproduction ‣ OpenVLHarness: Real World Visual Agentic Tool-Use with Multimodal Memory" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">G.2</span></a>). Averaged over the <math id="A2.SS3.p1.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="22" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mn>22</mn><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">22</annotation></semantics></math> datasets, OpenVLHarness reaches <math id="A2.SS3.p1.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="33.45" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mn>33.45</mn><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">33.45</annotation></semantics></math>, ahead of PixelReasoner (<math id="A2.SS3.p1.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="28.02" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mn>28.02</mn><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">28.02</annotation></semantics></math>), AdaReasoner (<math id="A2.SS3.p1.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="25.29" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mn>25.29</mn><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">25.29</annotation></semantics></math>), and DeepEyesV2 (<math id="A2.SS3.p1.m5" class="ltx_Math" alttext="25.02" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mn>25.02</mn><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">25.02</annotation></semantics></math>), without updating any weights.</p>
</div>
<section id="A2.SS3.SSS0.Px1" class="ltx_paragraph">
<h4 class="ltx_title ltx_title_paragraph">Where training helps and where it does not.</h4>

<div id="A2.SS3.SSS0.Px1.p1" class="ltx_para ltx_noindent">
<p id="A2.SS3.SSS0.Px1.p1.1" class="ltx_p">The margin is largest on grounded perception (<math id="A2.SS3.SSS0.Px1.p1.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="23.05" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mn>23.05</mn><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">23.05</annotation></semantics></math> vs. at most <math id="A2.SS3.SSS0.Px1.p1.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="10.41" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mn>10.41</mn><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">10.41</annotation></semantics></math>) and on visual search and deep research (<math id="A2.SS3.SSS0.Px1.p1.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="19.91" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mn>19.91</mn><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">19.91</annotation></semantics></math> vs. at most <math id="A2.SS3.SSS0.Px1.p1.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="15.59" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mn>15.59</mn><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">15.59</annotation></semantics></math>). The training-based harnesses are optimized for a fixed tool set and answer style: they rarely produce the dense, well-formed box lists that FSCD and OdinW require, and none of them retrieves web evidence in our setup, so knowledge-intensive search questions fall back on the 7B model’s parametric knowledge. On general VQA and hallucination and on spatial reasoning, which resemble their training distributions, the gap narrows (<math id="A2.SS3.SSS0.Px1.p1.m5" class="ltx_Math" alttext="50.58" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mn>50.58</mn><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">50.58</annotation></semantics></math> vs. <math id="A2.SS3.SSS0.Px1.p1.m6" class="ltx_Math" alttext="49.32" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mn>49.32</mn><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">49.32</annotation></semantics></math> and <math id="A2.SS3.SSS0.Px1.p1.m7" class="ltx_Math" alttext="39.54" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mn>39.54</mn><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">39.54</annotation></semantics></math> vs. <math id="A2.SS3.SSS0.Px1.p1.m8" class="ltx_Math" alttext="39.37" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mn>39.37</mn><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">39.37</annotation></semantics></math>), and the trained models are best on a few perception-centric datasets such as RealWorldQA and V*, where PixelReasoner’s learned zoom-in behavior pays off. Training a model into one tool protocol thus helps in-distribution but transfers poorly, whereas a training-free harness can cover all four domains with the same weights.</p>
</div>
</section>
<section id="A2.SS3.SSS0.Px2" class="ltx_paragraph">
<h4 class="ltx_title ltx_title_paragraph">The backbone as orchestrator.</h4>

<div id="A2.SS3.SSS0.Px2.p1" class="ltx_para ltx_noindent">
<p id="A2.SS3.SSS0.Px2.p1.1" class="ltx_p">Absolute scores with OpenVLHarness on Qwen2.5-VL-7B are well below those on Qwen3-VL (Table <a href="#S3.T1" title="Table 1 ‣ Tasks. ‣ 3.1 Tasks, Models, and Baselines ‣ 3 Experiments ‣ OpenVLHarness: Real World Visual Agentic Tool-Use with Multimodal Memory" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">1</span></a>). Because the harness relies on the backbone to plan tool calls and to follow output conventions, a weaker orchestrator limits its benefit; on OdinW-13-All, for example, OpenVLHarness reaches only <math id="A2.SS3.SSS0.Px2.p1.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="7.80" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mn>7.80</mn><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">7.80</annotation></semantics></math> AP with Qwen2.5-VL-7B versus <math id="A2.SS3.SSS0.Px2.p1.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="50.40" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mn>50.40</mn><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">50.40</annotation></semantics></math> with Qwen3-VL-8B. The harness therefore benefits directly from stronger backbones, with no retraining when a new backbone is adopted.</p>
</div>
</section>
</section>
<section id="A2.SS4" class="ltx_subsection">
<h3 class="ltx_title ltx_title_subsection"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_subsection">B.4 </span>Grounded Perception Across Object Counts</h3>

<div id="A2.SS4.p1" class="ltx_para ltx_noindent">
<p id="A2.SS4.p1.1" class="ltx_p">Most counting and grounding benchmarks rarely test models at high object counts, which can make headline scores misleading. To make this concrete, we bin every sample of widely used counting and grounding datasets by its number of target objects (Figures <a href="#A2.F9" title="Figure 9 ‣ B.4 Grounded Perception Across Object Counts ‣ Appendix B Additional Results and Analysis ‣ OpenVLHarness: Real World Visual Agentic Tool-Use with Multimodal Memory" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">9</span></a> and <a href="#A2.F10" title="Figure 10 ‣ B.4 Grounded Perception Across Object Counts ‣ Appendix B Additional Results and Analysis ‣ OpenVLHarness: Real World Visual Agentic Tool-Use with Multimodal Memory" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">10</span></a>). For counting, TallyQA <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_citep">(<a href="#bib.bib101" title="" class="ltx_ref">Acharya et al., 2019</a>)</cite>, CountBenchQA <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_citep">(<a href="#bib.bib102" title="" class="ltx_ref">Paiss et al., 2023</a>)</cite>, and PixmoCount place essentially all samples at ten or fewer objects; only FSC-147 and FSCD-LVIS contribute meaningful mass above <math id="A2.SS4.p1.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="50" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mn>50</mn><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">50</annotation></semantics></math> objects. Grounding is even more skewed: referring-expression datasets such as RefCOCO/+/g <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_citep">(<a href="#bib.bib43" title="" class="ltx_ref">Kazemzadeh et al., 2014</a>; <a href="#bib.bib104" title="" class="ltx_ref">Yu et al., 2016</a>; <a href="#bib.bib105" title="" class="ltx_ref">Mao et al., 2016</a>)</cite>, Ref-L4 <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_citep">(<a href="#bib.bib106" title="" class="ltx_ref">Chen et al., 2024a</a>)</cite>, and GroundingME <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_citep">(<a href="#bib.bib42" title="" class="ltx_ref">Li et al., 2025a</a>)</cite> ground exactly one target per sample, and detection-style benchmarks such as OdinW-13 and LVIS are dominated by a single object per image (<math id="A2.SS4.p1.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="97\%" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mn>97</mn><mo>%</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">97\%</annotation></semantics></math> and <math id="A2.SS4.p1.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="86\%" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mn>86</mn><mo>%</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">86\%</annotation></semantics></math> of samples in the <math id="A2.SS4.p1.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="0" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mn>0</mn><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">0</annotation></semantics></math>–<math id="A2.SS4.p1.m5" class="ltx_Math" alttext="1" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mn>1</mn><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">1</annotation></semantics></math> bucket). Dense, many-object grounding appears only in the FSCD variants. This is why our suite includes FSC-147, FSCD-147, and FSCD-LVIS, and why we break their results down by object count below.</p>
</div>
<figure id="A2.F9" class="ltx_figure"><object type="image/svg+xml" data="2610.26860v1/binned_stacked__counting.svg" id="A2.F9.g1" class="ltx_graphics ltx_centering ltx_img_landscape" style="aspect-ratio:521/331;" width="521" height="331"></object>
<figcaption class="ltx_caption ltx_centering"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_figure">Figure 9: </span><span id="A2.F9.3" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">Object-count distribution across counting benchmarks.</span>
Each bar shows the percentage of samples falling into binned object-count
buckets, with the per-dataset maximum count annotated above. Widely used
benchmarks such as TallyQA, CountBenchQA, and PixmoCount are dominated by
low counts: nearly all of their samples contain ten or fewer objects (and a
majority of TallyQA fall in the <math id="A2.F9.m6" class="ltx_Math" alttext="0" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mn>0</mn><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">0</annotation></semantics></math>–<math id="A2.F9.m7" class="ltx_Math" alttext="1" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mn>1</mn><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">1</annotation></semantics></math> bucket), capping at <math id="A2.F9.m8" class="ltx_Math" alttext="10" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mn>10</mn><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">10</annotation></semantics></math>–<math id="A2.F9.m9" class="ltx_Math" alttext="15" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mn>15</mn><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">15</annotation></semantics></math>. Only
FSC-147 and FSCD-LVIS contain substantial mass in the high-count regime
(<math id="A2.F9.m10" class="ltx_Math" alttext="&gt;50" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mphantom></mphantom><mo>&gt;</mo><mn>50</mn></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">&gt;50</annotation></semantics></math> objects), reaching into the hundreds or thousands. This skew means
accuracy on standard counting benchmarks largely reflects performance on
small object counts and reveals little about high-count visual measurement.</figcaption>
</figure>
<figure id="A2.F10" class="ltx_figure"><object type="image/svg+xml" data="2610.26860v1/binned_stacked__grounding.svg" id="A2.F10.g1" class="ltx_graphics ltx_centering ltx_img_landscape" style="aspect-ratio:521/331;" width="521" height="331"></object>
<figcaption class="ltx_caption ltx_centering"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_figure">Figure 10: </span><span id="A2.F10.3" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">Object-count distribution across grounding benchmarks.</span>
Each bar shows the percentage of samples falling into binned object-count
buckets, with the per-dataset maximum count annotated above. Referring-expression benchmarks such as RefCOCO/+/g and Ref-L4/GroundingME ground a
single target per sample (<math id="A2.F10.m7" class="ltx_Math" alttext="100\%" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mn>100</mn><mo>%</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">100\%</annotation></semantics></math> in the <math id="A2.F10.m8" class="ltx_Math" alttext="0" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mn>0</mn><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">0</annotation></semantics></math>–<math id="A2.F10.m9" class="ltx_Math" alttext="1" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mn>1</mn><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">1</annotation></semantics></math> bucket, <math id="A2.F10.m10" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\text{max}=1" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mtext>max</mtext><mo>=</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\text{max}=1</annotation></semantics></math>),
and even detection-style sets like OdinW-13 (<math id="A2.F10.m11" class="ltx_Math" alttext="97\%" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mn>97</mn><mo>%</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">97\%</annotation></semantics></math>) and LVIS (<math id="A2.F10.m12" class="ltx_Math" alttext="86\%" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mn>86</mn><mo>%</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">86\%</annotation></semantics></math>) are
overwhelmingly concentrated at one object per image. Only the FSCD variants
exercise dense, many-object grounding. As with counting, this distribution
implies that progress on common grounding benchmarks is measured almost
entirely in the single- or few-object regime.</figcaption>
</figure>
<section id="A2.SS4.SSS0.Px1" class="ltx_paragraph">
<h4 class="ltx_title ltx_title_paragraph">Results by object count.</h4>

<div id="A2.SS4.SSS0.Px1.p1" class="ltx_para ltx_noindent">
<p id="A2.SS4.SSS0.Px1.p1.1" class="ltx_p">Table <a href="#A2.T10" title="Table 10 ‣ Results by object count. ‣ B.4 Grounded Perception Across Object Counts ‣ Appendix B Additional Results and Analysis ‣ OpenVLHarness: Real World Visual Agentic Tool-Use with Multimodal Memory" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">10</span></a> breaks down FSC-147 and FSCD-LVIS by the number of ground-truth objects, using the bins defined above. On FSC-147, OpenVLHarness improves exact-match accuracy in every bucket, with the largest gains between <math id="A2.SS4.SSS0.Px1.p1.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="11" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mn>11</mn><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">11</annotation></semantics></math> and <math id="A2.SS4.SSS0.Px1.p1.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="50" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mn>50</mn><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">50</annotation></semantics></math> objects (e.g., <math id="A2.SS4.SSS0.Px1.p1.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="5.0\to 32.9" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mn>5.0</mn><mo stretchy="false">→</mo><mn>32.9</mn></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">5.0\to 32.9</annotation></semantics></math> at 8B for <math id="A2.SS4.SSS0.Px1.p1.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="21" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mn>21</mn><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">21</annotation></semantics></math>–<math id="A2.SS4.SSS0.Px1.p1.m5" class="ltx_Math" alttext="50" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mn>50</mn><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">50</annotation></semantics></math>), where the base models’ accuracy collapses. On FSCD-LVIS, where every image has at least <math id="A2.SS4.SSS0.Px1.p1.m6" class="ltx_Math" alttext="21" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mn>21</mn><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">21</annotation></semantics></math> instances, the base models recover almost none of the instances (F1 <math id="A2.SS4.SSS0.Px1.p1.m7" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\leq 12.5" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mphantom></mphantom><mo>≤</mo><mn>12.5</mn></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\leq 12.5</annotation></semantics></math>), whereas OpenVLHarness reaches F1 above <math id="A2.SS4.SSS0.Px1.p1.m8" class="ltx_Math" alttext="56" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mn>56</mn><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">56</annotation></semantics></math> up to <math id="A2.SS4.SSS0.Px1.p1.m9" class="ltx_Math" alttext="100" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mn>100</mn><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">100</annotation></semantics></math> instances and around <math id="A2.SS4.SSS0.Px1.p1.m10" class="ltx_Math" alttext="41" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mn>41</mn><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">41</annotation></semantics></math> beyond. Accuracy nevertheless falls steeply with count for every configuration: above <math id="A2.SS4.SSS0.Px1.p1.m11" class="ltx_Math" alttext="100" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mn>100</mn><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">100</annotation></semantics></math> objects exact counting remains essentially unsolved, confirming that the high-count regime is where the most headroom lies.</p>
</div>
