<h3 class="ltx_title ltx_title_subsection"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_subsection">B.6 </span>Reasoning-Effort Study: Setup and Full Results</h3>

<div id="A2.SS6.p1" class="ltx_para ltx_noindent">
<p id="A2.SS6.p1.1" class="ltx_p">OpenVLHarness answers a question over several model calls, and each call carries tool outputs and memory, so it spends more model tokens per question than prompting the base model directly. This raises the question of whether the harness helps simply by spending more compute: a reasoning model given a larger token budget might recover the gain by reasoning for longer. GPT-6 Luna exposes a reasoning-effort setting, which lets us test this directly by raising the effort of every method, including OpenVLHarness. Section <a href="#S3.SS3" title="3.3 Can More Reasoning Replace the Harness? ‣ 3 Experiments ‣ OpenVLHarness: Real World Visual Agentic Tool-Use with Multimodal Memory" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">3.3</span></a> and Figure <a href="#S0.F1" title="Figure 1 ‣ OpenVLHarness: Real World Visual Agentic Tool-Use with Multimodal Memory" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">1</span></a>c summarize the results. Table <a href="#A2.T13" title="Table 13 ‣ Setup. ‣ B.6 Reasoning-Effort Study: Setup and Full Results ‣ Appendix B Additional Results and Analysis ‣ OpenVLHarness: Real World Visual Agentic Tool-Use with Multimodal Memory" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">13</span></a> reports domain averages and costs, Table <a href="#A2.T14" title="Table 14 ‣ Token usage. ‣ B.6 Reasoning-Effort Study: Setup and Full Results ‣ Appendix B Additional Results and Analysis ‣ OpenVLHarness: Real World Visual Agentic Tool-Use with Multimodal Memory" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">14</span></a> the tokens per question behind the costs, Table <a href="#A2.T15" title="Table 15 ‣ Code execution in Codex. ‣ B.6 Reasoning-Effort Study: Setup and Full Results ‣ Appendix B Additional Results and Analysis ‣ OpenVLHarness: Real World Visual Agentic Tool-Use with Multimodal Memory" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">15</span></a> how often Codex executes code, and Table <a href="#A2.T16" title="Table 16 ‣ Code execution in Codex. ‣ B.6 Reasoning-Effort Study: Setup and Full Results ‣ Appendix B Additional Results and Analysis ‣ OpenVLHarness: Real World Visual Agentic Tool-Use with Multimodal Memory" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">16</span></a> the per-benchmark scores.</p>
</div>
<section id="A2.SS6.SSS0.Px1" class="ltx_paragraph">
<h4 class="ltx_title ltx_title_paragraph">Setup.</h4>

<div id="A2.SS6.SSS0.Px1.p1" class="ltx_para ltx_noindent">
<p id="A2.SS6.SSS0.Px1.p1.1" class="ltx_p">We run Base, Codex, Visual Sketchpad, and OpenVLHarness with GPT-6 Luna at five reasoning-effort settings (low, medium, high, xhigh, and max) on the full evaluation splits of all <math id="A2.SS6.SSS0.Px1.p1.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="22" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mn>22</mn><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">22</annotation></semantics></math> benchmarks of Table <a href="#S3.T1" title="Table 1 ‣ Tasks. ‣ 3.1 Tasks, Models, and Baselines ‣ 3 Experiments ‣ OpenVLHarness: Real World Visual Agentic Tool-Use with Multimodal Memory" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">1</span></a>, that is, <math id="A2.SS6.SSS0.Px1.p1.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="26{,}229" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mn>26,229</mn><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">26{,}229</annotation></semantics></math> questions per setting and <math id="A2.SS6.SSS0.Px1.p1.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="524{,}580" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mn>524,580</mn><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">524{,}580</annotation></semantics></math> in total. Each method keeps its configuration from Table <a href="#S3.T1" title="Table 1 ‣ Tasks. ‣ 3.1 Tasks, Models, and Baselines ‣ 3 Experiments ‣ OpenVLHarness: Real World Visual Agentic Tool-Use with Multimodal Memory" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">1</span></a> and Appendix <a href="#A7.SS1" title="G.1 Training-Free Harnesses ‣ Appendix G Baseline Reproduction ‣ OpenVLHarness: Real World Visual Agentic Tool-Use with Multimodal Memory" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">G.1</span></a>, and the medium-effort setting is the configuration reported in Table <a href="#S3.T1" title="Table 1 ‣ Tasks. ‣ 3.1 Tasks, Models, and Baselines ‣ 3 Experiments ‣ OpenVLHarness: Real World Visual Agentic Tool-Use with Multimodal Memory" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">1</span></a>. Apart from the effort, only two settings change. First, Base, Visual Sketchpad, and OpenVLHarness may produce up to <math id="A2.SS6.SSS0.Px1.p1.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="16{,}000" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mn>16,000</mn><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">16{,}000</annotation></semantics></math> output tokens per model call, raised to <math id="A2.SS6.SSS0.Px1.p1.m5" class="ltx_Math" alttext="64{,}000" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mn>64,000</mn><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">64{,}000</annotation></semantics></math> at max effort so that reasoning is not truncated; Codex uses its own default limit. Second, GPT-6 Luna accepts max effort only through the Responses API, so Base switches to that API at max effort; OpenVLHarness and Codex use it at every setting, and the Chat Completions requests of Visual Sketchpad are translated to it at every setting. The Python Coding Agent of OpenVLHarness uses the same reasoning effort as the orchestrator. Codex is the Codex CLI agent <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_citep">(<a href="#bib.bib116" title="" class="ltx_ref">OpenAI, 2025a</a>, see Appendix <a href="#A7.SS1" title="G.1 Training-Free Harnesses ‣ Appendix G Baseline Reproduction ‣ OpenVLHarness: Real World Visual Agentic Tool-Use with Multimodal Memory" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">G.1</span></a>)</cite>: every question runs in a fresh session with the image attached and web search disabled to prevent the model from searching the web for the benchmark’s gold answer, and model-written code executes in a read-only sandbox enforced with Landlock and seccomp. All settings were run on the Flex service tier.</p>
</div>
<div id="A2.SS6.SSS0.Px1.p2" class="ltx_para ltx_noindent">
<p id="A2.SS6.SSS0.Px1.p2.1" class="ltx_p">Cost is the GPT-6 Luna API bill for model tokens: uncached input, cache reads and writes, and output, including hidden reasoning tokens. We price all settings at Standard-tier rates; the Flex tier halves every price and leaves cost ratios unchanged. Costs exclude tool execution (e.g., SAM 3, OCR, and search APIs) and judging, and are averaged over the <math id="A2.SS6.SSS0.Px1.p2.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="22" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mn>22</mn><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">22</annotation></semantics></math> benchmarks in the same way as the scores. Scoring follows Table <a href="#S3.T1" title="Table 1 ‣ Tasks. ‣ 3.1 Tasks, Models, and Baselines ‣ 3 Experiments ‣ OpenVLHarness: Real World Visual Agentic Tool-Use with Multimodal Memory" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">1</span></a>; on the search benchmarks, every setting is scored with the GPT-4o judge used in that table. A question whose requests fail on every retry counts as incorrect; this occurred for <math id="A2.SS6.SSS0.Px1.p2.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="134" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mn>134</mn><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">134</annotation></semantics></math> of the <math id="A2.SS6.SSS0.Px1.p2.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="524{,}580" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mn>524,580</mn><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">524{,}580</annotation></semantics></math> questions (marked <sup id="A2.SS6.SSS0.Px1.p2.1.1" class="ltx_sup">†</sup> in Table <a href="#A2.T16" title="Table 16 ‣ Code execution in Codex. ‣ B.6 Reasoning-Effort Study: Setup and Full Results ‣ Appendix B Additional Results and Analysis ‣ OpenVLHarness: Real World Visual Agentic Tool-Use with Multimodal Memory" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">16</span></a>).</p>
</div>
