<section id="A2.SS7" class="ltx_subsection">
<h3 class="ltx_title ltx_title_subsection"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_subsection">B.7 </span>Codex with Open-Source Backbones</h3>

<div id="A2.SS7.p1" class="ltx_para ltx_noindent">
<p id="A2.SS7.p1.1" class="ltx_p">Table <a href="#S3.T1" title="Table 1 ‣ Tasks. ‣ 3.1 Tasks, Models, and Baselines ‣ 3 Experiments ‣ OpenVLHarness: Real World Visual Agentic Tool-Use with Multimodal Memory" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">1</span></a> compares OpenVLHarness with Codex only for GPT-6 Luna. To test whether that comparison depends on the backbone, we run Codex with Qwen3-VL-8B and Qwen3-VL-32B on all <math id="A2.SS7.p1.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="22" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mn>22</mn><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">22</annotation></semantics></math> benchmarks.</p>
</div>
<section id="A2.SS7.SSS0.Px1" class="ltx_paragraph">
<h4 class="ltx_title ltx_title_paragraph">Setup.</h4>

<div id="A2.SS7.SSS0.Px1.p1" class="ltx_para ltx_noindent">
<p id="A2.SS7.SSS0.Px1.p1.1" class="ltx_p">Codex is the Codex CLI agent <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_citep">(<a href="#bib.bib116" title="" class="ltx_ref">OpenAI, 2025a</a>)</cite> in code mode, configured as for GPT-6 Luna (Appendix <a href="#A7.SS1" title="G.1 Training-Free Harnesses ‣ Appendix G Baseline Reproduction ‣ OpenVLHarness: Real World Visual Agentic Tool-Use with Multimodal Memory" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">G.1</span></a>) except that it also appends a short answer-format instruction to each question. Every question runs in a fresh session with the image attached, web search disabled, and medium reasoning effort; the Qwen3-VL Instruct models have no separate reasoning mode, so the effort setting does not change their decoding. Both models are served with vLLM, and each response is capped at <math id="A2.SS7.SSS0.Px1.p1.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="2{,}048" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mn>2,048</mn><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">2{,}048</annotation></semantics></math> tokens, the per-turn limit used for OpenVLHarness. Codex runs model-written code in a read-only sandbox; in these runs it used its default sandbox, which cannot start shell processes on our evaluation host, so Codex could evaluate only simple expressions. These results therefore measure the Codex agent loop and prompt without shell-based tools. The grounded perception benchmarks use the validation splits, as in Table <a href="#S3.T1" title="Table 1 ‣ Tasks. ‣ 3.1 Tasks, Models, and Baselines ‣ 3 Experiments ‣ OpenVLHarness: Real World Visual Agentic Tool-Use with Multimodal Memory" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">1</span></a>. FSC-147 is scored by the last integer in each answer, and an empty answer counts as wrong.</p>
