<section id="A3.SS2" class="ltx_subsection">
<h3 class="ltx_title ltx_title_subsection"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_subsection">C.2 </span>Perception Tools</h3>

<section id="A3.SS2.SSS0.Px1" class="ltx_paragraph">
<h4 class="ltx_title ltx_title_paragraph">Zoom-in.</h4>

<div id="A3.SS2.SSS0.Px1.p1" class="ltx_para ltx_noindent">
<p id="A3.SS2.SSS0.Px1.p1.1" class="ltx_p">Crop a region of an image given a normalized bounding box on the <math id="A3.SS2.SSS0.Px1.p1.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="[0,1000]" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mo stretchy="false">[</mo><mn>0</mn><mo>,</mo><mn>1000</mn><mo stretchy="false">]</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">[0,1000]</annotation></semantics></math> scale plus padding, and return the cropped image. Used for closer visual inspection after the agent has already localized a region of interest.</p>
</div>
</section>
<section id="A3.SS2.SSS0.Px2" class="ltx_paragraph">
<h4 class="ltx_title ltx_title_paragraph">Visual Grounding.</h4>

<div id="A3.SS2.SSS0.Px2.p1" class="ltx_para ltx_noindent">
<p id="A3.SS2.SSS0.Px2.p1.1" class="ltx_p">Locate and highlight regions of an image that match a text query, backed by SAM 3 <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_citep">(<a href="#bib.bib59" title="" class="ltx_ref">Carion et al., 2025</a>)</cite> by default or by Grounding DINO <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_citep">(<a href="#bib.bib93" title="" class="ltx_ref">Liu et al., 2024</a>)</cite>. It returns an annotated image with matched regions highlighted and numbered, along with bounding boxes (and segmentation masks when the backend provides them).</p>
</div>
</section>
<section id="A3.SS2.SSS0.Px3" class="ltx_paragraph">
<h4 class="ltx_title ltx_title_paragraph">OCR.</h4>

<div id="A3.SS2.SSS0.Px3.p1" class="ltx_para ltx_noindent">
<p id="A3.SS2.SSS0.Px3.p1.1" class="ltx_p">Extract readable text from an image, backed by PaddleOCR-VL <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_citep">(<a href="#bib.bib60" title="" class="ltx_ref">PaddlePaddle Team, 2025</a>)</cite> by default or by DeepSeek-OCR <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_citep">(<a href="#bib.bib94" title="" class="ltx_ref">Wei et al., 2025a</a>)</cite>. Returns the transcribed text as a single string. Used when the task is about words, numbers, or printed labels in the image; not for general object localization or segmentation.</p>
</div>
</section>
<section id="A3.SS2.SSS0.Px4" class="ltx_paragraph">
<h4 class="ltx_title ltx_title_paragraph">Depth Estimation.</h4>

<div id="A3.SS2.SSS0.Px4.p1" class="ltx_para ltx_noindent">
<p id="A3.SS2.SSS0.Px4.p1.1" class="ltx_p">Estimate a per-pixel depth map for an input image, backed by Depth Anything 3 <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_citep">(<a href="#bib.bib62" title="" class="ltx_ref">Lin et al., 2025</a>)</cite> or VGGT <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_citep">(<a href="#bib.bib95" title="" class="ltx_ref">Wang et al., 2025b</a>)</cite>. Returns a depth image (closer surfaces brighter, farther darker). Used when the task requires reasoning about relative distance, foreground–background separation, or 3D scene structure cues that flat 2D crops cannot resolve.</p>
</div>
</section>
<section id="A3.SS2.SSS0.Px5" class="ltx_paragraph">
<h4 class="ltx_title ltx_title_paragraph">Camera Trajectory.</h4>

<div id="A3.SS2.SSS0.Px5.p1" class="ltx_para ltx_noindent">
<p id="A3.SS2.SSS0.Px5.p1.1" class="ltx_p">Given an ordered sequence of at least two images of the same scene, estimate each camera’s pose relative to the first image using Depth Anything 3 <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_citep">(<a href="#bib.bib62" title="" class="ltx_ref">Lin et al., 2025</a>)</cite> or VGGT <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_citep">(<a href="#bib.bib95" title="" class="ltx_ref">Wang et al., 2025b</a>)</cite>, and translate the result into a form the model can reason over directly. Rather than returning raw geometry alone, the tool reports, for every image, a plain-language summary of translation and rotation with respect to the reference frame (e.g. “salient direction: backward (-5.43); detailed: left (-0.20), down (+0.28), backward (-5.43)”), the accompanying scalars (camera-center offset, forward direction, yaw, pitch), an explicit statement of the coordinate convention (<math id="A3.SS2.SSS0.Px5.p1.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="+X" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mo>+</mo><mi>X</mi></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">+X</annotation></semantics></math> right, <math id="A3.SS2.SSS0.Px5.p1.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="+Y" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mo>+</mo><mi>Y</mi></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">+Y</annotation></semantics></math> down, <math id="A3.SS2.SSS0.Px5.p1.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="+Z" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mo>+</mo><mi>Z</mi></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">+Z</annotation></semantics></math> forward, with positive yaw turning right), and a bird’s-eye-view rendering of the camera positions on the reference frame’s <math id="A3.SS2.SSS0.Px5.p1.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="X" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>X</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">X</annotation></semantics></math>–<math id="A3.SS2.SSS0.Px5.p1.m5" class="ltx_Math" alttext="Z" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>Z</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">Z</annotation></semantics></math> plane. Per-image intrinsics and world-to-camera extrinsics are returned as well for downstream computation. Translations below one meter and rotations below ten degrees are reported as non-salient. Used when a question depends on how the viewpoint moved or rotated across frames, or on multi-view scene structure that no single image conveys; the estimate is metric only up to the model’s calibration and requires sufficient scene overlap between the reference image and the other views.</p>
</div>
</section>
</section>
<section id="A3.SS3" class="ltx_subsection">
<h3 class="ltx_title ltx_title_subsection"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_subsection">C.3 </span>Python Coding Agent</h3>

<section id="A3.SS3.SSS0.Px1" class="ltx_paragraph">
<h4 class="ltx_title ltx_title_paragraph">Python Coding Agent.</h4>

<div id="A3.SS3.SSS0.Px1.p1" class="ltx_para ltx_noindent">
<p id="A3.SS3.SSS0.Px1.p1.1" class="ltx_p">Run Python over the agent’s local computation state and the outputs of previously called tools (bounding boxes, OCR strings, segmentation masks, and so on), returning both standard output and the resulting local variables. The agent describes a computation goal in plain language, and by default a single-shot code-generation session of the same backbone model as the orchestrator writes the snippet using prior tool outputs as named variables before it is executed; the same interface can instead be backed by a full coding agent that inspects and revises its own code before returning. Used for aggregating numbers extracted by OCR, filtering detected regions by bounding-box area, or any cross-tool numerical reasoning the agent cannot complete in its own response.</p>
</div>
</section>
</section>
