<section id="A3.SS6.SSS0.Px1" class="ltx_paragraph">
<h4 class="ltx_title ltx_title_paragraph">Open-source backbones.</h4>

<div id="A3.SS6.SSS0.Px1.p1" class="ltx_para ltx_noindent">
<p id="A3.SS6.SSS0.Px1.p1.1" class="ltx_p">All Qwen models (Qwen2.5-VL <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_citep">(<a href="#bib.bib58" title="" class="ltx_ref">Qwen Team, 2025a</a>)</cite> and Qwen3-VL <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_citep">(<a href="#bib.bib57" title="" class="ltx_ref">Qwen Team, 2025b</a>)</cite>) use the public Hugging Face weights, served in bf16 with vLLM <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_citep">(<a href="#bib.bib110" title="" class="ltx_ref">Kwon et al., 2023</a>)</cite> behind an OpenAI-compatible endpoint with native tool calling. We sample at temperature <math id="A3.SS6.SSS0.Px1.p1.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="0.7" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mn>0.7</mn><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">0.7</annotation></semantics></math>, top-<math id="A3.SS6.SSS0.Px1.p1.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="p" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>p</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">p</annotation></semantics></math> <math id="A3.SS6.SSS0.Px1.p1.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="0.8" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mn>0.8</mn><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">0.8</annotation></semantics></math>, top-<math id="A3.SS6.SSS0.Px1.p1.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="k" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>k</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">k</annotation></semantics></math> <math id="A3.SS6.SSS0.Px1.p1.m5" class="ltx_Math" alttext="20" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mn>20</mn><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">20</annotation></semantics></math>, repetition penalty <math id="A3.SS6.SSS0.Px1.p1.m6" class="ltx_Math" alttext="1.0" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mn>1.0</mn><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">1.0</annotation></semantics></math>, and presence penalty <math id="A3.SS6.SSS0.Px1.p1.m7" class="ltx_Math" alttext="1.5" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mn>1.5</mn><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">1.5</annotation></semantics></math>, with up to <math id="A3.SS6.SSS0.Px1.p1.m8" class="ltx_Math" alttext="12{,}800" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mn>12,800</mn><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">12{,}800</annotation></semantics></math> generated tokens per turn. The agent runs for at most fifteen tool-calling iterations per query, and its context is capped at 168K tokens; when a trajectory approaches the cap, older turns are compacted while the latest Environment Update and the most recent turn are kept verbatim.</p>
</div>
</section>
<section id="A3.SS6.SSS0.Px2" class="ltx_paragraph">
<h4 class="ltx_title ltx_title_paragraph">Proprietary backbones.</h4>

<div id="A3.SS6.SSS0.Px2.p1" class="ltx_para ltx_noindent">
<p id="A3.SS6.SSS0.Px2.p1.1" class="ltx_p">We call GPT-5 <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_citep">(<a href="#bib.bib91" title="" class="ltx_ref">OpenAI, 2025b</a>)</cite>, GPT-6 Luna, GPT-6 Sol, and GPT-6 Astra <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_citep">(<a href="#bib.bib92" title="" class="ltx_ref">OpenAI, 2026</a>)</cite> through the OpenAI API with native function calling, provider-default sampling, and up to <math id="A3.SS6.SSS0.Px2.p1.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="16{,}000" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mn>16,000</mn><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">16{,}000</annotation></semantics></math> output tokens per turn. GPT-5 uses the pinned snapshot <span id="A3.SS6.SSS0.Px2.p1.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_typewriter">gpt-5-2025-08-07</span>, and the three GPT-6 models (<span id="A3.SS6.SSS0.Px2.p1.1.2" class="ltx_text ltx_font_typewriter">gpt-6-luna</span>, <span id="A3.SS6.SSS0.Px2.p1.1.3" class="ltx_text ltx_font_typewriter">gpt-6-sol</span>, and <span id="A3.SS6.SSS0.Px2.p1.1.4" class="ltx_text ltx_font_typewriter">gpt-6-astra</span>) use medium reasoning effort. All four use the same tools, multimodal memory, system prompt, and iteration cap as the open-source backbones.</p>
</div>
</section>
<section id="A3.SS6.SSS0.Px3" class="ltx_paragraph">
<h4 class="ltx_title ltx_title_paragraph">Tool backends.</h4>

<div id="A3.SS6.SSS0.Px3.p1" class="ltx_para ltx_noindent">
<p id="A3.SS6.SSS0.Px3.p1.1" class="ltx_p">Four tools call a language model internally: Text Search and Image Search use it to rerank and summarize the retrieved results, Webpage Visit uses it to extract goal-relevant content from a fetched page, and the Python Coding Agent uses it to write code for the agent’s objective. The three search-related tools use Qwen3-VL-8B-Instruct with greedy decoding, independent of the agent backbone. The Python Coding Agent instead routes to a fresh context of the same backbone model as the orchestrator, so that the code generator’s capability scales with the backbone. Every capability uses its default backend (Table <a href="#A3.T18" title="Table 18 ‣ Capabilities and backends. ‣ Appendix C Harness Implementation Details ‣ OpenVLHarness: Real World Visual Agentic Tool-Use with Multimodal Memory" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">18</span></a>). Perception tools are backed by SAM 3 <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_citep">(<a href="#bib.bib59" title="" class="ltx_ref">Carion et al., 2025</a>)</cite> (Visual Grounding), PaddleOCR-VL <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_citep">(<a href="#bib.bib60" title="" class="ltx_ref">PaddlePaddle Team, 2025</a>)</cite> (OCR), and Depth Anything 3 <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_citep">(<a href="#bib.bib62" title="" class="ltx_ref">Lin et al., 2025</a>)</cite> or VGGT <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_citep">(<a href="#bib.bib95" title="" class="ltx_ref">Wang et al., 2025b</a>)</cite> (Depth Estimation and Camera Trajectory), each served locally; search tools query the Serper API.</p>
</div>
</section>
<section id="A3.SS6.SSS0.Px4" class="ltx_paragraph">
<h4 class="ltx_title ltx_title_paragraph">Judges.</h4>

<div id="A3.SS6.SSS0.Px4.p1" class="ltx_para ltx_noindent">
<p id="A3.SS6.SSS0.Px4.p1.1" class="ltx_p">We use GPT-4o <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_citep">(<a href="#bib.bib61" title="" class="ltx_ref">OpenAI, 2024</a>)</cite> with the Vision-DeepResearch judge prompt (Appendix <a href="#A4.SS2.SSS0.Px14" title="Judge prompt. ‣ D.2 Visual Search and Deep Research ‣ Appendix D Benchmarks and Evaluation Protocol ‣ OpenVLHarness: Real World Visual Agentic Tool-Use with Multimodal Memory" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">D.2</span></a>) for the visual search and deep research benchmarks, and Qwen3-VL-8B-Instruct for the remaining LLM-judged benchmarks; both judges decode greedily. On the counting benchmarks the judge only extracts the final integer from free-form responses, and correctness is decided by exact match. Grounding benchmarks need no judge and are scored with COCO-style box AP.</p>
</div>
</section>
</section>
</section>
