<section id="A4.SS0.SSS0.Px1" class="ltx_paragraph">
<h4 class="ltx_title ltx_title_paragraph">Sampling Parameters.</h4>

<div id="A4.SS0.SSS0.Px1.p1" class="ltx_para ltx_noindent">
<p id="A4.SS0.SSS0.Px1.p1.1" class="ltx_p">All benchmarks use the same agent configuration. The model is served locally via vLLM <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_citep">(<a href="#bib.bib110" title="" class="ltx_ref">Kwon et al., 2023</a>)</cite> and sampled with temperature <math id="A4.SS0.SSS0.Px1.p1.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="0.7" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mn>0.7</mn><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">0.7</annotation></semantics></math>, top-<math id="A4.SS0.SSS0.Px1.p1.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="p" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>p</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">p</annotation></semantics></math> <math id="A4.SS0.SSS0.Px1.p1.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="0.8" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mn>0.8</mn><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">0.8</annotation></semantics></math>, top-<math id="A4.SS0.SSS0.Px1.p1.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="k" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>k</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">k</annotation></semantics></math> <math id="A4.SS0.SSS0.Px1.p1.m5" class="ltx_Math" alttext="20" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mn>20</mn><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">20</annotation></semantics></math>, repetition penalty <math id="A4.SS0.SSS0.Px1.p1.m6" class="ltx_Math" alttext="1.0" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mn>1.0</mn><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">1.0</annotation></semantics></math>, and presence penalty <math id="A4.SS0.SSS0.Px1.p1.m7" class="ltx_Math" alttext="1.5" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mn>1.5</mn><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">1.5</annotation></semantics></math>, with a cap of <math id="A4.SS0.SSS0.Px1.p1.m8" class="ltx_Math" alttext="12{,}800" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mn>12,800</mn><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">12{,}800</annotation></semantics></math> tokens per model call and a <math id="A4.SS0.SSS0.Px1.p1.m9" class="ltx_Math" alttext="168" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mn>168</mn><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">168</annotation></semantics></math>K-token context window. The agent uses native tool calls and is allowed at most <math id="A4.SS0.SSS0.Px1.p1.m10" class="ltx_Math" alttext="15" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mn>15</mn><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">15</annotation></semantics></math> tool-calling iterations per question; a trajectory that exhausts this budget, or that fails after three API retries, is scored incorrect. Web-page observations are capped at <math id="A4.SS0.SSS0.Px1.p1.m11" class="ltx_Math" alttext="20" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mn>20</mn><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">20</annotation></semantics></math> environment updates per trajectory.</p>
</div>
<div id="A4.SS0.SSS0.Px1.p2" class="ltx_para ltx_noindent">
<p id="A4.SS0.SSS0.Px1.p2.1" class="ltx_p">Because decoding is stochastic, all Qwen3-VL results are means of three runs with different random seeds; Appendix <a href="#A8" title="Appendix H Run-to-Run Variability ‣ OpenVLHarness: Real World Visual Agentic Tool-Use with Multimodal Memory" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">H</span></a> describes the protocol and reports standard deviations.</p>
</div>
</section>
<section id="A4.SS0.SSS0.Px2" class="ltx_paragraph">
<h4 class="ltx_title ltx_title_paragraph">Scoring.</h4>

<div id="A4.SS0.SSS0.Px2.p1" class="ltx_para ltx_noindent">
<p id="A4.SS0.SSS0.Px2.p1.1" class="ltx_p">Multiple-choice and yes/no answers are matched against the gold option after a rule-based parser extracts the choice from the response; when the parser fails, Qwen3-VL-8B-Instruct extracts the chosen option, without judging correctness. Open-ended answers are graded by an LLM judge: GPT-4o for visual search and deep research (Appendix <a href="#A4.SS2" title="D.2 Visual Search and Deep Research ‣ Appendix D Benchmarks and Evaluation Protocol ‣ OpenVLHarness: Real World Visual Agentic Tool-Use with Multimodal Memory" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">D.2</span></a>) and Qwen3-VL-8B-Instruct for the other domains. Grounded perception benchmarks are scored without a judge (Appendix <a href="#A4.SS1" title="D.1 Grounded Perception ‣ Appendix D Benchmarks and Evaluation Protocol ‣ OpenVLHarness: Real World Visual Agentic Tool-Use with Multimodal Memory" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">D.1</span></a>). All methods and backbones on a benchmark share the same prompt and scorer.</p>
</div>
</section>
<section id="A4.SS1" class="ltx_subsection">
<h3 class="ltx_title ltx_title_subsection"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_subsection">D.1 </span>Grounded Perception</h3>

<section id="A4.SS1.SSS0.Px1" class="ltx_paragraph">
<h4 class="ltx_title ltx_title_paragraph">FSC-147.</h4>

<div id="A4.SS1.SSS0.Px1.p1" class="ltx_para ltx_noindent">
<p id="A4.SS1.SSS0.Px1.p1.1" class="ltx_p">A class-agnostic counting benchmark <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_citep">(<a href="#bib.bib66" title="" class="ltx_ref">Ranjan et al., 2021</a>)</cite> covering 147 categories with a point annotation for every object. Scenes are dense (median 27 objects, up to 2,092). We evaluate on the 1,286-image val split and ask “How many <em id="A4.SS1.SSS0.Px1.p1.1.1" class="ltx_emph ltx_font_italic">category</em> are there in the image?”. Unlike the original few-shot protocol, we do not provide exemplar boxes, so the task is zero-shot.</p>
</div>
</section>
<section id="A4.SS1.SSS0.Px2" class="ltx_paragraph">
<h4 class="ltx_title ltx_title_paragraph">CountQA.</h4>

<div id="A4.SS1.SSS0.Px2.p1" class="ltx_para ltx_noindent">
<p id="A4.SS1.SSS0.Px2.p1.1" class="ltx_p">A benchmark <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_citep">(<a href="#bib.bib38" title="" class="ltx_ref">Tamarapalli et al., 2025</a>)</cite> of real-world counting questions designed to stress MLLMs with cluttered, occluded, and densely packed scenes. We use all 1,528 questions of the test split.</p>
</div>
</section>
<section id="A4.SS1.SSS0.Px3" class="ltx_paragraph">
<h4 class="ltx_title ltx_title_paragraph">OdinW-13-All.</h4>

<div id="A4.SS1.SSS0.Px3.p1" class="ltx_para ltx_noindent">
<p id="A4.SS1.SSS0.Px3.p1.1" class="ltx_p">Built from the thirteen detection datasets of Object Detection in the Wild (OdinW-13) <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_citep">(<a href="#bib.bib40" title="" class="ltx_ref">Li et al., 2022</a>)</cite>, spanning aerial and underwater imagery, egocentric hands, thermal images, vehicles, and everyday objects. Unlike the common protocol of querying one category at a time, each image is posed as a single query that lists every category of its source dataset (for example, all <math id="A4.SS1.SSS0.Px3.p1.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="20" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mn>20</mn><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">20</annotation></semantics></math> PascalVOC classes), hence the suffix <em id="A4.SS1.SSS0.Px3.p1.1.1" class="ltx_emph ltx_font_italic">-All</em>, and the model must return a box with a category label for every instance of every listed category. A missed or mislabeled category therefore costs AP within the same response, and our numbers are not directly comparable to results obtained with one query per category. We use the 4,938 val images and report AP averaged over the thirteen datasets.</p>
</div>
</section>
<section id="A4.SS1.SSS0.Px4" class="ltx_paragraph">
<h4 class="ltx_title ltx_title_paragraph">FSCD-147 and FSCD-LVIS.</h4>

<div id="A4.SS1.SSS0.Px4.p1" class="ltx_para ltx_noindent">
<p id="A4.SS1.SSS0.Px4.p1.1" class="ltx_p">The few-shot counting-and-detection benchmarks of <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_citet"><a href="#bib.bib39" title="" class="ltx_ref">Nguyen et al. (2022)</a></cite>, which annotate a box for every instance: FSCD-147 re-annotates FSC-147, and FSCD-LVIS draws dense scenes (21–401 instances) from LVIS <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_citep">(<a href="#bib.bib41" title="" class="ltx_ref">Gupta et al., 2019</a>)</cite>. We use the val splits (1,286 and 1,181 images), query one category per image, and again withhold the exemplar boxes.</p>
</div>
</section>
<section id="A4.SS1.SSS0.Px5" class="ltx_paragraph">
<h4 class="ltx_title ltx_title_paragraph">LVIS (federated).</h4>

<div id="A4.SS1.SSS0.Px5.p1" class="ltx_para ltx_noindent">
<p id="A4.SS1.SSS0.Px5.p1.1" class="ltx_p">In addition to the five benchmarks of Table <a href="#S3.T1" title="Table 1 ‣ Tasks. ‣ 3.1 Tasks, Models, and Baselines ‣ 3 Experiments ‣ OpenVLHarness: Real World Visual Agentic Tool-Use with Multimodal Memory" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">1</span></a>, Appendix <a href="#A2.SS4" title="B.4 Grounded Perception Across Object Counts ‣ Appendix B Additional Results and Analysis ‣ OpenVLHarness: Real World Visual Agentic Tool-Use with Multimodal Memory" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">B.4</span></a> reports grounding on the LVIS val set <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_citep">(<a href="#bib.bib41" title="" class="ltx_ref">Gupta et al., 2019</a>)</cite> (19,809 images, 1,203 categories) under its federated protocol, which queries both categories present in an image and verified-absent ones, so a model must also learn to return an empty list.</p>
</div>
</section>
<section id="A4.SS1.SSS0.Px6" class="ltx_paragraph">
<h4 class="ltx_title ltx_title_paragraph">Counting metrics.</h4>

<div id="A4.SS1.SSS0.Px6.p1" class="ltx_para ltx_noindent">
<p id="A4.SS1.SSS0.Px6.p1.1" class="ltx_p">Counting benchmarks are scored by exact-match accuracy between the predicted and ground-truth integers. A rule-based parser extracts the integer (or number word) from the response; for long free-form responses, Qwen3-VL-8B-Instruct first extracts the final answer with the prompt below, and correctness is still decided by exact match. We additionally report mean absolute error and, for CountQA, relative accuracy within <math id="A4.SS1.SSS0.Px6.p1.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="5\%" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mn>5</mn><mo>%</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">5\%</annotation></semantics></math> and <math id="A4.SS1.SSS0.Px6.p1.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="10\%" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mn>10</mn><mo>%</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">10\%</annotation></semantics></math> of the ground truth (Appendix <a href="#A2.SS4" title="B.4 Grounded Perception Across Object Counts ‣ Appendix B Additional Results and Analysis ‣ OpenVLHarness: Real World Visual Agentic Tool-Use with Multimodal Memory" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">B.4</span></a>).</p>
</div>
</section>
<section id="A4.SS1.SSS0.Px7" class="ltx_paragraph">
<h4 class="ltx_title ltx_title_paragraph">Grounding metrics.</h4>

<div id="A4.SS1.SSS0.Px7.p1" class="ltx_para ltx_noindent">
<p id="A4.SS1.SSS0.Px7.p1.1" class="ltx_p">Grounding benchmarks are scored with COCO box AP averaged over IoU thresholds <math id="A4.SS1.SSS0.Px7.p1.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="0.50" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mn>0.50</mn><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">0.50</annotation></semantics></math>:<math id="A4.SS1.SSS0.Px7.p1.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="0.95" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mn>0.95</mn><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">0.95</annotation></semantics></math>, computed with the official COCO evaluation code. Predicted boxes are parsed from the response (JSON, with a lenient fallback for lists of four numbers) and converted from the <math id="A4.SS1.SSS0.Px7.p1.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="0" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mn>0</mn><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">0</annotation></semantics></math>–<math id="A4.SS1.SSS0.Px7.p1.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="1000" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mn>1000</mn><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">1000</annotation></semantics></math> grid to pixel coordinates. For OdinW-13-All, predicted labels are matched to the category names, AP is computed per source dataset, and the thirteen values are averaged. FSCD-147, FSCD-LVIS, and LVIS are evaluated per queried category. No LLM judge is involved.</p>
</div>
</section>
<section id="A4.SS1.SSS0.Px8" class="ltx_paragraph">
<h4 class="ltx_title ltx_title_paragraph">Task prompts.</h4>

