<section id="A4.SS2" class="ltx_subsection">
<h3 class="ltx_title ltx_title_subsection"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_subsection">D.2 </span>Visual Search and Deep Research</h3>

<section id="A4.SS2.SSS0.Px1" class="ltx_paragraph">
<h4 class="ltx_title ltx_title_paragraph">FVQA.</h4>

<div id="A4.SS2.SSS0.Px1.p1" class="ltx_para ltx_noindent">
<p id="A4.SS2.SSS0.Px1.p1.1" class="ltx_p">A fact-based VQA dataset <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_citep">(<a href="#bib.bib53" title="" class="ltx_ref">Wu et al., 2025a</a>)</cite> that pairs images with questions whose answers require commonsense or world-knowledge facts not directly observable in the image. Following Vision-DeepResearch <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_citep">(<a href="#bib.bib75" title="" class="ltx_ref">Huang et al., 2026</a>)</cite>, we evaluate on the official test split of 1,800 samples; all samples are search-required.</p>
</div>
</section>
<section id="A4.SS2.SSS0.Px2" class="ltx_paragraph">
<h4 class="ltx_title ltx_title_paragraph">MMSearch.</h4>

<div id="A4.SS2.SSS0.Px2.p1" class="ltx_para ltx_noindent">
<p id="A4.SS2.SSS0.Px2.p1.1" class="ltx_p">A multimodal search benchmark <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_citep">(<a href="#bib.bib44" title="" class="ltx_ref">Jiang et al., 2024</a>)</cite> of 300 manually curated questions across 14 subfields, designed to assess large multimodal models as multimodal search engines. We evaluate on the End-to-End sub-task, which requires the agent to perform query understanding, retrieval, and answering in a single pipeline. To align with other works for fair comparison, we only use the multimodal subset, which contains 171 samples.</p>
</div>
</section>
<section id="A4.SS2.SSS0.Px3" class="ltx_paragraph">
<h4 class="ltx_title ltx_title_paragraph">MMSearch-Plus.</h4>

<div id="A4.SS2.SSS0.Px3.p1" class="ltx_para ltx_noindent">
<p id="A4.SS2.SSS0.Px3.p1.1" class="ltx_p">A harder, provenance-aware extension <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_citep">(<a href="#bib.bib45" title="" class="ltx_ref">Tao et al., 2026</a>)</cite> that emphasizes localized visual reasoning, verification under noisy or conflicting retrieval, and multi-step text–image search; each item provides 1–5 images and one or more valid answers. We use the full set of 311 samples for evaluation.</p>
</div>
</section>
<section id="A4.SS2.SSS0.Px4" class="ltx_paragraph">
<h4 class="ltx_title ltx_title_paragraph">BrowseComp-VL.</h4>

<div id="A4.SS2.SSS0.Px4.p1" class="ltx_para ltx_noindent">
<p id="A4.SS2.SSS0.Px4.p1.1" class="ltx_p">The vision–language extension of BrowseComp <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_citep">(<a href="#bib.bib46" title="" class="ltx_ref">Wei et al., 2025b</a>)</cite> introduced by WebWatcher <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_citep">(<a href="#bib.bib47" title="" class="ltx_ref">Geng et al., 2026</a>)</cite>, with cross-modal information-seeking questions spanning entertainment, humanities, technology, natural science, and other domains. We use all 399 samples for evaluation.</p>
</div>
</section>
<section id="A4.SS2.SSS0.Px5" class="ltx_paragraph">
<h4 class="ltx_title ltx_title_paragraph">HLE-VL.</h4>

<div id="A4.SS2.SSS0.Px5.p1" class="ltx_para ltx_noindent">
<p id="A4.SS2.SSS0.Px5.p1.1" class="ltx_p">The multimodal subset of Humanity’s Last Exam <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_citep">(<a href="#bib.bib51" title="" class="ltx_ref">Phan et al., 2025</a>)</cite>, drawn from frontier-knowledge questions across mathematics, the natural sciences, and the humanities. We use all 330 samples for evaluation.</p>
</div>
</section>
<section id="A4.SS2.SSS0.Px6" class="ltx_paragraph">
<h4 class="ltx_title ltx_title_paragraph">RealX-Bench.</h4>

<div id="A4.SS2.SSS0.Px6.p1" class="ltx_para ltx_noindent">
<p id="A4.SS2.SSS0.Px6.p1.1" class="ltx_p">Introduced by DeepEyesV2 <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_citep">(<a href="#bib.bib4" title="" class="ltx_ref">Hong et al., 2026a</a>)</cite>, it contains expert-verified instances that require integrating fine-grained visual perception, dynamic multi-hop web retrieval, and multi-step logical reasoning. This benchmark contains perception and search types, which makes it easy to attribute gains to visual versus search tools. We use all 194 samples for evaluation.</p>
</div>
</section>
<section id="A4.SS2.SSS0.Px7" class="ltx_paragraph">
<h4 class="ltx_title ltx_title_paragraph">Extended benchmarks.</h4>

<div id="A4.SS2.SSS0.Px7.p1" class="ltx_para ltx_noindent">
<p id="A4.SS2.SSS0.Px7.p1.1" class="ltx_p">The following five benchmarks appear only in the extended search results (Appendix <a href="#A2.SS5" title="B.5 Extended Visual Search and Deep Research Results ‣ Appendix B Additional Results and Analysis ‣ OpenVLHarness: Real World Visual Agentic Tool-Use with Multimodal Memory" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">B.5</span></a>, Table <a href="#A2.T12" title="Table 12 ‣ B.5 Extended Visual Search and Deep Research Results ‣ Appendix B Additional Results and Analysis ‣ OpenVLHarness: Real World Visual Agentic Tool-Use with Multimodal Memory" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">12</span></a>). They use the same judge and prompt as the six benchmarks above.</p>
</div>
</section>
<section id="A4.SS2.SSS0.Px8" class="ltx_paragraph">
<h4 class="ltx_title ltx_title_paragraph">MM-BrowseComp.</h4>

<div id="A4.SS2.SSS0.Px8.p1" class="ltx_para ltx_noindent">
<p id="A4.SS2.SSS0.Px8.p1.1" class="ltx_p">Hand-crafted multimodal browsing questions <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_citep">(<a href="#bib.bib48" title="" class="ltx_ref">Li et al., 2025b</a>)</cite> across several sub-tasks, where the answer-relevant content is hard to find. There are two versions of MM-BrowseComp. We use the newest version, which has 243 multimodal tasks.</p>
</div>
</section>
<section id="A4.SS2.SSS0.Px9" class="ltx_paragraph">
<h4 class="ltx_title ltx_title_paragraph">VDR-Bench.</h4>

<div id="A4.SS2.SSS0.Px9.p1" class="ltx_para ltx_noindent">
<p id="A4.SS2.SSS0.Px9.p1.1" class="ltx_p">The benchmark released alongside Vision-DeepResearch <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_citep">(<a href="#bib.bib75" title="" class="ltx_ref">Huang et al., 2026</a>)</cite>: long-horizon multimodal deep-research instances that interleave many reasoning turns, designed to evaluate sustained multi-step browsing under heavy real-world noise. We use its test-mini split of 500 samples.</p>
</div>
</section>
<section id="A4.SS2.SSS0.Px10" class="ltx_paragraph">
<h4 class="ltx_title ltx_title_paragraph">VisBrowse-Bench.</h4>

<div id="A4.SS2.SSS0.Px10.p1" class="ltx_para ltx_noindent">
<p id="A4.SS2.SSS0.Px10.p1.1" class="ltx_p">A benchmark of visual-native search instances <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_citep">(<a href="#bib.bib50" title="" class="ltx_ref">Zhang et al., 2026c</a>)</cite> that require cross-validation of multimodal evidence via interleaved text–image retrieval and joint reasoning, including cases where the answer is not present in any text snippet. We use the full 169 samples for evaluation.</p>
</div>
</section>
<section id="A4.SS2.SSS0.Px11" class="ltx_paragraph">
<h4 class="ltx_title ltx_title_paragraph">InfoSeek.</h4>

<div id="A4.SS2.SSS0.Px11.p1" class="ltx_para ltx_noindent">
<p id="A4.SS2.SSS0.Px11.p1.1" class="ltx_p">A visual information-seeking benchmark <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_citep">(<a href="#bib.bib55" title="" class="ltx_ref">Chen et al., 2023</a>)</cite> of fine-grained questions about entities depicted in images (e.g., species, models, landmarks). The official test split has no public answers, so we evaluate on the 2,000-sample test subset used by MMSearch-R1 <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_citep">(<a href="#bib.bib53" title="" class="ltx_ref">Wu et al., 2025a</a>)</cite>.</p>
</div>
</section>
<section id="A4.SS2.SSS0.Px12" class="ltx_paragraph">
<h4 class="ltx_title ltx_title_paragraph">EncyclopedicVQA.</h4>

<div id="A4.SS2.SSS0.Px12.p1" class="ltx_para ltx_noindent">
<p id="A4.SS2.SSS0.Px12.p1.1" class="ltx_p">A benchmark <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_citep">(<a href="#bib.bib56" title="" class="ltx_ref">Mensink et al., 2023</a>)</cite> of questions about detailed properties of fine-grained categories. We draw a random subset of 2,000 samples from the test split. 40 of its Google Landmarks v2 images are no longer publicly available, which leaves 1,960 samples for evaluation.</p>
</div>
</section>
<section id="A4.SS2.SSS0.Px13" class="ltx_paragraph">
<h4 class="ltx_title ltx_title_paragraph">No retrieval shortcut.</h4>

<div id="A4.SS2.SSS0.Px13.p1" class="ltx_para ltx_noindent">
<p id="A4.SS2.SSS0.Px13.p1.1" class="ltx_p">Across all visual search and deep research benchmarks, we did not observe cases in which the search tools surface the gold answer verbatim from indexed pages. This reduces the likelihood that gains are driven by trivial retrieval of the gold answer.</p>
</div>
</section>
<section id="A4.SS2.SSS0.Px14" class="ltx_paragraph">
<h4 class="ltx_title ltx_title_paragraph">Judge prompt.</h4>

<div id="A4.SS2.SSS0.Px14.p1" class="ltx_para ltx_noindent">
<p id="A4.SS2.SSS0.Px14.p1.1" class="ltx_p">To align with recent multimodal deep-research work <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_citep">(<a href="#bib.bib75" title="" class="ltx_ref">Huang et al., 2026</a>)</cite>, we adopt the same LLM judge and use GPT-4o as the judge model, applying the prompt below uniformly across all search-related benchmarks.</p>
</div>
