<section id="A4.SS2.SSS0.Px15" class="ltx_paragraph">
<h4 class="ltx_title ltx_title_paragraph">Refusals on identity queries.</h4>

<div id="A4.SS2.SSS0.Px15.p1" class="ltx_para ltx_noindent">
<p id="A4.SS2.SSS0.Px15.p1.1" class="ltx_p">Some of our evaluation benchmarks contain queries that reference a real person, such as “who is the person in this photograph,” “what is the affiliation of this researcher”. Proprietary models are increasingly trained to decline such queries under their privacy and identification policies, even when the gold answer is a public-figure fact already widely indexed on the open web. We observe this for the GPT models in our setting, which return a refusal in place of an answer, which the judge counts as incorrect. Therefore, in some of the benchmarks, the GPT performance is affected to some extent.</p>
</div>
</section>
</section>
<section id="A4.SS3" class="ltx_subsection">
<h3 class="ltx_title ltx_title_subsection"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_subsection">D.3 </span>General VQA and Hallucination</h3>

<div id="A4.SS3.p1" class="ltx_para ltx_noindent">
<p id="A4.SS3.p1.1" class="ltx_p">This domain tests whether tool use helps, or at least does not hurt, on everyday visual questions and on questions designed to provoke hallucination.</p>
</div>
<section id="A4.SS3.SSS0.Px1" class="ltx_paragraph">
<h4 class="ltx_title ltx_title_paragraph">RealWorldQA.</h4>

<div id="A4.SS3.SSS0.Px1.p1" class="ltx_para ltx_noindent">
<p id="A4.SS3.SSS0.Px1.p1.1" class="ltx_p">Real-world photographs, many taken from vehicles, with multiple-choice questions about spatial layout and scene understanding <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_citep">(<a href="#bib.bib65" title="" class="ltx_ref">xAI, 2024</a>)</cite>; we use all 765 questions.</p>
</div>
</section>
<section id="A4.SS3.SSS0.Px2" class="ltx_paragraph">
<h4 class="ltx_title ltx_title_paragraph">SimpleVQA.</h4>

<div id="A4.SS3.SSS0.Px2.p1" class="ltx_para ltx_noindent">
<p id="A4.SS3.SSS0.Px2.p1.1" class="ltx_p">A factuality benchmark <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_citep">(<a href="#bib.bib49" title="" class="ltx_ref">Cheng et al., 2025</a>)</cite> of short, knowledge-bound questions about images. We use all 2,025 questions and grade answers as correct or incorrect with an LLM judge.</p>
</div>
</section>
<section id="A4.SS3.SSS0.Px3" class="ltx_paragraph">
<h4 class="ltx_title ltx_title_paragraph">HallusionBench.</h4>

<div id="A4.SS3.SSS0.Px3.p1" class="ltx_para ltx_noindent">
<p id="A4.SS3.SSS0.Px3.p1.1" class="ltx_p">Yes/no questions built from paired original and edited images and charts to expose language hallucination and visual illusion <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_citep">(<a href="#bib.bib67" title="" class="ltx_ref">Guan et al., 2024</a>)</cite>. Of the <math id="A4.SS3.SSS0.Px3.p1.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="1{,}129" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mn>1,129</mn><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">1{,}129</annotation></semantics></math> questions in the released set, we evaluate the <math id="A4.SS3.SSS0.Px3.p1.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="951" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mn>951</mn><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">951</annotation></semantics></math> that are grounded in an image. Free-form responses are first mapped to yes/no by an LLM extraction pass before scoring. The benchmark defines three accuracies: <em id="A4.SS3.SSS0.Px3.p1.1.1" class="ltx_emph ltx_font_italic">aAcc</em>, the fraction of individual questions answered correctly; <em id="A4.SS3.SSS0.Px3.p1.1.2" class="ltx_emph ltx_font_italic">qAcc</em>, the fraction of question groups for which <em id="A4.SS3.SSS0.Px3.p1.1.3" class="ltx_emph ltx_font_italic">every</em> question is correct; and <em id="A4.SS3.SSS0.Px3.p1.1.4" class="ltx_emph ltx_font_italic">fAcc</em>, the fraction of figures for which every associated question is correct. The latter two are deliberately strict, since a model that answers from language priors tends to succeed on the original image and fail on its edited counterpart. We report the benchmark’s <em id="A4.SS3.SSS0.Px3.p1.1.5" class="ltx_emph ltx_font_italic">Overall</em> score, the unweighted mean of the three.</p>
</div>
</section>
<section id="A4.SS3.SSS0.Px4" class="ltx_paragraph">
<h4 class="ltx_title ltx_title_paragraph">FREAK-VQA and FREAK-MCQ.</h4>

<div id="A4.SS3.SSS0.Px4.p1" class="ltx_para ltx_noindent">
<p id="A4.SS3.SSS0.Px4.p1.1" class="ltx_p">FREAK <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_citep">(<a href="#bib.bib68" title="" class="ltx_ref">Yin et al., 2026</a>)</cite> asks about counter-commonsense images, where answering from priors rather than the pixels leads to hallucination, across detection, counting, attribute, position, and OCR questions. It comes in an open-ended format (799 questions, graded by an LLM judge) and a four-option multiple-choice format (994 questions) that includes “the correct answer is not listed”.</p>
</div>
</section>
<section id="A4.SS3.SSS0.Px5" class="ltx_paragraph">
<h4 class="ltx_title ltx_title_paragraph">V*.</h4>

<div id="A4.SS3.SSS0.Px5.p1" class="ltx_para ltx_noindent">
<p id="A4.SS3.SSS0.Px5.p1.1" class="ltx_p">Questions about small attributes and relative positions of objects in high-resolution images <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_citep">(<a href="#bib.bib74" title="" class="ltx_ref">Wu and Xie, 2024</a>)</cite>, where the relevant detail is easy to miss at the model’s input resolution; we use all 191 questions.</p>
</div>
</section>
</section>
<section id="A4.SS4" class="ltx_subsection">
<h3 class="ltx_title ltx_title_subsection"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_subsection">D.4 </span>Spatial Reasoning</h3>

<div id="A4.SS4.p1" class="ltx_para ltx_noindent">
<p id="A4.SS4.p1.1" class="ltx_p">The spatial reasoning benchmarks are multiple-choice, and several of them provide multiple views of a scene, which exercises the harness’s handling of several input images. We follow the evaluation split used by the EASI leaderboard <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_citep">(<a href="#bib.bib108" title="" class="ltx_ref">Cai et al., 2025</a>)</cite> and use its official implementation in the VLMEvalKit repository <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_citep">(<a href="#bib.bib109" title="" class="ltx_ref">Duan et al., 2024</a>)</cite>.</p>
</div>
<section id="A4.SS4.SSS0.Px1" class="ltx_paragraph">
<h4 class="ltx_title ltx_title_paragraph">MMSI-Bench.</h4>

<div id="A4.SS4.SSS0.Px1.p1" class="ltx_para ltx_noindent">
<p id="A4.SS4.SSS0.Px1.p1.1" class="ltx_p">Multi-image spatial-intelligence questions <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_citep">(<a href="#bib.bib69" title="" class="ltx_ref">Yang et al., 2025b</a>)</cite> spanning positional relations, attributes, motion, and multi-step reasoning across views; we use the 1,000 questions in the standard (non-circular) setting.</p>
</div>
</section>
<section id="A4.SS4.SSS0.Px2" class="ltx_paragraph">
<h4 class="ltx_title ltx_title_paragraph">ERQA.</h4>

<div id="A4.SS4.SSS0.Px2.p1" class="ltx_para ltx_noindent">
<p id="A4.SS4.SSS0.Px2.p1.1" class="ltx_p">The embodied reasoning benchmark released with Gemini Robotics <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_citep">(<a href="#bib.bib70" title="" class="ltx_ref">Team et al., 2025</a>)</cite>, with 400 multiple-choice questions on spatial reasoning, trajectories, actions, and state estimation, some with several images.</p>
</div>
</section>
<section id="A4.SS4.SSS0.Px3" class="ltx_paragraph">
<h4 class="ltx_title ltx_title_paragraph">EmbSpatial-Bench.</h4>

<div id="A4.SS4.SSS0.Px3.p1" class="ltx_para ltx_noindent">
<p id="A4.SS4.SSS0.Px3.p1.1" class="ltx_p">Spatial-relation questions posed from an egocentric viewpoint in embodied 3D scenes <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_citep">(<a href="#bib.bib71" title="" class="ltx_ref">Du et al., 2024</a>)</cite>; we use all 3,640 questions.</p>
</div>
</section>
<section id="A4.SS4.SSS0.Px4" class="ltx_paragraph">
<h4 class="ltx_title ltx_title_paragraph">SparBench.</h4>

<div id="A4.SS4.SSS0.Px4.p1" class="ltx_para ltx_noindent">
<p id="A4.SS4.SSS0.Px4.p1.1" class="ltx_p">Spatial perception and reasoning tasks over single and multiple views <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_citep">(<a href="#bib.bib72" title="" class="ltx_ref">Zhang et al., 2026a</a>)</cite>. We use the tiny split: <math id="A4.SS4.SSS0.Px4.p1.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="20" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mn>20</mn><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">20</annotation></semantics></math> tasks <math id="A4.SS4.SSS0.Px4.p1.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\times" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mo>×</mo><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\times</annotation></semantics></math> <math id="A4.SS4.SSS0.Px4.p1.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="50" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mn>50</mn><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">50</annotation></semantics></math> questions <math id="A4.SS4.SSS0.Px4.p1.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="=1{,}000" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mphantom></mphantom><mo>=</mo><mn>1,000</mn></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">=1{,}000</annotation></semantics></math> questions. Tasks are scored by type. Eleven multiple-choice tasks (spatial relations, spatial imagination, position matching, camera-motion inference) are scored by option-match accuracy. Eight numeric tasks (depth and distance prediction, object-centric and object-to-object, single- and multi-view) are scored by mean relative accuracy (MRA), which averages the indicator that the relative error falls below a threshold, sweeping the threshold from <math id="A4.SS4.SSS0.Px4.p1.m5" class="ltx_Math" alttext="0.5" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mn>0.5</mn><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">0.5</annotation></semantics></math> to <math id="A4.SS4.SSS0.Px4.p1.m6" class="ltx_Math" alttext="0.95" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mn>0.95</mn><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">0.95</annotation></semantics></math> in steps of <math id="A4.SS4.SSS0.Px4.p1.m7" class="ltx_Math" alttext="0.05" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mn>0.05</mn><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">0.05</annotation></semantics></math>, so partially accurate magnitude estimates receive partial credit. One task, view-change inference, uses the benchmark’s own view-change metric. We report the <em id="A4.SS4.SSS0.Px4.p1.1.1" class="ltx_emph ltx_font_italic">Overall</em> score: the unweighted mean of the <math id="A4.SS4.SSS0.Px4.p1.m8" class="ltx_Math" alttext="20" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mn>20</mn><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">20</annotation></semantics></math> per-task metrics, so each task contributes equally regardless of its scoring type and regardless of the differing scales of accuracy and MRA.</p>
</div>
</section>
<section id="A4.SS4.SSS0.Px5" class="ltx_paragraph">
<h4 class="ltx_title ltx_title_paragraph">MindCube.</h4>

<div id="A4.SS4.SSS0.Px5.p1" class="ltx_para ltx_noindent">
<p id="A4.SS4.SSS0.Px5.p1.1" class="ltx_p">Questions that require building a mental model of a scene from a few partial views, covering “among”, “around”, and rotation settings <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_citep">(<a href="#bib.bib73" title="" class="ltx_ref">Wang et al., 2025c</a>)</cite>; we use the 1,040-question MindCube-Tiny split.</p>
</div>
</section>
</section>
</section>
<section id="A5" class="ltx_appendix">
<h2 class="ltx_title ltx_title_appendix"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_appendix">Appendix E </span>Failure Analysis and Harness Optimization Details</h2>

<div id="A5.p1" class="ltx_para ltx_noindent">
<p id="A5.p1.1" class="ltx_p">This appendix describes how failure diagnosis guides specialization of OpenVLHarness on a user’s data. We detail the critique protocol, the mapping from diagnoses to remedy channels, and the two workflows that modify harness text or add task-specific tools without updating model weights. We then provide examples of the resulting edits and tools.</p>
</div>
<section id="A5.SS1" class="ltx_subsection">
<h3 class="ltx_title ltx_title_subsection"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_subsection">E.1 </span>Critique Protocol</h3>

<section id="A5.SS1.SSS0.Px1" class="ltx_paragraph">
<h4 class="ltx_title ltx_title_paragraph">Failure selection and coverage.</h4>

<div id="A5.SS1.SSS0.Px1.p1" class="ltx_para ltx_noindent">
<p id="A5.SS1.SSS0.Px1.p1.1" class="ltx_p">We use Kimi K3 to critique failures of the unoptimized OpenVLHarness with Qwen3-VL-8B, obtaining <math id="A5.SS1.SSS0.Px1.p1.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="9{,}893" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mn>9,893</mn><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">9{,}893</annotation></semantics></math> completed critiques across 22 benchmarks. Failures follow each benchmark’s judge verdict. For OdinW-13-All, FSCD-147, and FSCD-LVIS, which report box AP without per-image verdicts, we select images whose predicted boxes have F1 below <math id="A5.SS1.SSS0.Px1.p1.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="0.5" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mn>0.5</mn><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">0.5</annotation></semantics></math> at IoU <math id="A5.SS1.SSS0.Px1.p1.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="0.5" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mn>0.5</mn><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">0.5</annotation></semantics></math>. Table <a href="#A5.T23" title="Table 23 ‣ E.2 Remedy Channels and Per-Dataset Results ‣ Appendix E Failure Analysis and Harness Optimization Details ‣ OpenVLHarness: Real World Visual Agentic Tool-Use with Multimodal Memory" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">23</span></a> reports the completed coverage.</p>
</div>
</section>
<section id="A5.SS1.SSS0.Px2" class="ltx_paragraph">
<h4 class="ltx_title ltx_title_paragraph">Critic inputs and outputs.</h4>

<div id="A5.SS1.SSS0.Px2.p1" class="ltx_para ltx_noindent">
<p id="A5.SS1.SSS0.Px2.p1.1" class="ltx_p">Each critique includes the question, reference and predicted answers, full trajectory, input and tool-generated images, and toolbox from the run’s configuration. Tool descriptions and argument schemas are supplied with called tools marked, so the critic can assess omissions against the capabilities actually available. For each call, it labels information usefulness (<em id="A5.SS1.SSS0.Px2.p1.1.1" class="ltx_emph ltx_font_italic">yes</em>, <em id="A5.SS1.SSS0.Px2.p1.1.2" class="ltx_emph ltx_font_italic">partial</em>, or <em id="A5.SS1.SSS0.Px2.p1.1.3" class="ltx_emph ltx_font_italic">no</em>) and correctness (<em id="A5.SS1.SSS0.Px2.p1.1.4" class="ltx_emph ltx_font_italic">correct</em>, <em id="A5.SS1.SSS0.Px2.p1.1.5" class="ltx_emph ltx_font_italic">wrong</em>, or <em id="A5.SS1.SSS0.Px2.p1.1.6" class="ltx_emph ltx_font_italic">unverifiable</em>). It also returns one primary failure cause, a proposed fix type and sketch, supporting transcript excerpts, and confidence, all in a structured JSON record. Each critique uses approximately <math id="A5.SS1.SSS0.Px2.p1.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="3" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mn>3</mn><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">3</annotation></semantics></math>k prompt tokens and <math id="A5.SS1.SSS0.Px2.p1.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="0.7" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mn>0.7</mn><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">0.7</annotation></semantics></math>–<math id="A5.SS1.SSS0.Px2.p1.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="2" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mn>2</mn><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">2</annotation></semantics></math>k completion tokens.</p>
</div>
</section>
<section id="A5.SS1.SSS0.Px3" class="ltx_paragraph">
<h4 class="ltx_title ltx_title_paragraph">Taxonomy.</h4>

<div id="A5.SS1.SSS0.Px3.p1" class="ltx_para ltx_noindent">
<p id="A5.SS1.SSS0.Px3.p1.1" class="ltx_p">The critic separately identifies where the trajectory failed and what change could address it (Table <a href="#A5.T22" title="Table 22 ‣ Taxonomy. ‣ E.1 Critique Protocol ‣ Appendix E Failure Analysis and Harness Optimization Details ‣ OpenVLHarness: Real World Visual Agentic Tool-Use with Multimodal Memory" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">22</span></a>). A <em id="A5.SS1.SSS0.Px3.p1.1.1" class="ltx_emph ltx_font_italic">build-tool</em> fix requires a new callable, including wrappers that verify, filter, or translate outputs and compositions of existing capabilities. A <em id="A5.SS1.SSS0.Px3.p1.1.2" class="ltx_emph ltx_font_italic">text-only</em> fix changes instructions for using existing tools. These labels are subsequently combined into remedy channels; they do not directly determine which workflow to use.</p>
</div>
