<section id="A5.SS1.SSS0.Px4" class="ltx_paragraph">
<h4 class="ltx_title ltx_title_paragraph">Human agreement.</h4>

<div id="A5.SS1.SSS0.Px4.p1" class="ltx_para ltx_noindent">
<p id="A5.SS1.SSS0.Px4.p1.1" class="ltx_p">Human annotators independently labeled 100 randomly sampled critiqued failures, with an average agreement of <math id="A5.SS1.SSS0.Px4.p1.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="92\%" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mn>92</mn><mo>%</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">92\%</annotation></semantics></math> with the critic. The abridged prompt below specifies the evidence requirements and fixed labels.</p>
</div>
<div id="A5.SS1.SSS0.Px4.p2" class="ltx_para ltx_noindent"><span class="ltx_inline-block"><svg id="A5.SS1.SSS0.Px4.p2.pic1" class="ltx_picture" height="594.88" overflow="visible" version="1.1" viewBox="0 0 550 594.88" width="550"><g style="--ltx-stroke-color:#000000;--ltx-fill-color:#000000;" fill="#000000" stroke="#000000" stroke-width="0.4pt" transform="translate(0,594.88) matrix(1 0 0 -1 0 0)"><g style="--ltx-fill-color:#404040;" fill="#404040" fill-opacity="1.0"><path style="stroke:none" d="M 0 5.91 L 0 588.97 C 0 592.23 2.64 594.88 5.91 594.88 L 544.09 594.88 C 547.36 594.88 550 592.23 550 588.97 L 550 5.91 C 550 2.64 547.36 0 544.09 0 L 5.91 0 C 2.64 0 0 2.64 0 5.91 Z"></path></g><g style="--ltx-fill-color:#F2F2F2;" fill="#F2F2F2" fill-opacity="1.0"><path style="stroke:none" d="M 1.97 5.91 L 1.97 569.23 L 548.03 569.23 L 548.03 5.91 C 548.03 3.73 546.27 1.97 544.09 1.97 L 5.91 1.97 C 3.73 1.97 1.97 3.73 1.97 5.91 Z"></path></g><g fill-opacity="1.0" transform="matrix(1.0 0.0 0.0 1.0 21.65 578.6)"><foreignObject style="--ltx-fo-width:36.62em;--ltx-fo-height:0.75em;--ltx-fo-depth:0.25em;font-size:10pt;" height="13.84" overflow="visible" transform="matrix(1 0 0 -1 0 10.38)" width="506.72"><span class="ltx_foreignobject_container"><span class="ltx_foreignobject_content">
<span id="A5.SS1.SSS0.Px4.p2.pic1.1" class="ltx_inline-block ltx_minipage ltx_align_bottom" style="width:36.62em;">
<span id="A5.SS1.SSS0.Px4.p2.pic1.1.1" class="ltx_p"><span id="A5.SS1.SSS0.Px4.p2.pic1.1.1.1" class="ltx_text" style="--ltx-fg-color:#FFFFFF;">Critic prompt (abridged)</span></span>
</span></span></span></foreignObject></g><g fill-opacity="1.0" transform="matrix(1.0 0.0 0.0 1.0 21.65 16.47)"><foreignObject style="--ltx-fo-width:36.62em;--ltx-fo-height:39.09em;--ltx-fo-depth:0.19em;font-size:10pt;" height="543.65" overflow="visible" transform="matrix(1 0 0 -1 0 540.96)" width="506.72"><span class="ltx_foreignobject_container"><span class="ltx_foreignobject_content">
<span id="A5.SS1.SSS0.Px4.p2.pic1.2" class="ltx_inline-block ltx_minipage ltx_align_bottom" style="width:36.62em;">
<span id="A5.SS1.SSS0.Px4.p2.pic1.2.1" class="ltx_p"><span id="A5.SS1.SSS0.Px4.p2.pic1.2.1.1" class="ltx_text" style="--ltx-fg-color:#000000;">You are a strict, evidence-driven critic of a vision-language tool-using agent. You are shown ONE trajectory where the agent answered a visual question wrongly, together with the correct answer and the catalog of tools the agent could call. […] The single question your critique must answer cleanly is: does fixing this family of failures require NEW TOOL CODE the agent can call, or only a TEXT change? […]</span></span>
<span id="A5.SS1.SSS0.Px4.p2.pic1.2.2" class="ltx_p"><span id="A5.SS1.SSS0.Px4.p2.pic1.2.2.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold" style="--ltx-fg-color:#000000;">How to judge each tool call.<span id="A5.SS1.SSS0.Px4.p2.pic1.2.2.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_medium"> For every tool the agent called, decide <span id="A5.SS1.SSS0.Px4.p2.pic1.2.2.1.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_typewriter">info_useful</span> (did the returned information bear on answering this question?) and <span id="A5.SS1.SSS0.Px4.p2.pic1.2.2.1.1.2" class="ltx_text ltx_font_typewriter">info_correct</span> (was that information actually right, judged against the image content, the question, and the correct answer?). If a grounding tool reports 3 boxes but the correct answer implies 5 objects, that output is wrong. If you cannot tell, say unverifiable; do not guess.</span></span></span>
<span id="A5.SS1.SSS0.Px4.p2.pic1.2.3" class="ltx_p"><span id="A5.SS1.SSS0.Px4.p2.pic1.2.3.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold" style="--ltx-fg-color:#000000;">Root cause.<span id="A5.SS1.SSS0.Px4.p2.pic1.2.3.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_medium"> Choose exactly one of <span id="A5.SS1.SSS0.Px4.p2.pic1.2.3.1.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_typewriter">tool_not_called</span>, <span id="A5.SS1.SSS0.Px4.p2.pic1.2.3.1.1.2" class="ltx_text ltx_font_typewriter">tool_output_wrong</span>, <span id="A5.SS1.SSS0.Px4.p2.pic1.2.3.1.1.3" class="ltx_text ltx_font_typewriter">tool_output_unusable_form</span>, <span id="A5.SS1.SSS0.Px4.p2.pic1.2.3.1.1.4" class="ltx_text ltx_font_typewriter">tool_output_ignored</span>, <span id="A5.SS1.SSS0.Px4.p2.pic1.2.3.1.1.5" class="ltx_text ltx_font_typewriter">reasoning_error</span>, <span id="A5.SS1.SSS0.Px4.p2.pic1.2.3.1.1.6" class="ltx_text ltx_font_typewriter">label_or_question_issue</span>. <span id="A5.SS1.SSS0.Px4.p2.pic1.2.3.1.1.7" class="ltx_text ltx_font_typewriter">tool_not_called</span> must name a tool from the agent’s toolbox that would have supplied the missing evidence; if no listed tool could have, describe the missing capability in the fix sketch instead. […]</span></span></span>
<span id="A5.SS1.SSS0.Px4.p2.pic1.2.4" class="ltx_p"><span id="A5.SS1.SSS0.Px4.p2.pic1.2.4.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold" style="--ltx-fg-color:#000000;">Rules.<span id="A5.SS1.SSS0.Px4.p2.pic1.2.4.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_medium"> Ground every claim in the transcript: quote the tool output or the agent’s text. Never assume a tool was right just because the agent believed it. Do not propose memorizing answers or matching question wording; a fix sketch must describe a general capability or change. Set confidence low when the evidence is thin. Return ONLY a JSON object.</span></span></span>
</span></span></span></foreignObject></g></g></svg></span>
</div>
</section>
</section>
<section id="A5.SS2" class="ltx_subsection">
<h3 class="ltx_title ltx_title_subsection"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_subsection">E.2 </span>Remedy Channels and Per-Dataset Results</h3>

<div id="A5.SS2.p1" class="ltx_para ltx_noindent">
<p id="A5.SS2.p1.1" class="ltx_p">Each failed trajectory receives two labels from the critic: a <em id="A5.SS2.p1.1.1" class="ltx_emph ltx_font_italic">root cause</em>, describing where it failed, and a <em id="A5.SS2.p1.1.2" class="ltx_emph ltx_font_italic">fix type</em>, indicating whether the proposed remedy requires new tool code, a text change, or neither. We determine its remedy channel jointly from this pair, using the tool-call assessments where needed. For example, an omitted call paired with a build-tool fix maps to tool composition (C), whereas the same cause paired with a text-only fix maps to harness text (Y).</p>
</div>
<div id="A5.SS2.p2" class="ltx_para ltx_noindent">
<p id="A5.SS2.p2.1" class="ltx_p">The channels distinguish <em id="A5.SS2.p2.1.1" class="ltx_emph ltx_font_italic">adding task-specific tools</em> (A+B+C) from <em id="A5.SS2.p2.1.2" class="ltx_emph ltx_font_italic">refining harness text</em> (Y). New tools can make evidence interpretation explicit, refine outputs, or compose existing capabilities. Harness text comprises the system prompt, tool descriptions, and evidence-rendering templates; editing it guides when existing tools are called and how their results are used. We first assign cases with fix type <em id="A5.SS2.p2.1.3" class="ltx_emph ltx_font_italic">none</em> or a question/reference-answer issue to Z, then map the remaining cause–fix pairs as follows:</p>
<ul id="A5.I1" class="ltx_itemize" style="--ltx-enum-leftmargin:1.4em;">
<li id="A5.I1.i1" class="ltx_item" style="list-style-type:none;"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_item">•</span> 
<div id="A5.I1.i1.p1" class="ltx_para ltx_noindent">
<p id="A5.I1.i1.p1.1" class="ltx_p"><span id="A5.I1.i1.p1.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">A: evidence interpretation tools.</span> An ignored-output or reasoning-error cause paired with a build-tool fix. We also include text-only fixes for ignored outputs, or for reasoning errors when at least one call returned correct, at least partially useful evidence. These tools turn available evidence into explicit task-level conclusions that the model can more readily use.</p>
</div></li>
<li id="A5.I1.i2" class="ltx_item" style="list-style-type:none;"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_item">•</span> 
<div id="A5.I1.i2.p1" class="ltx_para ltx_noindent">
<p id="A5.I1.i2.p1.1" class="ltx_p"><span id="A5.I1.i2.p1.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">B: output verification and refinement tools.</span> A wrong- or unusable-output cause paired with a build-tool fix, motivating systematic verification, filtering, or translation.</p>
</div></li>
<li id="A5.I1.i3" class="ltx_item" style="list-style-type:none;"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_item">•</span> 
<div id="A5.I1.i3.p1" class="ltx_para ltx_noindent">
<p id="A5.I1.i3.p1.1" class="ltx_p"><span id="A5.I1.i3.p1.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">C: tool composition.</span> An omitted-call cause paired with a build-tool fix, motivating a task-specific callable that combines existing capabilities into the required operation.</p>
</div></li>
<li id="A5.I1.i4" class="ltx_item" style="list-style-type:none;"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_item">•</span> 
<div id="A5.I1.i4.p1" class="ltx_para ltx_noindent">
<p id="A5.I1.i4.p1.1" class="ltx_p"><span id="A5.I1.i4.p1.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">Y: harness text for tool-use guidance.</span> The remaining causes paired with a text-only fix.</p>
</div></li>
<li id="A5.I1.i5" class="ltx_item" style="list-style-type:none;"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_item">•</span> 
<div id="A5.I1.i5.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="A5.I1.i5.p1.1" class="ltx_p"><span id="A5.I1.i5.p1.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">Z: no proposed remedy.</span> Cases excluded above because no fix is proposed or the question/reference answer is problematic.</p>
</div></li>
</ul>
<p id="A5.SS2.p2.2" class="ltx_p">The motivation for A is that correct, useful evidence may still require interpretation before it supports an answer. A reasoning error despite such evidence can therefore indicate difficulty interpreting or incorporating it, as can an ignored output. We include these cases even when the critic proposes a text change, to test whether a tool that makes the relevant conclusion explicit can address the difficulty. This motivates a candidate remedy rather than establishing that interpretation caused every such failure.</p>
</div>
<div id="A5.SS2.p3" class="ltx_para ltx_noindent">
<p id="A5.SS2.p3.1" class="ltx_p">Table <a href="#A5.T23" title="Table 23 ‣ E.2 Remedy Channels and Per-Dataset Results ‣ Appendix E Failure Analysis and Harness Optimization Details ‣ OpenVLHarness: Real World Visual Agentic Tool-Use with Multimodal Memory" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">23</span></a> reports the channel shares among critiqued failures. The final column illustrates why an omitted call alone does not identify the remedy: the critic proposes new tool code for <math id="A5.SS2.p3.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="70\%" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mn>70</mn><mo>%</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">70\%</annotation></semantics></math> of such failures on SparBench, but only <math id="A5.SS2.p3.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="11\%" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mn>11</mn><mo>%</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">11\%</annotation></semantics></math> on ERQA. The four datasets in Sec. <a href="#S3.SS6" title="3.6 Harness Optimization ‣ 3 Experiments ‣ OpenVLHarness: Real World Visual Agentic Tool-Use with Multimodal Memory" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">3.6</span></a> span contrasting profiles: Y accounts for <math id="A5.SS2.p3.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="71\%" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mn>71</mn><mo>%</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">71\%</annotation></semantics></math> of ERQA and <math id="A5.SS2.p3.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="56\%" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mn>56</mn><mo>%</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">56\%</annotation></semantics></math> of RealWorldQA failures, while A+B+C account for <math id="A5.SS2.p3.m5" class="ltx_Math" alttext="75\%" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mn>75</mn><mo>%</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">75\%</annotation></semantics></math> of SparBench and <math id="A5.SS2.p3.m6" class="ltx_Math" alttext="62\%" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mn>62</mn><mo>%</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">62\%</annotation></semantics></math> of FREAK-VQA failures. These shares guide the choice of workflow; they do not predict accuracy gains. Critiques of failures alone cannot establish whether an edit harms previously correct answers or whether the orchestrator will use a generated tool.</p>
</div>
