<section id="A5.SS3" class="ltx_subsection">
<h3 class="ltx_title ltx_title_subsection"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_subsection">E.3 </span>Optimization Algorithms</h3>

<div id="A5.SS3.p1" class="ltx_para ltx_noindent">
<p id="A5.SS3.p1.1" class="ltx_p">Both workflows use a coding agent (Codex with GPT-5.5) to inspect trajectories and modify a scratch copy of the harness. Candidates are evaluated by running the Qwen3-VL-8B orchestrator and applying the benchmark judge. Harness search changes instructions and evidence presentation; tool generation adds callable operations built from existing capabilities. Both keep model weights fixed.</p>
</div>
<section id="A5.SS3.SSS0.Px1" class="ltx_paragraph">
<h4 class="ltx_title ltx_title_paragraph">Harness search.</h4>

<div id="A5.SS3.SSS0.Px1.p1" class="ltx_para ltx_noindent">
<p id="A5.SS3.SSS0.Px1.p1.1" class="ltx_p">Algorithm <a href="#alg1" title="Algorithm 1 ‣ Harness search. ‣ E.3 Optimization Algorithms ‣ Appendix E Failure Analysis and Harness Optimization Details ‣ OpenVLHarness: Real World Visual Agentic Tool-Use with Multimodal Memory" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">1</span></a> maintains a pool of harness states represented as file overlays on the base repository. The editable components are the system prompt and tool-use policy, tool descriptions, and model-facing output templates. For each parent state, an analysis agent inspects failed trajectories and may test hypotheses on up to 20 tasks with five trials each. A proposer then produces <math id="A5.SS3.SSS0.Px1.p1.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="M" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>M</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">M</annotation></semantics></math> distinct patches without instance-specific rules. Candidates include individual patches and compatible combinations of up to <math id="A5.SS3.SSS0.Px1.p1.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="K" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>K</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">K</annotation></semantics></math> patches. Each candidate is evaluated with <math id="A5.SS3.SSS0.Px1.p1.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="S" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>S</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">S</annotation></semantics></math> samples per instance using paired sampling seeds. The top <math id="A5.SS3.SSS0.Px1.p1.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="N" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>N</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">N</annotation></semantics></math> parents and children by mean search accuracy survive, with older states winning exact ties. A campaign runbook records proposals and scores for subsequent rounds.</p>
</div>
<div id="A5.SS3.SSS0.Px1.p2" class="ltx_para ltx_noindent">
<p id="A5.SS3.SSS0.Px1.p2.1" class="ltx_p">We use <math id="A5.SS3.SSS0.Px1.p2.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="N{=}4" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>N</mi><mo>=</mo><mn>4</mn></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">N{=}4</annotation></semantics></math>, <math id="A5.SS3.SSS0.Px1.p2.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="M{=}4" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>M</mi><mo>=</mo><mn>4</mn></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">M{=}4</annotation></semantics></math>, <math id="A5.SS3.SSS0.Px1.p2.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="K{=}4" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>K</mi><mo>=</mo><mn>4</mn></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">K{=}4</annotation></semantics></math>, <math id="A5.SS3.SSS0.Px1.p2.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="S{=}3" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>S</mi><mo>=</mo><mn>3</mn></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">S{=}3</annotation></semantics></math>, and three rounds. ERQA uses 100 samples and is evaluated on a disjoint 300-sample validation split for assessing leading candidates. Similarly, RealWorldQA uses 100 samples and is reported on the disjoint 665 validation split. The first-round analysis can optionally use an earlier critique report, provided its claims are checked against the current trajectories. Thus, failure diagnosis can guide the choice of workflow without being a required input to the search algorithm.</p>
</div>
