<h4 class="ltx_title ltx_title_paragraph">Tool generation.</h4>

<div id="A5.SS3.SSS0.Px2.p1" class="ltx_para ltx_noindent">
<p id="A5.SS3.SSS0.Px2.p1.1" class="ltx_p">Algorithm <a href="#alg2" title="Algorithm 2 ‣ Tool generation. ‣ E.3 Optimization Algorithms ‣ Appendix E Failure Analysis and Harness Optimization Details ‣ OpenVLHarness: Real World Visual Agentic Tool-Use with Multimodal Memory" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">2</span></a> groups source failures into at most <math id="A5.SS3.SSS0.Px2.p1.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="D" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>D</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">D</annotation></semantics></math> candidate tool directions, each describing a reusable operation and the failures it should address. Supporting examples are divided equally into tuning and held-out subsets. A tool-writing agent implements each candidate by composing existing capabilities; it may invoke tools, execute code, and query the base model. For up to <math id="A5.SS3.SSS0.Px2.p1.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="T" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>T</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">T</annotation></semantics></math> rounds, the candidate is registered in the scratch harness and evaluated on the tuning subset. A separate diagnosis agent classifies its use as not called, execution failed, evidence inadequate, evidence misinterpreted, or used correctly, and the tool-writing agent revises the implementation accordingly. Dataset- or instance-specific answer rules and lookup tables are prohibited.</p>
</div>
<div id="A5.SS3.SSS0.Px2.p2" class="ltx_para ltx_noindent">
<p id="A5.SS3.SSS0.Px2.p2.1" class="ltx_p">The final gate requires complete scoring, at least two recovered failures and positive net lift across all supporting examples, and at least one recovered failure with positive net lift on the held-out half, whose trajectories were not shown to the tool-writing agent. Because these examples are selected from baseline failures, this gate measures recovery rather than regressions on initially correct answers. Accepted tools are collected into a bank and evaluated together with the original harness on the target dataset. We use <math id="A5.SS3.SSS0.Px2.p2.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="D{\leq}5" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>D</mi><mo>≤</mo><mn>5</mn></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">D{\leq}5</annotation></semantics></math>, <math id="A5.SS3.SSS0.Px2.p2.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="T{=}5" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>T</mi><mo>=</mo><mn>5</mn></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">T{=}5</annotation></semantics></math>, and 50 examples per direction on SparBench and 30 on FREAK-VQA. For both SparBench and FREAK-VQA, we generate tools on data disjoint from the standard evaluation splits and evaluate the resulting harness on the same splits used in our main experiments.</p>
</div>
