<section id="A6" class="ltx_appendix">
<h2 class="ltx_title ltx_title_appendix"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_appendix">Appendix F </span>Qualitative Examples</h2>

<div id="A6.p1" class="ltx_para ltx_noindent">
<p id="A6.p1.1" class="ltx_p">This section shows seventeen complete OpenVLHarness trajectories, ordered as the datasets appear in Table <a href="#S3.T1" title="Table 1 ‣ Tasks. ‣ 3.1 Tasks, Models, and Baselines ‣ 3 Experiments ‣ OpenVLHarness: Real World Visual Agentic Tool-Use with Multimodal Memory" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">1</span></a>: grounded perception (Examples <a href="#A6" title="Appendix F Qualitative Examples ‣ OpenVLHarness: Real World Visual Agentic Tool-Use with Multimodal Memory" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">F</span></a>–<a href="#A6" title="Appendix F Qualitative Examples ‣ OpenVLHarness: Real World Visual Agentic Tool-Use with Multimodal Memory" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">F</span></a>), visual search and deep research (Examples <a href="#A6" title="Appendix F Qualitative Examples ‣ OpenVLHarness: Real World Visual Agentic Tool-Use with Multimodal Memory" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">F</span></a>–<a href="#A6" title="Appendix F Qualitative Examples ‣ OpenVLHarness: Real World Visual Agentic Tool-Use with Multimodal Memory" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">F</span></a>), general VQA and hallucination (Examples <a href="#A6" title="Appendix F Qualitative Examples ‣ OpenVLHarness: Real World Visual Agentic Tool-Use with Multimodal Memory" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">F</span></a>–<a href="#A6" title="Appendix F Qualitative Examples ‣ OpenVLHarness: Real World Visual Agentic Tool-Use with Multimodal Memory" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">F</span></a>) and spatial reasoning (Examples <a href="#A6" title="Appendix F Qualitative Examples ‣ OpenVLHarness: Real World Visual Agentic Tool-Use with Multimodal Memory" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">F</span></a>–<a href="#A6" title="Appendix F Qualitative Examples ‣ OpenVLHarness: Real World Visual Agentic Tool-Use with Multimodal Memory" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">F</span></a>). Eleven come from GPT-6 Sol and GPT-6 Luna, taken from the runs behind Table <a href="#S3.T1" title="Table 1 ‣ Tasks. ‣ 3.1 Tasks, Models, and Baselines ‣ 3 Experiments ‣ OpenVLHarness: Real World Visual Agentic Tool-Use with Multimodal Memory" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">1</span></a>; six come from OpenVLHarness runs of Qwen3-VL-32B. Each example starts on a new page under a dark title bar and ends with an “End of Example” rule, and every step header names its example. Each example is laid out the same way:</p>
</div>
<div id="A6.p2" class="ltx_para ltx_noindent">
<ul id="A6.I1" class="ltx_itemize" style="--ltx-enum-leftmargin:1.2em;">
<li id="A6.I1.i1" class="ltx_item" style="list-style-type:none;"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_item">•</span> 
<div id="A6.I1.i1.p1" class="ltx_para ltx_noindent">
<p id="A6.I1.i1.p1.1" class="ltx_p"><span id="A6.I1.i1.p1.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">Overview and step notes.</span> Each example opens with a <span id="A6.I1.i1.p1.1.2" class="ltx_text" style="--ltx-bg-color:#EEF6F0;">green overview box</span> that explains in plain words what the task asks, why it is hard without tools, and how the agent solves it. For the proprietary GPT-6 models, each step then opens with a short green “What happens” note that says what the call does and why it matters. We add these notes only for proprietary models, whose reasoning traces we cannot access: the API returns the tool calls and any visible text, but not the model’s reasoning, and GPT-6 often calls a tool without writing any text at all. The open Qwen3-VL models expose their reasoning, which they write in <span id="A6.I1.i1.p1.1.3" class="ltx_text ltx_font_typewriter">&lt;thinking&gt;</span> tags before each call and which we show verbatim, so their examples have no step notes. The overviews and notes are our descriptions, written to guide the reader; everything else in the trajectory is verbatim.</p>
</div></li>
<li id="A6.I1.i2" class="ltx_item" style="list-style-type:none;"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_item">•</span> 
<div id="A6.I1.i2.p1" class="ltx_para ltx_noindent">
<p id="A6.I1.i2.p1.1" class="ltx_p"><span id="A6.I1.i2.p1.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">Inputs, question and results.</span> The input images and the question exactly as sent to the model, then a table comparing the answer with and without the harness against the ground truth. For grounded perception examples, the table also shows what SAM 3, the model behind Visual Grounding, returns when queried alone, where the trajectory makes that measurable. For some Qwen3-VL examples no run of the same model without tools is available, and the table says so.</p>
</div></li>
<li id="A6.I1.i3" class="ltx_item" style="list-style-type:none;"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_item">•</span> 
<div id="A6.I1.i3.p1" class="ltx_para ltx_noindent">
<p id="A6.I1.i3.p1.1" class="ltx_p"><span id="A6.I1.i3.p1.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">Without tools.</span> The same model’s full response to the same question with no tools, where such a run exists.</p>
</div></li>
<li id="A6.I1.i4" class="ltx_item" style="list-style-type:none;"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_item">•</span> 
<div id="A6.I1.i4.p1" class="ltx_para ltx_noindent">
<p id="A6.I1.i4.p1.1" class="ltx_p"><span id="A6.I1.i4.p1.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">Full trajectory with OpenVLHarness.</span> Every tool call in order. Each step shows the tool, colour-coded by family (<span id="A6.I1.i4.p1.1.2" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold" style="--ltx-fg-color:#1F6F8B;">perception</span>, <span id="A6.I1.i4.p1.1.3" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold" style="--ltx-fg-color:#B4530A;">search</span>, <span id="A6.I1.i4.p1.1.4" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold" style="--ltx-fg-color:#6A2E7A;">computation</span>); the complete arguments the model sent; the text the model wrote alongside the call, when there is any; the tool’s output, including the complete code the coding agent wrote and its execution output; and every image the tool returned, which the model sees in its next turn. For grounding examples, whose final answer is a list of coordinates, we add a panel drawing the final boxes on the input image, marked as ours.</p>
</div></li>
<li id="A6.I1.i5" class="ltx_item" style="list-style-type:none;"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_item">•</span> 
<div id="A6.I1.i5.p1" class="ltx_para ltx_noindent">
<p id="A6.I1.i5.p1.1" class="ltx_p"><span id="A6.I1.i5.p1.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">Harness messages.</span> On a <span id="A6.I1.i5.p1.1.2" class="ltx_text" style="--ltx-bg-color:#FBF7EC;">cream background</span>: the environment updates through which the multimodal memory tells the model what it currently holds (Appendix <a href="#A3.SS4" title="C.4 Multimodal Memory Artifacts ‣ Appendix C Harness Implementation Details ‣ OpenVLHarness: Real World Visual Agentic Tool-Use with Multimodal Memory" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">C.4</span></a>), including the data bindings that pass tool results to the coding agent.</p>
</div></li>
<li id="A6.I1.i6" class="ltx_item" style="list-style-type:none;"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_item">•</span> 
<div id="A6.I1.i6.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="A6.I1.i6.p1.1" class="ltx_p"><span id="A6.I1.i6.p1.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">What to notice.</span> A closing note on how the harness contributed.</p>
</div></li>
</ul>
</div>
<div id="A6.p3" class="ltx_para ltx_noindent">
<p id="A6.p3.1" class="ltx_p">All model text, arguments and tool outputs are verbatim, generated directly from the trajectory files. To keep each example readable we shorten in five ways, and every cut is marked in red where it happens. Search results show their first two entries, with the number omitted. Coding-agent execution outputs longer than 70 lines keep their first 25 and last 10 lines; the code itself is always complete. A coding-agent run that failed with an error shows the error but not its code. Other tool outputs longer than 1,500 characters are cut, with the remaining length stated; one such output in Example <a href="#A6" title="Appendix F Qualitative Examples ‣ OpenVLHarness: Real World Visual Agentic Tool-Use with Multimodal Memory" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">F</span></a> is a webpage summary that loops, repeating the same sentences for over 128,000 characters. After the first environment update, which is shown in full, each update is summarized as the memory entries it adds, with any new data bindings kept verbatim. We also show escaped newlines inside JSON strings as line breaks, and emoji in search results as [emoji] or [flag]. In Example <a href="#A6" title="Appendix F Qualitative Examples ‣ OpenVLHarness: Real World Visual Agentic Tool-Use with Multimodal Memory" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">F</span></a> the question and the model’s reasoning are in Chinese; the overview translates the question. The system prompt is given in Appendix <a href="#A3.SS5" title="C.5 System Prompt ‣ Appendix C Harness Implementation Details ‣ OpenVLHarness: Real World Visual Agentic Tool-Use with Multimodal Memory" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">C.5</span></a>.</p>
</div>
<div id="A6.p4" class="ltx_para ltx_noindent">
<p id="A6.p4.1" class="ltx_p">The examples were chosen by hand to illustrate how the harness composes tools, and are not a random sample. Not every one is a clean win, and the overviews say so where it matters: in Example <a href="#A6" title="Appendix F Qualitative Examples ‣ OpenVLHarness: Real World Visual Agentic Tool-Use with Multimodal Memory" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">F</span></a> the no-tool answer fails on coordinate format; in Example <a href="#A6" title="Appendix F Qualitative Examples ‣ OpenVLHarness: Real World Visual Agentic Tool-Use with Multimodal Memory" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">F</span></a> the perception step does not contribute; and in Examples <a href="#A6" title="Appendix F Qualitative Examples ‣ OpenVLHarness: Real World Visual Agentic Tool-Use with Multimodal Memory" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">F</span></a>, <a href="#A6" title="Appendix F Qualitative Examples ‣ OpenVLHarness: Real World Visual Agentic Tool-Use with Multimodal Memory" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">F</span></a> and <a href="#A6" title="Appendix F Qualitative Examples ‣ OpenVLHarness: Real World Visual Agentic Tool-Use with Multimodal Memory" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">F</span></a> the final answer is correct but only partly supported by the trajectory’s evidence.</p>
</div>
