<span id="A6.p6.pic1.2" class="ltx_inline-block ltx_minipage ltx_align_bottom" style="width:37.68em;">
<span id="A6.p6.pic1.2.1" class="ltx_p"><span id="A6.p6.pic1.2.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold" style="--ltx-fg-color:#000000;">Task.<span id="A6.p6.pic1.2.1.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_medium"> Count the pills in a photograph of about 140 pills of many colours and two shapes, oblong capsules and small round tablets, scattered on a dark surface.</span></span></span>
<span id="A6.p6.pic1.2.2" class="ltx_p"><span id="A6.p6.pic1.2.2.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold" style="--ltx-fg-color:#000000;">Why it is hard.<span id="A6.p6.pic1.2.2.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_medium"> There are too many to count reliably by eye, and a single detector query does not reliably find both shapes.</span></span></span>
<span id="A6.p6.pic1.2.3" class="ltx_p"><span id="A6.p6.pic1.2.3.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold" style="--ltx-fg-color:#000000;">How the agent solves it.<span id="A6.p6.pic1.2.3.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_medium"> It runs two grounding queries, one general and one for round pills, then asks the coding agent to merge the two sets of detections without counting any pill twice. The first merge fails silently; the agent notices the impossible result and rewrites the merge, which then finds the five pills the first query missed.</span></span></span>
</span></span></span></foreignObject></g></g></svg></span>
