<span id="A6.p61.pic1.1.1" class="ltx_p"><span id="A6.p61.pic1.1.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold" style="--ltx-fg-color:#000000;">What to notice.<span id="A6.p61.pic1.1.1.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_medium"> SAM 3 finds 134 pills with the query “pill” and 72 with “round pill”; the two sets overlap, and neither is the answer. The model asks the coding agent to merge them. The first run reports zero matches and a union of zero (Step 3), which cannot be right, since both queries find the same pills. The model rewrites the objective: match by box overlap or centre distance only, with no mask decoding. The second run (Step 4) finds five round pills missing from the first set, and the model answers <math id="A6.p61.pic1.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="134+5=139" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mn style="--ltx-fg-color:#000000;" mathcolor="#000000">134</mn><mo style="--ltx-fg-color:#000000;" mathcolor="#000000">+</mo><mn style="--ltx-fg-color:#000000;" mathcolor="#000000">5</mn></mrow><mo style="--ltx-fg-color:#000000;" mathcolor="#000000">=</mo><mn style="--ltx-fg-color:#000000;" mathcolor="#000000">139</mn></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">134+5=139</annotation></semantics></math>, the exact count. Without tools the model says 137. The detections reach the code as data bindings held in memory, so the model can recover from a bad run by restating the objective instead of re-detecting.</span></span></span>
