<span id="A6.p508.pic1.2.2" class="ltx_p"><span id="A6.p508.pic1.2.2.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold" style="--ltx-fg-color:#000000;">Why it is hard.<span id="A6.p508.pic1.2.2.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_medium"> Without tools the model guesses Dior.</span></span></span>
<span id="A6.p508.pic1.2.3" class="ltx_p"><span id="A6.p508.pic1.2.3.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold" style="--ltx-fg-color:#000000;">How the agent solves it.<span id="A6.p508.pic1.2.3.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_medium"> OCR reads nothing, and reverse-searching a crop of the bottle finds no match. The agent then searches by description (“faceted crystal cap”), tries a Jill Stuart hypothesis and drops it, and reverse-searches a tighter crop. That search’s summary names the bottle as KATOKATO, and a targeted image search returns a KATO-KATO foundation whose cap matches the crop.</span></span></span>
<span id="A6.p508.pic1.2.4" class="ltx_p"><span id="A6.p508.pic1.2.4.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold" style="--ltx-fg-color:#000000;">Caveat.<span id="A6.p508.pic1.2.4.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_medium"> The brand name first appears in the reverse-search tool’s own summary of the crop, so the decisive step is partly inside the tool.</span></span></span>
