<span id="A6.p822.pic1.2.1" class="ltx_p"><span id="A6.p822.pic1.2.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold" style="--ltx-fg-color:#000000;">Task.<span id="A6.p822.pic1.2.1.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_medium"> In a photograph taken from a car at a city intersection, are there any cars closer to the camera than the nearest crosswalk?</span></span></span>
<span id="A6.p822.pic1.2.2" class="ltx_p"><span id="A6.p822.pic1.2.2.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold" style="--ltx-fg-color:#000000;">Why it is hard.<span id="A6.p822.pic1.2.2.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_medium"> Judging relative distance along a street from one image is error-prone; without tools the same model answers “yes”, pointing at a silver minivan.</span></span></span>
<span id="A6.p822.pic1.2.3" class="ltx_p"><span id="A6.p822.pic1.2.3.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold" style="--ltx-fg-color:#000000;">How the agent solves it.<span id="A6.p822.pic1.2.3.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_medium"> It estimates metric depth for the whole image, grounds the crosswalk and the cars, and has the coding agent compare the crosswalk’s depth with each car’s. The crosswalk is 17.4 m away and no car is closer.</span></span></span>
</span></span></span></foreignObject></g></g></svg></span>
