<span id="A6.p1224.pic1.2" class="ltx_inline-block ltx_minipage ltx_align_bottom" style="width:37.68em;">
<span id="A6.p1224.pic1.2.1" class="ltx_p"><span id="A6.p1224.pic1.2.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold" style="--ltx-fg-color:#000000;">Task.<span id="A6.p1224.pic1.2.1.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_medium"> Two photographs show two rooms of a historic house: a parlour with a piano, and a dining room. Which is longer, the piano or the table under the mirror? Without tools the same model answers D, that the piano is longer.</span></span></span>
<span id="A6.p1224.pic1.2.2" class="ltx_p"><span id="A6.p1224.pic1.2.2.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold" style="--ltx-fg-color:#000000;">How the agent approaches it.<span id="A6.p1224.pic1.2.2.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_medium"> It grounds both objects, estimates metric depth in both photos, and has the coding agent convert each box to a real-world length.</span></span></span>
<span id="A6.p1224.pic1.2.3" class="ltx_p"><span id="A6.p1224.pic1.2.3.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold" style="--ltx-fg-color:#000000;">Caveat.<span id="A6.p1224.pic1.2.3.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_medium"> The answer is correct, but the evidence does not support it. The mirror hangs above the fireplace, so the “table under the mirror” is the mantel shelf. Grounding returns it as object 3, but the agent chooses the round dining table (object 2) instead, and concludes that the table, at 2.77 m, is longer than the piano, at 2.21 m. This example shows the harness’s tool chain, not a sound comparison.</span></span></span>
</span></span></span></foreignObject></g></g></svg></span>
</div>
