<span id="A6.p1290.pic1.2.1" class="ltx_p"><span id="A6.p1290.pic1.2.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold" style="--ltx-fg-color:#000000;">Task.<span id="A6.p1290.pic1.2.1.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_medium"> Three photographs of a library are taken from nearby positions. A red point marks a bookshelf in the first photo and a blue point marks another bookshelf in the third. Give the 3D distance between them in metres.</span></span></span>
<span id="A6.p1290.pic1.2.2" class="ltx_p"><span id="A6.p1290.pic1.2.2.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold" style="--ltx-fg-color:#000000;">Why it is hard.<span id="A6.p1290.pic1.2.2.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_medium"> The two points never appear in the same photo, so the distance cannot be judged from any single view; without tools the model answers 4.0 m.</span></span></span>
<span id="A6.p1290.pic1.2.3" class="ltx_p"><span id="A6.p1290.pic1.2.3.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold" style="--ltx-fg-color:#000000;">How the agent solves it.<span id="A6.p1290.pic1.2.3.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_medium"> It recovers where each photo was taken from, measures metric depth at each point, and has the coding agent place both points in one 3D frame and measure between them: 2.0 m, against a ground truth of 1.9 m.</span></span></span>
