<span id="A6.p1348.pic1.2" class="ltx_inline-block ltx_minipage ltx_align_bottom" style="width:37.68em;">
<span id="A6.p1348.pic1.2.1" class="ltx_p"><span id="A6.p1348.pic1.2.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold" style="--ltx-fg-color:#000000;">Task.<span id="A6.p1348.pic1.2.1.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_medium"> Three photographs of a basement storage area are taken from nearby positions; the first is the observer’s view. A red point marks a cabinet, visible only in the third photo. Give the distance from the observer to the cabinet in metres.</span></span></span>
<span id="A6.p1348.pic1.2.2" class="ltx_p"><span id="A6.p1348.pic1.2.2.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold" style="--ltx-fg-color:#000000;">Why it is hard.<span id="A6.p1348.pic1.2.2.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_medium"> The cabinet is seen from a different position than the one the question asks about, so its depth in the third photo is not the answer; without tools the model answers 2.0 m.</span></span></span>
<span id="A6.p1348.pic1.2.3" class="ltx_p"><span id="A6.p1348.pic1.2.3.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold" style="--ltx-fg-color:#000000;">How the agent solves it.<span id="A6.p1348.pic1.2.3.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_medium"> It recovers the camera positions, reads the cabinet’s depth in the third photo, and has the coding agent move that point into the observer’s frame: 2.40 m, exactly the ground truth.</span></span></span>
