</span></span></span></foreignObject></g><g fill-opacity="1.0" transform="matrix(1.0 0.0 0.0 1.0 14.31 11.55)"><foreignObject style="--ltx-fo-width:37.68em;--ltx-fo-height:18.69em;--ltx-fo-depth:0em;font-size:10pt;" height="258.68" overflow="visible" transform="matrix(1 0 0 -1 0 258.68)" width="521.39"><span class="ltx_foreignobject_container"><span class="ltx_foreignobject_content">
<span id="A6.p1414.pic1.2" class="ltx_inline-block ltx_minipage ltx_align_bottom" style="width:37.68em;">
<span id="A6.p1414.pic1.2.1" class="ltx_p"><span id="A6.p1414.pic1.2.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold" style="--ltx-fg-color:#000000;">Task.<span id="A6.p1414.pic1.2.1.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_medium"> Three photographs of an office corner are taken from nearby positions. A red point marks a window in the first photo and a blue point marks a cabinet in the third. Give the 3D distance between them in metres. Without tools the same model answers 1.7 m.</span></span></span>
<span id="A6.p1414.pic1.2.2" class="ltx_p"><span id="A6.p1414.pic1.2.2.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold" style="--ltx-fg-color:#000000;">How the agent approaches it.<span id="A6.p1414.pic1.2.2.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_medium"> It chains four kinds of tool: camera trajectory to relate the views, depth in all three views, grounding to locate the window and the cabinet, and the coding agent to back-project both points into one frame and measure between them.</span></span></span>
<span id="A6.p1414.pic1.2.3" class="ltx_p"><span id="A6.p1414.pic1.2.3.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold" style="--ltx-fg-color:#000000;">Caveat.<span id="A6.p1414.pic1.2.3.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_medium"> The pipeline is the right one, but its last step does not use the marked points. The generated code reads each box as position plus width and height, although the boxes are corner coordinates, and it takes the first “cabinet” box, an upper wall cabinet at the top edge of the photo, instead of the lower cabinet with the blue point. The answer, 1.20 m against a ground truth of 1.1 m, is therefore close partly by coincidence.</span></span></span>
</span></span></span></foreignObject></g></g></svg></span>
</div>
