<section id="A7.SS1" class="ltx_subsection">
<h3 class="ltx_title ltx_title_subsection"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_subsection">G.1 </span>Training-Free Harnesses</h3>

<div id="A7.SS1.p1" class="ltx_para ltx_noindent">
<p id="A7.SS1.p1.1" class="ltx_p">The training-free harnesses run on the same model endpoints as OpenVLHarness, so any difference reflects the harness rather than the model.</p>
</div>
<section id="A7.SS1.SSS0.Px1" class="ltx_paragraph">
<h4 class="ltx_title ltx_title_paragraph">OctoTools.</h4>

<div id="A7.SS1.SSS0.Px1.p1" class="ltx_para ltx_noindent">
<p id="A7.SS1.SSS0.Px1.p1.1" class="ltx_p">OctoTools <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_citep">(<a href="#bib.bib11" title="" class="ltx_ref">Lu et al., 2025</a>)</cite> is a planner–executor framework in which a planner analyzes the query and proposes the next step, an executor turns it into a tool command, and a verifier decides whether to stop. We use the official implementation and route every language-model role, including the planner, executor, verifier, and the Generalist Solution Generator tool, to the Qwen3-VL backbone. We use its base toolset, the Generalist Solution Generator, with a budget of ten steps and greedy decoding. Because OctoTools accepts a single image per query, multi-image inputs are tiled into one composite with labeled panels (“Image 1”, “Image 2”, …). The final answer is taken from its direct-output mode.</p>
</div>
</section>
<section id="A7.SS1.SSS0.Px2" class="ltx_paragraph">
<h4 class="ltx_title ltx_title_paragraph">Visual Sketchpad.</h4>

<div id="A7.SS1.SSS0.Px2.p1" class="ltx_para ltx_noindent">
<p id="A7.SS1.SSS0.Px2.p1.1" class="ltx_p">Visual Sketchpad <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_citep">(<a href="#bib.bib10" title="" class="ltx_ref">Hu et al., 2024</a>)</cite> prompts the model to write Python that calls vision tools and draws on the image, in a ReAct-style loop. We use the official implementation with the Qwen3-VL backbone as its single planning agent and its vision-task toolset: open-vocabulary detection with Grounding DINO <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_citep">(<a href="#bib.bib93" title="" class="ltx_ref">Liu et al., 2024</a>)</cite>, sliding-window detection, Set-of-Mark segmentation <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_citep">(<a href="#bib.bib96" title="" class="ltx_ref">Yang et al., 2023a</a>)</cite>, depth estimation with Depth Anything <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_citep">(<a href="#bib.bib97" title="" class="ltx_ref">Yang et al., 2024</a>)</cite>, zoom-in, and image overlay, all served with the original expert models. The agent may take up to ten replies with greedy decoding, receives multiple images as separate inputs, and ends with an <span id="A7.SS1.SSS0.Px2.p1.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_typewriter">ANSWER:</span> line from which we read the prediction. For grounding tasks, it is instructed to convert its detector’s boxes to our output format.</p>
</div>
</section>
<section id="A7.SS1.SSS0.Px3" class="ltx_paragraph">
<h4 class="ltx_title ltx_title_paragraph">Codex.</h4>

<div id="A7.SS1.SSS0.Px3.p1" class="ltx_para ltx_noindent">
<p id="A7.SS1.SSS0.Px3.p1.1" class="ltx_p">Codex <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_citep">(<a href="#bib.bib116" title="" class="ltx_ref">OpenAI, 2025a</a>)</cite> (v0.155.1) is OpenAI’s general-purpose coding agent. We run it non-interactively (<span id="A7.SS1.SSS0.Px3.p1.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_typewriter">codex exec</span>) in code mode, with a fresh, ephemeral session for every question, the question’s images attached as inputs, and its final agent message taken as the prediction. Web search, browser use, apps, plugins, image generation, and sub-agents are disabled, and model-written code runs in a read-only sandbox. With GPT-6 Luna, Codex calls the OpenAI Responses API at medium reasoning effort and receives the question text unchanged. Codex’s default sandbox cannot start shell processes on our evaluation host. For the GPT-6 Luna runs, we therefore enforce the same read-only policy with Linux Landlock and seccomp, under which model-written code executes (Table <a href="#A2.T15" title="Table 15 ‣ Code execution in Codex. ‣ B.6 Reasoning-Effort Study: Setup and Full Results ‣ Appendix B Additional Results and Analysis ‣ OpenVLHarness: Real World Visual Agentic Tool-Use with Multimodal Memory" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">15</span></a> reports how often); the Qwen3-VL runs used the default sandbox and could evaluate only simple expressions (Appendix <a href="#A2.SS7" title="B.7 Codex with Open-Source Backbones ‣ Appendix B Additional Results and Analysis ‣ OpenVLHarness: Real World Visual Agentic Tool-Use with Multimodal Memory" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">B.7</span></a>). With Qwen3-VL-8B and Qwen3-VL-32B, it calls the same vLLM endpoints as OpenVLHarness through a small proxy that exposes Codex’s code tool as a standard function tool, which our vLLM server otherwise fails to stream, and each response is capped at <math id="A7.SS1.SSS0.Px3.p1.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="2{,}048" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mn>2,048</mn><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">2{,}048</annotation></semantics></math> tokens. For the Qwen3-VL runs, Codex also appends a short instruction to the question: answer directly, give the option letter for multiple-choice questions, and use the image as evidence.
A question whose request fails or exceeds the time limit counts as incorrect; the limit is <math id="A7.SS1.SSS0.Px3.p1.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="300" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mn>300</mn><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">300</annotation></semantics></math> seconds with Qwen3-VL and <math id="A7.SS1.SSS0.Px3.p1.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="1{,}800" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mn>1,800</mn><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">1{,}800</annotation></semantics></math> seconds with GPT-6 Luna. With Qwen3-VL, LLM-judged benchmarks, including the search benchmarks, are scored with the Qwen3-VL-8B judge; with GPT-6 Luna, scoring follows Table <a href="#S3.T1" title="Table 1 ‣ Tasks. ‣ 3.1 Tasks, Models, and Baselines ‣ 3 Experiments ‣ OpenVLHarness: Real World Visual Agentic Tool-Use with Multimodal Memory" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">1</span></a>, so the search benchmarks are scored with the GPT-4o judge.</p>
</div>
</section>
</section>
<section id="A7.SS2" class="ltx_subsection">
<h3 class="ltx_title ltx_title_subsection"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_subsection">G.2 </span>Training-Based Harnesses</h3>

<div id="A7.SS2.p1" class="ltx_para ltx_noindent">
<p id="A7.SS2.p1.1" class="ltx_p">For the training-based baselines, the tool-use behavior lives in the fine-tuned weights, so we evaluate each method’s released checkpoint inside its authors’ inference loop rather than re-training it. We followed a two-stage reproduction procedure. First, we executed each repository’s own reproduction workflow—including its released environment, model checkpoint, prompt and image preprocessing, tool implementations, and agent loop—until the baseline ran correctly on its documented benchmarks. Second, we added a lightweight HTTP serving layer around that verified path. The serving layer standardizes request transport, process lifecycle, and input normalization, while the model-specific reasoning logic remains in the released implementation. Each service accepts a question and one or more ordered images, returns a final prediction and an agent trajectory, and keeps the model and tool processes resident across examples.</p>
</div>
<div id="A7.SS2.p2" class="ltx_para ltx_noindent">
<p id="A7.SS2.p2.1" class="ltx_p">All hosted baselines are evaluated through a shared external harness. For each benchmark example, the harness invokes the dataset’s prompt builder, converts the resulting text and ordered images into a common request, and records the returned prediction. The harness controls outer concurrency and, after inference, writes the prediction file expected by the benchmark evaluator and invokes the dataset’s native <span id="A7.SS2.p2.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_typewriter">evaluate</span> method using the same configured language-model judge where required. Crucially, scoring is performed entirely outside the baseline services: no service inspects ground truth, selects an evaluator, or computes accuracy. This separation ensures that all baselines are scored through a single consistent protocol. We compare them against OpenVLHarness on Qwen2.5-VL-7B-Instruct, the base model all three were fine-tuned from (Table <a href="#A2.T9" title="Table 9 ‣ B.3 Training-Based Baselines ‣ Appendix B Additional Results and Analysis ‣ OpenVLHarness: Real World Visual Agentic Tool-Use with Multimodal Memory" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">9</span></a>).</p>
</div>
<section id="A7.SS2.SSS0.Px1" class="ltx_paragraph">
<h4 class="ltx_title ltx_title_paragraph">AdaReasoner.</h4>

<div id="A7.SS2.SSS0.Px1.p1" class="ltx_para ltx_noindent">
<p id="A7.SS2.SSS0.Px1.p1.1" class="ltx_p">AdaReasoner <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_citep">(<a href="#bib.bib3" title="" class="ltx_ref">Song et al., 2026</a>)</cite> trains the model to adaptively compose tools. We use the AdaReasoner-7B checkpoint and the authors’ <span id="A7.SS2.SSS0.Px1.p1.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_typewriter">BaseToolInferencer</span>, which exposes pointing (backed by Molmo <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_citep">(<a href="#bib.bib37" title="" class="ltx_ref">Deitke et al., 2025</a>)</cite>), OCR, cropping, and drawing and path-planning tools through <span id="A7.SS2.SSS0.Px1.p1.1.2" class="ltx_text ltx_font_typewriter">&lt;tool_call&gt;</span> blocks, for up to six rounds. AdaReasoner is the only baseline requiring two Conda environments: the model/vLLM stack and the GPU PaddleOCR tool workers have incompatible tested dependencies. The launcher starts a tool controller and dedicated Point, OCR, and Crop workers alongside the model server.</p>
</div>
</section>
<section id="A7.SS2.SSS0.Px2" class="ltx_paragraph">
<h4 class="ltx_title ltx_title_paragraph">PixelReasoner.</h4>

<div id="A7.SS2.SSS0.Px2.p1" class="ltx_para ltx_noindent">
<p id="A7.SS2.SSS0.Px2.p1.1" class="ltx_p">PixelReasoner <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_citep">(<a href="#bib.bib6" title="" class="ltx_ref">Wang et al., 2025a</a>)</cite> is trained to reason in pixel space. We use the PixelReasoner-RL-v1 checkpoint and initialize the released Ray/vLLM engines, multimodal processor, and function-call prompt once at startup. The service retains the released tool parser and pixel-space operations (normalized cropping and frame selection). Inference proceeds at the granularity of model/tool rounds: ready requests are batched across vLLM engines, generated tool calls are executed, resulting crops or selected frames are appended to the conversation, and unfinished requests are requeued. The final answer is read from <span id="A7.SS2.SSS0.Px2.p1.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_typewriter">\boxed{}</span>. The documented image-resolution budgets (<span id="A7.SS2.SSS0.Px2.p1.1.2" class="ltx_text ltx_font_typewriter">MIN_PIXELS</span>=401408, <span id="A7.SS2.SSS0.Px2.p1.1.3" class="ltx_text ltx_font_typewriter">MAX_PIXELS</span>=4014080) are preserved as they affect how much visual information reaches the model.</p>
</div>
</section>
<section id="A7.SS2.SSS0.Px3" class="ltx_paragraph">
<h4 class="ltx_title ltx_title_paragraph">DeepEyesV2.</h4>

<div id="A7.SS2.SSS0.Px3.p1" class="ltx_para ltx_noindent">
<p id="A7.SS2.SSS0.Px3.p1.1" class="ltx_p">DeepEyesV2 <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_citep">(<a href="#bib.bib4" title="" class="ltx_ref">Hong et al., 2026a</a>)</cite> is trained for agentic multimodal reasoning with code execution. We use the released DeepEyesV2-7B checkpoint with the agent mode of the authors’ vendored VLMEvalKit <span id="A7.SS2.SSS0.Px3.p1.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_typewriter">VLLMAPIWrapper</span>. A single launcher supervises the text-search adapter, Python code service, one or more OpenAI-compatible vLLM model replicas, and the public inference API. The wrapper controls model calls, Python execution, retrieval turns, stopping rules, and answer extraction. All ordered input images are bound in the Python sandbox as <span id="A7.SS2.SSS0.Px3.p1.1.2" class="ltx_text ltx_font_typewriter">image_1</span>, <span id="A7.SS2.SSS0.Px3.p1.1.3" class="ltx_text ltx_font_typewriter">image_2</span>, … so that generated code can address every input.</p>
</div>
</section>
<section id="A7.SS2.SSS0.Px4" class="ltx_paragraph">
<h4 class="ltx_title ltx_title_paragraph">Fair-comparison controls.</h4>

<div id="A7.SS2.SSS0.Px4.p1" class="ltx_para ltx_noindent">
<p id="A7.SS2.SSS0.Px4.p1.1" class="ltx_p">For a defensible comparison, the following are held fixed or explicitly reported across all baselines: dataset version, split, and prompt builder; model checkpoint and quantization; image preprocessing and resolution limits; context window, per-call generation cap, temperature, and maximum agent/tool rounds; enabled tools and external search backend; concurrency and failure-retry policy; judge model, judge prompt, and judge retry count; and whether failures are scored as incorrect or retried.</p>
</div>
</section>
</section>
</section>
<section id="A8" class="ltx_appendix">
<h2 class="ltx_title ltx_title_appendix"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_appendix">Appendix H </span>Run-to-Run Variability</h2>

<div id="A8.p1" class="ltx_para ltx_noindent">
<p id="A8.p1.1" class="ltx_p">Agentic evaluation is stochastic: the backbone samples at a non-zero temperature, the harness makes further model calls inside its tools, and live search results change over time. This section describes how we account for this variability and quantifies it for the Qwen3-VL results in the main paper.</p>
</div>
<section id="A8.SS0.SSS0.Px1" class="ltx_paragraph">
<h4 class="ltx_title ltx_title_paragraph">Protocol.</h4>

<div id="A8.SS0.SSS0.Px1.p1" class="ltx_para ltx_noindent">
<p id="A8.SS0.SSS0.Px1.p1.1" class="ltx_p">Every Qwen3-VL number in the main paper, including the Base, OctoTools, Visual Sketchpad, and OpenVLHarness columns of Table <a href="#S3.T1" title="Table 1 ‣ Tasks. ‣ 3.1 Tasks, Models, and Baselines ‣ 3 Experiments ‣ OpenVLHarness: Real World Visual Agentic Tool-Use with Multimodal Memory" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">1</span></a> and every entry of Table <a href="#S3.T3" title="Table 3 ‣ 3.4 Ablation Study ‣ 3 Experiments ‣ OpenVLHarness: Real World Visual Agentic Tool-Use with Multimodal Memory" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">3</span></a>, is the mean of three independent runs. Each run uses a different random seed, which controls both the backbone’s sampling and every stochastic component of the harness, such as the model calls made inside the Webpage Visit and Python Coding Agent tools. All other factors are held fixed across runs: the benchmark instances, prompts, tool backends, decoding parameters, iteration and context limits, and the scorers. Judges and answer extractors decode greedily, so the variation we measure comes from the agent rather than from scoring. We report all three runs of every configuration; no run is discarded or re-run, and a request that fails after retries is scored as incorrect in the run where it occurs. Search tools query the live web and cannot be seeded, so for search benchmarks the variation also reflects changes in retrieved content between runs. Results with proprietary backbones (GPT-5, GPT-6 Luna, GPT-6 Sol, and GPT-6 Astra) are single runs because of API cost.</p>
</div>
</section>
<section id="A8.SS0.SSS0.Px2" class="ltx_paragraph">
<h4 class="ltx_title ltx_title_paragraph">Reported statistics.</h4>

<div id="A8.SS0.SSS0.Px2.p1" class="ltx_para ltx_noindent">
<p id="A8.SS0.SSS0.Px2.p1.1" class="ltx_p">Tables <a href="#A8.T26" title="Table 26 ‣ Reported statistics. ‣ Appendix H Run-to-Run Variability ‣ OpenVLHarness: Real World Visual Agentic Tool-Use with Multimodal Memory" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">26</span></a> and <a href="#A8.T27" title="Table 27 ‣ Main results. ‣ Appendix H Run-to-Run Variability ‣ OpenVLHarness: Real World Visual Agentic Tool-Use with Multimodal Memory" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">27</span></a> report, for each entry, the standard deviation of the score across the three runs. For averages over datasets, we first compute the average within each run and then take the standard deviation of these three averages; this is smaller than the per-dataset deviations because independent fluctuations partly cancel. With only three runs, each standard deviation is itself a coarse estimate and should be read as indicating the scale of run-to-run variation rather than an exact value.</p>
</div>
