<h4 class="ltx_title ltx_title_paragraph">Main results.</h4>

<div id="A8.SS0.SSS0.Px3.p1" class="ltx_para ltx_noindent">
<p id="A8.SS0.SSS0.Px3.p1.1" class="ltx_p">Per-dataset standard deviations are small relative to the differences between methods (Table <a href="#A8.T26" title="Table 26 ‣ Reported statistics. ‣ Appendix H Run-to-Run Variability ‣ OpenVLHarness: Real World Visual Agentic Tool-Use with Multimodal Memory" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">26</span></a>). For OpenVLHarness, most are below <math id="A8.SS0.SSS0.Px3.p1.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="2" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mn>2</mn><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">2</annotation></semantics></math> points; the largest occur on search benchmarks with live retrieval, such as MMSearch (<math id="A8.SS0.SSS0.Px3.p1.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="171" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mn>171</mn><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">171</annotation></semantics></math> questions, where one question is worth <math id="A8.SS0.SSS0.Px3.p1.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="0.58" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mn>0.58</mn><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">0.58</annotation></semantics></math> points) and FVQA with Qwen3-VL-8B. OpenVLHarness varies somewhat more than Base, as expected for multi-turn trajectories in which an early difference in a tool call can change the rest of the trajectory. The <math id="A8.SS0.SSS0.Px3.p1.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="22" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mn>22</mn><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">22</annotation></semantics></math>-dataset averages are very stable, with standard deviations of at most <math id="A8.SS0.SSS0.Px3.p1.m5" class="ltx_Math" alttext="0.35" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mn>0.35</mn><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">0.35</annotation></semantics></math> points. The gain of OpenVLHarness over Base in this average (<math id="A8.SS0.SSS0.Px3.p1.m6" class="ltx_Math" alttext="+11.2" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mo>+</mo><mn>11.2</mn></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">+11.2</annotation></semantics></math> at 8B and <math id="A8.SS0.SSS0.Px3.p1.m7" class="ltx_Math" alttext="+12.6" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mo>+</mo><mn>12.6</mn></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">+12.6</annotation></semantics></math> at 32B) is therefore about <math id="A8.SS0.SSS0.Px3.p1.m8" class="ltx_Math" alttext="29" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mn>29</mn><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">29</annotation></semantics></math> and <math id="A8.SS0.SSS0.Px3.p1.m9" class="ltx_Math" alttext="44" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mn>44</mn><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">44</annotation></semantics></math> times the combined standard deviation of the two averages, so the improvement cannot be attributed to run-to-run variation.</p>
</div>
