<section id="A8.SS0.SSS0.Px4" class="ltx_paragraph">
<h4 class="ltx_title ltx_title_paragraph">Ablation.</h4>

<div id="A8.SS0.SSS0.Px4.p1" class="ltx_para ltx_noindent">
<p id="A8.SS0.SSS0.Px4.p1.1" class="ltx_p">The overall averages in Table <a href="#S3.T3" title="Table 3 ‣ 3.4 Ablation Study ‣ 3 Experiments ‣ OpenVLHarness: Real World Visual Agentic Tool-Use with Multimodal Memory" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">3</span></a> have standard deviations between <math id="A8.SS0.SSS0.Px4.p1.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="0.2" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mn>0.2</mn><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">0.2</annotation></semantics></math> and <math id="A8.SS0.SSS0.Px4.p1.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="0.35" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mn>0.35</mn><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">0.35</annotation></semantics></math> points for every variant and backbone (Table <a href="#A8.T27" title="Table 27 ‣ Main results. ‣ Appendix H Run-to-Run Variability ‣ OpenVLHarness: Real World Visual Agentic Tool-Use with Multimodal Memory" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">27</span></a>). Adding tools, the capability layer, and multimodal memory each changes the overall average by several points, far beyond this variation. The textual-memory step (<math id="A8.SS0.SSS0.Px4.p1.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="+0.7" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mo>+</mo><mn>0.7</mn></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">+0.7</annotation></semantics></math> at 8B and <math id="A8.SS0.SSS0.Px4.p1.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="+0.3" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mo>+</mo><mn>0.3</mn></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">+0.3</annotation></semantics></math> at 32B) is comparable to run-to-run variation, which is consistent with our conclusion that text descriptions of images are a weak substitute for retaining the images themselves.</p>
</div>
</section>
<section id="A8.SS0.SSS0.Px5" class="ltx_paragraph">
<h4 class="ltx_title ltx_title_paragraph">Single-run results.</h4>

<div id="A8.SS0.SSS0.Px5.p1" class="ltx_para ltx_noindent">
<p id="A8.SS0.SSS0.Px5.p1.1" class="ltx_p">Proprietary backbones are evaluated once, so their per-dataset numbers carry run-to-run variation of the scale measured above. Their conclusions, however, rest on large differences: OpenVLHarness improves the <math id="A8.SS0.SSS0.Px5.p1.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="22" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mn>22</mn><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">22</annotation></semantics></math>-dataset average by <math id="A8.SS0.SSS0.Px5.p1.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="+10.8" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mo>+</mo><mn>10.8</mn></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">+10.8</annotation></semantics></math> points with GPT-6 Luna, <math id="A8.SS0.SSS0.Px5.p1.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="+11.6" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mo>+</mo><mn>11.6</mn></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">+11.6</annotation></semantics></math> with GPT-6 Sol, and <math id="A8.SS0.SSS0.Px5.p1.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="+9.9" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mo>+</mo><mn>9.9</mn></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">+9.9</annotation></semantics></math> with GPT-6 Astra, more than twenty-five times the largest average-level standard deviation we observe with Qwen3-VL.</p>
</div>
</section>
