<section id="A9" class="ltx_appendix">
<h2 class="ltx_title ltx_title_appendix"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_appendix">Appendix I </span>Additional Related Work</h2>

<section id="A9.SS0.SSS0.Px1" class="ltx_paragraph">
<h4 class="ltx_title ltx_title_paragraph">Multimodal search agents.</h4>

<div id="A9.SS0.SSS0.Px1.p1" class="ltx_para ltx_noindent">
<p id="A9.SS0.SSS0.Px1.p1.1" class="ltx_p">Our work targets multimodal search, where a query is resolved by orchestrating reasoning, perception, and live web retrieval across many turns. A growing collection of benchmarks frames this task: MMSearch <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_citep">(<a href="#bib.bib44" title="" class="ltx_ref">Jiang et al., 2024</a>)</cite> couples a multimodal query with a curated search engine; MMSearch-Plus <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_citep">(<a href="#bib.bib45" title="" class="ltx_ref">Tao et al., 2026</a>)</cite> adds provenance-aware verification; BrowseComp-VL <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_citep">(<a href="#bib.bib47" title="" class="ltx_ref">Geng et al., 2026</a>)</cite> and MM-BrowseComp <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_citep">(<a href="#bib.bib48" title="" class="ltx_ref">Li et al., 2025b</a>)</cite> raise cross-modal information-seeking difficulty; VisBrowse-Bench <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_citep">(<a href="#bib.bib50" title="" class="ltx_ref">Zhang et al., 2026c</a>)</cite> targets visual-native search; and RealX-Bench <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_citep">(<a href="#bib.bib4" title="" class="ltx_ref">Hong et al., 2026a</a>)</cite> couples fine-grained perception with multi-hop retrieval. Beyond pure search, the landscape extends to embodied tasks that integrate web information with physical action <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_citep">(<a href="#bib.bib83" title="" class="ltx_ref">Hong et al., 2025</a>)</cite>. Recent browsing agents tackle these benchmarks via hand-engineered tool inventories <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_citep">(<a href="#bib.bib47" title="" class="ltx_ref">Geng et al., 2026</a>; <a href="#bib.bib75" title="" class="ltx_ref">Huang et al., 2026</a>; <a href="#bib.bib52" title="" class="ltx_ref">Yao et al., 2026</a>; <a href="#bib.bib76" title="" class="ltx_ref">Chen et al., 2026</a>; <a href="#bib.bib53" title="" class="ltx_ref">Wu et al., 2025a</a>; <a href="#bib.bib54" title="" class="ltx_ref">Narayan et al., 2025</a>)</cite>, supervised fine-tuning or reinforcement learning that teaches tool use, or memory learned over a fixed inventory <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_citep">(<a href="#bib.bib84" title="" class="ltx_ref">Xiao et al., 2025</a>; <a href="#bib.bib85" title="" class="ltx_ref">Hasegawa et al., 2025</a>)</cite>. Our contribution is orthogonal: rather than freeze the inventory, we <em id="A9.SS0.SSS0.Px1.p1.1.1" class="ltx_emph ltx_font_italic">generate</em> it.</p>
</div>
</section>
<section id="A9.SS0.SSS0.Px2" class="ltx_paragraph">
<h4 class="ltx_title ltx_title_paragraph">Grounded perception.</h4>

<div id="A9.SS0.SSS0.Px2.p1" class="ltx_para ltx_noindent">
<p id="A9.SS0.SSS0.Px2.p1.1" class="ltx_p">Class-agnostic counting was popularized by FSC-147 <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_citep">(<a href="#bib.bib66" title="" class="ltx_ref">Ranjan et al., 2021</a>)</cite>, which asks for the number of objects of an arbitrary category; specialized counters such as CounTR <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_citep">(<a href="#bib.bib98" title="" class="ltx_ref">Liu et al., 2022</a>)</cite>, LOCA <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_citep">(<a href="#bib.bib99" title="" class="ltx_ref">Djukic et al., 2023</a>)</cite>, and CountGD <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_citep">(<a href="#bib.bib100" title="" class="ltx_ref">Amini-Naieni et al., 2024</a>)</cite> regress density maps or detections, and FSCD <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_citep">(<a href="#bib.bib39" title="" class="ltx_ref">Nguyen et al., 2022</a>)</cite> extends the task to predicting a box for every instance. Open-vocabulary detectors and segmenters, including GLIP <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_citep">(<a href="#bib.bib40" title="" class="ltx_ref">Li et al., 2022</a>)</cite>, Grounding DINO <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_citep">(<a href="#bib.bib93" title="" class="ltx_ref">Liu et al., 2024</a>)</cite>, and SAM 3 <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_citep">(<a href="#bib.bib59" title="" class="ltx_ref">Carion et al., 2025</a>)</cite>, localize arbitrary text-specified categories, and MLLMs have begun to count by pointing <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_citep">(<a href="#bib.bib37" title="" class="ltx_ref">Deitke et al., 2025</a>)</cite>. Nevertheless, MLLMs remain unreliable once scenes contain more than a handful of objects <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_citep">(<a href="#bib.bib102" title="" class="ltx_ref">Paiss et al., 2023</a>; <a href="#bib.bib38" title="" class="ltx_ref">Tamarapalli et al., 2025</a>)</cite>. Tool-use systems such as ViperGPT <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_citep">(<a href="#bib.bib12" title="" class="ltx_ref">Surís et al., 2023</a>)</cite>, VisProg <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_citep">(<a href="#bib.bib13" title="" class="ltx_ref">Gupta and Kembhavi, 2023</a>)</cite>, and Visual Sketchpad <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_citep">(<a href="#bib.bib10" title="" class="ltx_ref">Hu et al., 2024</a>)</cite> let a language model call detectors and count their outputs programmatically. OpenVLHarness follows this direction but keeps grounding outputs as persistent evidence: boxes, masks, and scores are stored as typed bindings that the Python Coding Agent can filter, deduplicate, and aggregate across turns, instead of a count that is read once from text.</p>
</div>
</section>
<section id="A9.SS0.SSS0.Px3" class="ltx_paragraph">
<h4 class="ltx_title ltx_title_paragraph">Spatial reasoning.</h4>

<div id="A9.SS0.SSS0.Px3.p1" class="ltx_para ltx_noindent">
<p id="A9.SS0.SSS0.Px3.p1.1" class="ltx_p">Spatial reasoning benchmarks test models beyond static single-image relations. EmbSpatial-Bench <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_citep">(<a href="#bib.bib71" title="" class="ltx_ref">Du et al., 2024</a>)</cite> and ERQA <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_citep">(<a href="#bib.bib70" title="" class="ltx_ref">Team et al., 2025</a>)</cite> target spatial and embodied reasoning in simulated and real-world scenes; SparBench <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_citep">(<a href="#bib.bib72" title="" class="ltx_ref">Zhang et al., 2026a</a>)</cite> covers diverse single- and multi-view spatial tasks; and MindCube <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_citep">(<a href="#bib.bib73" title="" class="ltx_ref">Wang et al., 2025c</a>)</cite> and MMSI-Bench <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_citep">(<a href="#bib.bib69" title="" class="ltx_ref">Yang et al., 2025b</a>)</cite> emphasize reasoning across multiple or limited views. In parallel, VGGT <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_citep">(<a href="#bib.bib95" title="" class="ltx_ref">Wang et al., 2025b</a>)</cite> and subsequent VGGT-style geometric foundation models <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_citep">(<a href="#bib.bib117" title="" class="ltx_ref">Hu et al., 2026</a>)</cite> recover explicit scene geometry, including depth, camera pose, point maps, and tracks from visual observations. OpenVLHarness treats such geometric models as callable capabilities rather than requiring the MLLM itself to internalize 3D reconstruction, allowing their outputs to be composed with grounding and other persistent evidence during reasoning.</p>
</div>
</section>
<section id="A9.SS0.SSS0.Px4" class="ltx_paragraph">
<h4 class="ltx_title ltx_title_paragraph">Experience learning and harness optimization.</h4>

<div id="A9.SS0.SSS0.Px4.p1" class="ltx_para ltx_noindent">
<p id="A9.SS0.SSS0.Px4.p1.1" class="ltx_p">Learning from past experience has a long history in reinforcement learning, exemplified by Hindsight Experience Replay <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_citep">(<a href="#bib.bib24" title="" class="ltx_ref">Andrychowicz et al., 2017</a>)</cite>, which relabels failed rollouts as successes, STaR <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_citep">(<a href="#bib.bib25" title="" class="ltx_ref">Zelikman et al., 2022</a>)</cite>, which bootstraps a reasoner on its own correct rationales, and ARMAP <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_citep">(<a href="#bib.bib114" title="" class="ltx_ref">Chen et al., 2025b</a>)</cite>, which learns a reward model from an agent’s own trajectories to guide its planning. A newer line treats memory as a learning signal, distilling reusable knowledge from cross-sample working memory, though these methods often require additional training or model-weight updates <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_citep">(<a href="#bib.bib32" title="" class="ltx_ref">Packer et al., 2023</a>; <a href="#bib.bib33" title="" class="ltx_ref">Yan et al., 2025b</a>; <a href="#bib.bib27" title="" class="ltx_ref">Zhou et al., 2025b</a>; <a href="#bib.bib28" title="" class="ltx_ref">Wang et al., 2024b</a>; <a href="#bib.bib103" title="" class="ltx_ref">Yang et al., 2025a</a>; <a href="#bib.bib29" title="" class="ltx_ref">Ouyang et al., 2026</a>; <a href="#bib.bib30" title="" class="ltx_ref">Zhang et al., 2025</a>; <a href="#bib.bib31" title="" class="ltx_ref">Yan et al., 2025a</a>; <a href="#bib.bib34" title="" class="ltx_ref">Ren et al., 2026</a>; <a href="#bib.bib64" title="" class="ltx_ref">Shi et al., 2026</a>; <a href="#bib.bib26" title="" class="ltx_ref">Hong et al., 2026b</a>; <a href="#bib.bib113" title="" class="ltx_ref">Jiang et al., 2026</a>)</cite>. A third line instead optimizes prompts and harnesses without any model-weight updates, using LLM- and coding-agent-driven optimizers that rewrite prompts, pipelines, and harness code from observed failures <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_citep">(<a href="#bib.bib79" title="" class="ltx_ref">Khattab et al., 2024</a>; <a href="#bib.bib77" title="" class="ltx_ref">Yuksekgonul et al., 2024</a>; <a href="#bib.bib78" title="" class="ltx_ref">Agrawal and others, 2025</a>; <a href="#bib.bib80" title="" class="ltx_ref">Lee et al., 2026</a>; <a href="#bib.bib81" title="" class="ltx_ref">Yuksekgonul et al., 2026</a>; <a href="#bib.bib82" title="" class="ltx_ref">Sharma, 2025</a>)</cite>, but to date this is done purely in the text-only prompt and harness domain.</p>
</div>
</section>
